# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Newton's law of gravitation and field strength
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Newton's law of gravitation and gravitational potential",
    title="Newton's law of gravitation and gravitational field strength",
    slug="phu1-gravitation-field-strength",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Use Newton's inverse-square law of gravitation, define gravitational field strength as force per unit mass, and find where the pulls of two bodies cancel.",
    objectives=[
        "State and use Newton's law of gravitation for point masses and uniform spheres",
        "Define gravitational field strength and show that g = GM/r^2",
        "Find g at a height above a planet and compare it with the surface value",
        "Locate a null point between two masses",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Newton's law of gravitation", "Gravitational constant G", "Gravitational field strength", "Inverse-square law", "Null point"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A mango falls from a tree in Mbalmayo, and the Moon stays in orbit around Earth. Newton saw that one pull explains both. Every mass attracts every other mass. The pull is tiny for everyday objects but huge for planets, because Earth's mass is about $6 \times 10^{24}$ kg."),
        T(r"At Lower Sixth you used $g = 9.81$ N/kg near Earth's surface. In this lesson you will see why $g$ has that value, and why it becomes smaller as you rise above the ground."),
        H("Newton's law of gravitation"),
        D(r"Two **point masses** attract each other with a force that is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.", title="Newton's law of gravitation",
          formula=r"F = \frac{G m_1 m_2}{r^2}",
          note=r"$G = 6.67\times10^{-11}$ N m$^2$ kg$^{-2}$ (the gravitational constant). The force is always attractive and acts along the line joining the masses."),
        T(r"A **point mass** is an object so small, compared with the distance $r$, that its size does not matter. A **uniform sphere** (such as a planet) behaves as if all its mass were at its centre, for points outside the sphere. So for Earth, $r$ is measured from Earth's **centre**."),
        T(r"The two forces form a Newton's third law pair: Earth pulls the Moon with the same force as the Moon pulls Earth, in opposite directions. The smaller mass simply accelerates more."),
        KEY("Inverse-square law", r"If the distance is doubled, the force becomes one quarter. If the distance is tripled, it becomes one ninth.", cards=[["Double $r$", r"$F$ falls to $\frac{1}{4}$"], ["Triple $r$", r"$F$ falls to $\frac{1}{9}$"], ["Double one mass", r"$F$ doubles"]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: Earth and Moon", intro=r"Earth has mass $5.97 \times 10^{24}$ kg and the Moon $7.35 \times 10^{22}$ kg. Their centres are $3.84 \times 10^{8}$ m apart. Find the gravitational force between them.", steps=[
            S("Write the law", math=r"F = \frac{G m_1 m_2}{r^2}"),
            S("Substitute (SI units)", math=r"F = \frac{(6.67\times 10^{-11})(5.97\times 10^{24})(7.35\times 10^{22})}{(3.84\times 10^{8})^2}"),
            S("Calculate", math=r"F = 1.98 \times 10^{20}\ \text{N} \approx 2.0 \times 10^{20}\ \text{N}"),
        ]),
        H("Gravitational field strength"),
        D(r"The **gravitational field strength** $g$ at a point is the gravitational force acting per unit mass placed at that point. It is a vector that points towards the mass creating the field. Unit: N/kg (equal to m/s²).", title="Definition", formula=r"g = \frac{F}{m}"),
        T(r"For a test mass $m$ at distance $r$ from the centre of a body of mass $M$, Newton's law gives $F = \dfrac{GMm}{r^2}$. Dividing by $m$:"),
        F(r"g = \frac{GM}{r^2}"),
        T(r"The mass $m$ cancels, so the field strength does not depend on the size of the test mass. This is why a stone and a coconut fall with the same acceleration when air resistance is negligible."),
        EX("Worked example 2: Earth's surface and at 400 km", intro=r"Take $M = 5.97 \times 10^{24}$ kg and Earth's radius $R = 6.37 \times 10^{6}$ m. Find $g$ at the surface and at a height of 400 km, the height of the International Space Station.", steps=[
            S("Surface value", math=r"g = \frac{GM}{R^2} = \frac{(6.67\times10^{-11})(5.97\times10^{24})}{(6.37\times10^{6})^2} = 9.81\ \text{N/kg}"),
            S("Distance from the centre at 400 km", math=r"r = 6.37\times10^{6} + 0.40\times10^{6} = 6.77\times10^{6}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Field strength there", math=r"g = \frac{(6.67\times10^{-11})(5.97\times10^{24})}{(6.77\times10^{6})^2} = 8.69\ \text{N/kg}"),
            S("Compare", text=r"At 400 km, $g$ is still 89% of its surface value. Astronauts on the station feel weightless because they are in free fall, not because gravity has gone."),
        ]),
        TIP(r"Always measure $r$ from the centre of the planet. A height $h$ above the surface means $r = R + h$."),
        H("Where the pulls cancel: the null point"),
        T(r"Between two masses, their fields point in opposite directions. At one point the two field strengths are equal and opposite, so the resultant field is zero. This is the **null point** (or neutral point)."),
        EX("Worked example 3: null point between Earth and Moon", intro=r"Find the distance from Earth's centre to the point on the line to the Moon where the resultant field is zero. Use $M_E = 5.97\times10^{24}$ kg, $M_M = 7.35\times10^{22}$ kg and $d = 3.84\times10^{8}$ m.", steps=[
            S("Let $x$ be the distance from Earth's centre. Equal fields:", math=r"\frac{GM_E}{x^2} = \frac{GM_M}{(d - x)^2}"),
            S("Take the square root of both sides (G cancels)", math=r"\frac{x}{d - x} = \sqrt{\frac{M_E}{M_M}} = \sqrt{81.2} = 9.01"),
            S("Solve for $x$", math=r"x = 9.01(d - x) \;\Rightarrow\; x = \frac{9.01\,d}{10.01} = 3.46 \times 10^{8}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Meaning", text=r"The null point is about 90% of the way to the Moon, only $3.8 \times 10^{7}$ m from it, because Earth's mass is 81 times larger."),
        ], check=r"\frac{GM_E}{x^2} = 3.33\times10^{-3}\ \text{N/kg},\ \ \frac{GM_M}{(d-x)^2} = 3.33\times10^{-3}\ \text{N/kg}"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using height instead of distance from the centre", r"In $g = GM/r^2$ the distance $r$ is from the centre. For a satellite 400 km above the ground, $r$ is $R + 400$ km, not 400 km."),
        MISTAKE("Adding fields as if they were just numbers", r"Fields are vectors. Between two masses they point in opposite directions, so subtract their magnitudes. Outside the pair they point the same way, so they add."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking a heavier object falls faster because the force is bigger", r"The force is bigger, but so is the mass. $g = F/m$ does not depend on the object's mass."),
        QUIZ(r"The gravitational force between two masses is $F$. The distance between them is halved and one mass is doubled. What is the new force?",
             [r"$2F$", r"$4F$", r"$8F$", r"$F/2$"], 2,
             r"Halving the distance multiplies the force by 4 (inverse square). Doubling one mass multiplies it by 2. Together: $4 \times 2 = 8$, so $8F$.",
             hint=r"Combine the effects: $F \propto m_1 m_2 / r^2$.",
             feedback=[r"This only counts the doubled mass. The distance change also matters.", r"This only counts the halved distance. The doubled mass also matters.", "", r"The force increases when the masses get closer; it does not decrease."]),
        QUIZ(r"At the surface of a planet of radius $R$, the field strength is $g_0$. What is the field strength at a height of $2R$ above the surface?",
             [r"$g_0/2$", r"$g_0/3$", r"$g_0/4$", r"$g_0/9$"], 3,
             r"Height $2R$ above the surface means $r = 3R$. Since $g \propto 1/r^2$, $g = g_0/3^2 = g_0/9$.",
             hint=r"What is $r$ measured from?",
             feedback=[r"Gravity follows an inverse-square law, not an inverse law.", r"You used $r = 3R$ but did not square it.", r"You used $r = 2R$. The height $2R$ is above the surface, so $r = R + 2R = 3R$.", ""]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"State Newton's law of gravitation, and explain why the gravitational field strength at a point does not depend on the mass of the object placed there.",
           r"Any two point masses attract each other with a force proportional to the product of the masses and inversely proportional to the square of their separation, $F = Gm_1m_2/r^2$. Field strength is force per unit mass, $g = F/m = GM/r^2$. The test mass $m$ cancels, so $g$ depends only on the mass $M$ creating the field and the distance $r$."),
        PQ(r"Mars has mass $6.42\times10^{23}$ kg and radius $3.39\times10^{6}$ m. Calculate the gravitational field strength at its surface.",
           r"$g = \dfrac{GM}{R^2} = \dfrac{(6.67\times10^{-11})(6.42\times10^{23})}{(3.39\times10^{6})^2} = 3.73$ N/kg."),
        PQ(r"A satellite orbits at a height of $2.0\times10^{6}$ m above Earth's surface. Earth's radius is $6.37\times10^{6}$ m and $g$ at the surface is 9.81 N/kg. Find $g$ at the satellite's height.",
           r"$r = 8.37\times10^{6}$ m. $g \propto 1/r^2$, so $g = 9.81 \times \left(\dfrac{6.37}{8.37}\right)^2 = 9.81 \times 0.579 = 5.68$ N/kg."),
        PQ(r"Two small spheres of mass 1000 kg are 2.0 m apart (centre to centre). Calculate the force between them and say why you do not notice such forces between everyday objects.",
           r"$F = \dfrac{(6.67\times10^{-11})(1000)(1000)}{2.0^2} = 1.67\times10^{-5}$ N. This is tiny because $G$ is very small. Only when one mass is as large as a planet is the force noticeable."),
        TIP(r"Gravitational fields appear in Paper 1 as quick ratio questions (double the distance, change the mass) and in Paper 2 as calculations of $g$ and null points. Check your powers of ten carefully."),
        SUM(r"Newton's law: $F = Gm_1m_2/r^2$, attractive, inverse square; use the distance between centres.",
            r"Gravitational field strength is force per unit mass: $g = F/m = GM/r^2$ (N/kg).",
            r"At height $h$, use $r = R + h$; $g$ falls as $1/r^2$.",
            r"Between two masses the fields oppose; the null point is where their magnitudes are equal."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2. Gravitational potential and escape speed
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Newton's law of gravitation and gravitational potential",
    title="Gravitational potential, potential energy and escape speed",
    slug="phu1-gravitational-potential-escape",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Define gravitational potential as work done per unit mass from infinity, use V = -GM/r and g = -dV/dr, and calculate energy changes and escape speed.",
    objectives=[
        "Define gravitational potential and explain why it is negative",
        "Use V = -GM/r and potential energy U = mV to find energy changes",
        "Relate field strength to the gradient of the potential graph",
        "Derive and use the escape speed of a planet",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Gravitational potential", "Gravitational potential energy", "Potential gradient", "Escape speed", "Equipotential"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Rockets launched to put communication satellites into orbit must climb out of Earth's gravitational field. How much energy does that take? Using $g = GM/r^2$ with a changing $g$ would need calculus every time. A smarter idea is to attach an energy value to every **point** in the field. That value is the **gravitational potential**."),
        H("Definition and the formula V = -GM/r"),
        D(r"The **gravitational potential** $V$ at a point is the work done per unit mass in bringing a small test mass from infinity to that point. Unit: J/kg. It is a **scalar**.", title="Gravitational potential",
          formula=r"V = -\frac{GM}{r}",
          note=r"Infinity is the reference where $V = 0$. Gravity is attractive, so you must take energy away (do negative work) to bring a mass in. Hence $V$ is negative everywhere."),
        T(r"**Where the formula comes from.** To bring a 1 kg mass slowly in from infinity, an external force must balance gravity. At distance $x$ that force points outwards and has magnitude $GM/x^2$, while the mass moves inwards. Adding up the work (the outward force times the inward step) from $x = \infty$ to $x = r$ gives"),
        F(r"V = \int_{\infty}^{r} \frac{GM}{x^2}\,dx = \left[-\frac{GM}{x}\right]_{\infty}^{r} = -\frac{GM}{r}"),
        T(r"In words: the closer you are to the mass, the more negative the potential. At infinity $V = 0$, the highest value. Moving upwards means $V$ increases (becomes less negative)."),
        IMG("al-phu1-gravity-graphs.svg", alt="Two graphs against r over R from 1 to 5. Field strength falls steeply as 1 over r squared; potential rises from minus 1 at the surface towards zero as 1 over r.", caption="Figure 1. Field strength g and potential V outside a planet, in units of their surface values g0 and V0 = -GM/R."),
        H("Potential energy and energy changes"),
        D(r"A mass $m$ at a point with potential $V$ has gravitational potential energy $U = mV$. When a mass moves between two points, the work done by an external force is", title="Energy change", formula=r"\Delta W = m\,\Delta V = m\,(V_2 - V_1)"),
        T(r"If $V_2$ is higher than $V_1$ (moving away from the planet), $\Delta W$ is positive: you must supply energy. Near the surface, $\Delta V \approx g\,\Delta h$, which gives the familiar $mg\Delta h$. Far from the surface you must use $V = -GM/r$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: potential and energy to reach orbit height", intro=r"Using $GM = 3.98\times10^{14}$ m³/s² and $R = 6.37\times10^{6}$ m for Earth, find the potential at the surface and at 400 km height, and the minimum extra potential energy for a 1000 kg satellite lifted to 400 km.", steps=[
            S("Potential at the surface", math=r"V_1 = -\frac{3.98\times10^{14}}{6.37\times10^{6}} = -6.25\times10^{7}\ \text{J/kg}"),
            S(r"Potential at $r = 6.77\times10^{6}$ m", math=r"V_2 = -\frac{3.98\times10^{14}}{6.77\times10^{6}} = -5.88\times10^{7}\ \text{J/kg}"),
            S("Change in potential", math=r"\Delta V = V_2 - V_1 = +3.7\times10^{6}\ \text{J/kg}"),
            S("Energy for 1000 kg", math=r"\Delta U = m\Delta V = 1000 \times 3.7\times10^{6} = 3.7\times10^{9}\ \text{J}"),
        ]),
        T(r"Notice that the result is not the same as $mgh = 1000 \times 9.81 \times 4.0\times10^{5} = 3.9\times10^{9}$ J, because $g$ decreases as you climb. (This is energy only to raise the satellite; it needs extra kinetic energy to move sideways at orbital speed.)"),
        H("Field strength is the potential gradient"),
        D(r"The field strength equals the negative of the gradient of the potential graph.", title="Link between g and V", formula=r"g = -\frac{dV}{dr}"),
        T(r"Check with $V = -GM/r$: differentiating gives $\dfrac{dV}{dr} = +\dfrac{GM}{r^2}$, so $g = -\dfrac{dV}{dr}$ has magnitude $GM/r^2$. The minus sign shows that the field points towards decreasing $V$, that is, towards the mass. On a graph of $V$ against $r$, the gradient is steepest at the surface and gets shallower further out, matching the falling $g$ in Figure 1."),
        T(r"An **equipotential** is a surface on which $V$ is the same. Equipotentials around a planet are spheres, and field lines cross them at right angles. No work is done moving along an equipotential."),
        MISTAKE("Adding potentials like vectors", r"Potential is a scalar. For two masses, add the potentials as signed numbers: $V = -\frac{GM_1}{r_1} - \frac{GM_2}{r_2}$. The potential at the null point of Earth and Moon is not zero, even though the field is."),
        H("Escape speed"),
        D(r"The **escape speed** is the minimum speed at which an object must be launched from the surface so that it can just reach infinity, where it comes to rest (with no further propulsion and ignoring air resistance).", title="Escape speed"),
        T(r"Infinity has $V = 0$. At the surface the object needs total energy (kinetic plus potential) of at least zero:"),
        F(r"\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 + m\left(-\frac{GM}{R}\right) = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; v_{\text{esc}} = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}} = \sqrt{2gR}"),
        T(r"The mass $m$ cancels, so a grain of sand and a spacecraft have the same escape speed."),
        EX("Worked example 2: escape speeds of Earth and Moon", intro=r"Calculate the escape speed of Earth ($GM = 3.98\times10^{14}$ m³/s², $R = 6.37\times10^{6}$ m) and of the Moon ($M = 7.35\times10^{22}$ kg, $R = 1.74\times10^{6}$ m).", steps=[
            S("Earth", math=r"v = \sqrt{\frac{2(3.98\times10^{14})}{6.37\times10^{6}}} = 1.12\times10^{4}\ \text{m/s} = 11.2\ \text{km/s}"),
            S("Moon: first $GM$", math=r"GM = (6.67\times10^{-11})(7.35\times10^{22}) = 4.90\times10^{12}\ \text{m}^3/\text{s}^2"),
            S("Moon: speed", math=r"v = \sqrt{\frac{2(4.90\times10^{12})}{1.74\times10^{6}}} = 2.37\times10^{3}\ \text{m/s} = 2.37\ \text{km/s}"),
            S("Why it matters", text=r"The Moon's escape speed is about a fifth of Earth's, which is why it has kept almost no atmosphere: gas molecules can reach such speeds."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: how high does a slower launch reach?", intro=r"A probe leaves Earth's surface at 8.0 km/s straight up. How far from Earth's centre does it stop? Ignore air resistance and Earth's rotation.", steps=[
            S("Total energy per kg at launch", math=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}(8.0\times10^{3})^2 - \frac{3.98\times10^{14}}{6.37\times10^{6}} = 3.2\times10^{7} - 6.25\times10^{7} = -3.05\times10^{7}\ \text{J/kg}"),
            S("At the highest point the speed is zero, so $E = V = -GM/r$", math=r"r = \frac{3.98\times10^{14}}{3.05\times10^{7}} = 1.31\times10^{7}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Height above the surface", math=r"h = 1.31\times10^{7} - 6.37\times10^{6} = 6.7\times10^{6}\ \text{m}"),
        ]),
        TIP(r"Energy is conserved, so you can handle any launch question by writing $\tfrac{1}{2}v^2 + V$ at start and end (per kilogram). A negative total means the object is bound and cannot escape."),
        QUIZ(r"Why is the gravitational potential near a planet negative?",
             [r"Because gravity acts downwards", r"Because the zero of potential is at infinity and work is gained as a mass falls in", r"Because the planet's mass is negative", r"Because potential energy is always negative"], 1,
             r"The reference $V = 0$ is at infinity. Gravity is attractive, so the field does positive work as a mass moves in; the mass has less potential energy than at infinity, so $V < 0$.",
             hint=r"Where is $V = 0$ defined?",
             feedback=[r"'Downwards' is a direction; potential is a scalar and has no direction.", "", r"Mass is never negative. The sign comes from the choice of zero and the attractive force.", r"Potential energy can be positive (for example the energy of a stretched spring)."]),
        QUIZ(r"A planet has escape speed $v$. A second planet has the same mass but twice the radius. What is its escape speed?",
             [r"$v/2$", r"$v/\sqrt{2}$", r"$\sqrt{2}\,v$", r"$2v$"], 1,
             r"$v_{\text{esc}} = \sqrt{2GM/R}$. Doubling $R$ divides $v$ by $\sqrt{2}$.",
             hint=r"Escape speed depends on $1/\sqrt{R}$.",
             feedback=[r"This treats $v$ as proportional to $1/R$. It depends on $\sqrt{1/R}$.", "", r"Larger radius means weaker gravity at the surface, so the speed falls.", r"Larger radius lowers, not raises, the escape speed."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Define gravitational potential at a point in a gravitational field, and explain why it has a negative value.",
           r"It is the work done per unit mass in bringing a small test mass from infinity to that point. Infinity is taken as the zero of potential and gravity is attractive, so the field does work on the mass as it comes in; its potential energy decreases below zero, so the potential is negative."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the work needed to move a 500 kg satellite from Earth's surface to a point at $r = 2R$ from Earth's centre. ($GM = 3.98\times10^{14}$ m³/s², $R = 6.37\times10^{6}$ m.)",
           r"$\Delta W = m\left(V_2 - V_1\right) = 500\,GM\left(\dfrac{1}{R} - \dfrac{1}{2R}\right) = \dfrac{500\,GM}{2R} = \dfrac{500(3.98\times10^{14})}{2(6.37\times10^{6})} = 1.56\times10^{10}$ J."),
        PQ(r"Show that the escape speed from a planet of mass $M$ and radius $R$ is $\sqrt{2GM/R}$, and calculate it for a planet with $M = 6.42\times10^{23}$ kg and $R = 3.39\times10^{6}$ m.",
           r"Minimum launch energy takes the object just to infinity with zero kinetic energy: $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 - GMm/R = 0$, so $v = \sqrt{2GM/R}$. Here $GM = (6.67\times10^{-11})(6.42\times10^{23}) = 4.28\times10^{13}$, so $v = \sqrt{2(4.28\times10^{13})/(3.39\times10^{6})} = 5.02\times10^{3}$ m/s."),
        PQ(r"At the point between Earth and the Moon where the resultant field is zero, the potential is $-1.28\times10^{6}$ J/kg. Explain why the potential is not zero there even though the field is.",
           r"Field is the gradient of potential, so a zero field means the gradient is zero (a maximum of $V$ along the line), not that $V = 0$. Potential is the sum of two negative contributions, one from Earth and one from the Moon, so it is negative everywhere in the field."),
        TIP(r"Escape speed and energy to reach a given height are typical Paper 2 questions. Gravitational potential also appears in Paper 1 as short conceptual items, so learn the definition word by word."),
        SUM(r"Gravitational potential is the work per unit mass to bring a mass from infinity; $V = -GM/r$ is negative, a scalar, with $V = 0$ at infinity.",
            r"Potential energy $U = mV$ and the work needed is $\Delta W = m\Delta V$.",
            r"Field strength is the negative gradient of the potential: $g = -dV/dr$.",
            r"Escape speed $v = \sqrt{2GM/R}$ is independent of the mass of the object; Earth's is 11.2 km/s."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. Kepler's laws and satellites
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Kepler's laws and satellites",
    title="Kepler's laws, orbits and geostationary satellites",
    slug="phu1-kepler-satellites",
    minutes=24,
    summary="State Kepler's three laws, derive T^2 proportional to r^3 for circular orbits, find orbital speed and energy, and work out the radius of a geostationary orbit.",
    objectives=[
        "State Kepler's three laws and link the second to conservation of angular momentum",
        "Derive T^2 = 4 pi^2 r^3 / (GM) for a circular orbit and use it",
        "Calculate orbital speed and the energy of a satellite in orbit",
        "Explain the conditions for a geostationary orbit and find its radius",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Kepler's laws", "Orbital period", "Orbital speed", "Total energy of a satellite", "Geostationary orbit"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A satellite dish on a house in Douala points at a fixed spot in the sky and never moves. Yet the satellite it listens to is travelling at over 3 km/s. How can both be true? The answer is a special orbit, where the satellite takes exactly one day to circle Earth. To find it, we need the laws of planetary motion."),
        T(r"Between about 1609 and 1619 Johannes Kepler found three laws from careful observations of the planets. Newton later showed that they follow from his law of gravitation."),
        H("Kepler's three laws"),
        KEY("The three laws", r"They describe any body moving under an inverse-square gravitational force around a much heavier mass.", cards=[
            ["First law", "Each planet moves in an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus."],
            ["Second law", "The line from the Sun to a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal times."],
            ["Third law", r"The square of the orbital period is proportional to the cube of the mean distance from the Sun: $T^2 \propto r^3$."]]),
        T(r"**The second law** means that a planet moves fastest when closest to the Sun and slowest when farthest away. It is a statement of the conservation of **angular momentum**: gravity acts along the line to the Sun, so it has no turning effect about the Sun. For the two extreme points, $mv_p r_p = m v_a r_a$, so $v_p r_p = v_a r_a$ (perihelion $p$, aphelion $a$)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: speed changes in an elliptical orbit", intro=r"A planet (invented numbers) is $1.0\times10^{11}$ m from its star at closest approach, moving at $3.0\times10^{4}$ m/s. At its farthest point it is $1.5\times10^{11}$ m away. Find its speed there.", steps=[
            S("Conserve angular momentum", math=r"v_p r_p = v_a r_a"),
            S("Solve", math=r"v_a = \frac{(3.0\times10^{4})(1.0\times10^{11})}{1.5\times10^{11}} = 2.0\times10^{4}\ \text{m/s}"),
        ]),
        H("Deriving the third law for a circular orbit"),
        T(r"For a satellite of mass $m$ in a circular orbit of radius $r$ around a planet of mass $M$, gravity provides the centripetal force:"),
        F(r"\frac{GMm}{r^2} = m\omega^2 r = m\left(\frac{2\pi}{T}\right)^2 r"),
        T(r"The mass $m$ cancels. Rearranging:"),
        D(r"For a circular orbit about a mass $M$:", title="Kepler's third law (Newton's form)", formula=r"T^2 = \frac{4\pi^2}{GM}\,r^3",
          note=r"$r$ is the orbital radius measured from the centre of the planet. The period does not depend on the satellite's own mass."),
        T(r"The orbital speed follows from $v = \omega r = \dfrac{2\pi r}{T}$, or directly from $\dfrac{GMm}{r^2} = \dfrac{mv^2}{r}$:"),
        F(r"v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}"),
        T(r"A higher orbit means a longer period and a **slower** speed. This surprises many students: to catch up with a satellite ahead of you, you cannot just speed up, because speeding up moves you into a higher, slower orbit."),
        IMG("al-phu1-orbits.svg", alt="Earth with a small circular low orbit and a much larger circular geostationary orbit, with a satellite on each; a box lists the properties of the geostationary orbit.", caption="Figure 1. A low orbit and the geostationary orbit. Both are centred on Earth's centre."),
        EX("Worked example 2: the International Space Station", intro=r"Find the orbital period and speed of a satellite 400 km above Earth. Use $GM = 3.98\times10^{14}$ m³/s² and $R = 6.37\times10^{6}$ m.", steps=[
            S("Orbital radius", math=r"r = 6.37\times10^{6} + 4.0\times10^{5} = 6.77\times10^{6}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Period", math=r"T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{(6.77\times10^{6})^3}{3.98\times10^{14}}} = 5.55\times10^{3}\ \text{s} = 92.5\ \text{min}"),
            S("Speed", math=r"v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} = \sqrt{\frac{3.98\times10^{14}}{6.77\times10^{6}}} = 7.67\times10^{3}\ \text{m/s}"),
            S("Meaning", text="The station circles Earth about 15.6 times a day."),
        ]),
        H("Geostationary orbit"),
        D(r"A **geostationary satellite** stays above the same point on Earth's surface. It must (1) orbit above the equator, (2) move in the same direction as Earth's spin (west to east), and (3) have a period equal to Earth's rotation period, about 24 hours.", title="Geostationary orbit"),
        T(r"Only the equator works. A satellite not above the equator would have an orbit whose plane passes through Earth's centre, so it would swing north and south of the equator and could not stay above one point."),
        EX("Worked example 3: radius of the geostationary orbit", intro=r"Calculate the orbital radius and the height above the surface. Use $T = 24$ h $= 86\,400$ s, $GM = 3.98\times10^{14}$ m³/s².", steps=[
            S("Rearrange Kepler's third law", math=r"r^3 = \frac{GMT^2}{4\pi^2} = \frac{(3.98\times10^{14})(86\,400)^2}{4\pi^2} = 7.53\times10^{22}\ \text{m}^3"),
            S("Cube root", math=r"r = 4.22\times10^{7}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Height above the surface", math=r"h = 4.22\times10^{7} - 6.37\times10^{6} = 3.58\times10^{7}\ \text{m} \approx 36\,000\ \text{km}"),
            S("Speed", math=r"v = \frac{2\pi r}{T} = \frac{2\pi(4.22\times10^{7})}{86\,400} = 3.07\times10^{3}\ \text{m/s}"),
        ], check=r"v = \sqrt{GM/r} = \sqrt{3.98\times10^{14}/4.22\times10^{7}} = 3.07\times10^{3}\ \text{m/s}"),
        TIP(r"Strictly, Earth turns once in 23 h 56 min (the sidereal day), which gives $r = 4.22\times10^{7}$ m as well, to three figures. Using 24 h is accepted in examinations."),
        H("Energy of a satellite"),
        T(r"In a circular orbit, kinetic energy is $E_k = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 = \dfrac{GMm}{2r}$. Gravitational potential energy is $E_p = -\dfrac{GMm}{r}$. Adding:"),
        F(r"E_{\text{total}} = \frac{GMm}{2r} - \frac{GMm}{r} = -\frac{GMm}{2r}"),
        T(r"The total energy is negative, so the satellite is **bound**. It is equal to minus the kinetic energy, and half the potential energy. A satellite that loses energy to air drag falls to a lower orbit, where its total energy is more negative, yet it **speeds up** because the kinetic energy increases. The lost energy goes into heat."),
        MISTAKE("Using the height as the orbital radius", r"In $T^2 = 4\pi^2r^3/GM$, $r$ is measured from the centre of the planet, so $r = R + h$. Using $h$ alone gives a wrong period."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking a heavier satellite orbits faster", r"The satellite's mass cancels. Any satellite at the same radius has the same speed and period."),
        MISTAKE("Saying 'gravity is zero in orbit'", r"Gravity is what keeps the satellite in orbit. At the geostationary radius $g$ is still $0.22$ N/kg. Objects feel weightless because they are in free fall."),
        QUIZ(r"Two satellites orbit the same planet. Satellite B has an orbital radius four times that of satellite A. What is the ratio of the period of B to the period of A?",
             [r"4", r"8", r"16", r"2"], 1,
             r"$T^2 \propto r^3$, so $T \propto r^{3/2}$. For $r_B = 4r_A$, $T_B/T_A = 4^{3/2} = 8$.",
             hint=r"Use $T \propto r^{3/2}$.",
             feedback=[r"This treats $T$ as proportional to $r$. The law is $T^2 \propto r^3$.", "", r"This is $r^2$; the power for $T$ is $3/2$, not 2.", r"This is $\sqrt{4}$; you used $T \propto r^{1/2}$."]),
        QUIZ(r"Which of these is NOT a condition for a geostationary orbit?",
             [r"It lies in the plane of the equator", r"Its period is about 24 hours", r"It travels from east to west, opposite to Earth's spin", r"Its orbital radius is about $4.2\times10^{7}$ m"], 2,
             r"The satellite must move in the same direction as Earth rotates, from west to east. East to west would not keep it above a fixed point.",
             hint=r"Think of two runners on a track going the same way at the same angular speed.",
             feedback=[r"This is a real condition.", r"This is a real condition.", "", r"This is the correct radius for a 24 h period."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"State Kepler's second law and explain how it follows from the conservation of angular momentum.",
           r"A line from the planet to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times. Gravity acts along the line joining planet and Sun, so it has no moment about the Sun and the planet's angular momentum $mvr\sin\theta$ about the Sun is constant. When $r$ is smaller the speed must be larger, and the area swept per second, $\tfrac{1}{2}vr\sin\theta$, stays constant."),
        PQ(r"Show that for a satellite in circular orbit of radius $r$ about a mass $M$, $T^2 = \dfrac{4\pi^2r^3}{GM}$.",
           r"Gravity provides the centripetal force: $\dfrac{GMm}{r^2} = m\omega^2r$ with $\omega = \dfrac{2\pi}{T}$. So $\dfrac{GM}{r^2} = \dfrac{4\pi^2}{T^2}r$, giving $T^2 = \dfrac{4\pi^2r^3}{GM}$."),
        PQ(r"A satellite orbits Earth with a period of 2.0 hours. Find its orbital radius and its height above the surface. ($GM = 3.98\times10^{14}$ m³/s², $R = 6.37\times10^{6}$ m.)",
           r"$T = 7200$ s. $r^3 = \dfrac{GMT^2}{4\pi^2} = \dfrac{(3.98\times10^{14})(7200)^2}{4\pi^2} = 5.23\times10^{20}$ m³, so $r = 8.05\times10^{6}$ m. Height $= 8.05\times10^{6} - 6.37\times10^{6} = 1.7\times10^{6}$ m."),
        PQ(r"A 1000 kg satellite is moved from a 400 km circular orbit ($r = 6.77\times10^{6}$ m) to a geostationary orbit ($r = 4.22\times10^{7}$ m). Find the change in its total energy. ($GM = 3.98\times10^{14}$ m³/s².)",
           r"$E = -\dfrac{GMm}{2r}$. $\Delta E = \dfrac{GMm}{2}\left(\dfrac{1}{r_1} - \dfrac{1}{r_2}\right) = \dfrac{(3.98\times10^{14})(1000)}{2}\left(\dfrac{1}{6.77\times10^{6}} - \dfrac{1}{4.22\times10^{7}}\right) = 2.5\times10^{10}$ J (an increase, since $E$ becomes less negative)."),
        TIP(r"Satellites and Kepler's third law are tested in Paper 1 (ratios of periods and radii) and Paper 2 (derivations and geostationary data). Check whether the question gives $R$ and the height or the radius from the centre."),
        SUM(r"Kepler: elliptical orbits (1), equal areas in equal times (2, conservation of angular momentum), $T^2 \propto r^3$ (3).",
            r"For a circular orbit, $T^2 = 4\pi^2r^3/GM$ and $v = \sqrt{GM/r}$; both are independent of the satellite's mass.",
            r"Total energy in orbit is $-GMm/2r$: bound and negative; lower orbits are faster.",
            r"A geostationary orbit is above the equator, west to east, with period about 24 h, radius $4.22\times10^{7}$ m, height about $3.6\times10^{7}$ m."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4. Capacitors and dielectrics
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Capacitors and dielectrics",
    title="Capacitance, parallel-plate capacitors and dielectrics",
    slug="phu1-capacitors-dielectrics",
    minutes=23,
    summary="Define capacitance, derive C = epsilon0 A / d for parallel plates, explain what a dielectric does, and combine capacitors in series and in parallel.",
    objectives=[
        "Define capacitance and the farad, and use C = Q/V",
        "Derive and use C = epsilon0 epsilon_r A / d for a parallel-plate capacitor",
        "Explain how a dielectric increases capacitance by polarisation",
        "Find the total capacitance and the charge or pd on each capacitor in series and parallel combinations",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Capacitance", "Farad", "Parallel-plate capacitor", "Relative permittivity", "Dielectric", "Capacitors in series and parallel"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Inside the electronic flash of a camera, a phone charger and the starter unit of a ceiling fan, there are **capacitors**: components that store electric charge. A capacitor is two conductors close together, separated by an insulator. When you connect it to a battery, charge flows on to one plate and off the other until the capacitor's pd equals the battery's pd."),
        H("Capacitance"),
        D(r"**Capacitance** $C$ is the charge stored per unit potential difference across a capacitor. The unit is the **farad** (F): one farad is one coulomb per volt.", title="Capacitance", formula=r"C = \frac{Q}{V}",
          note=r"$Q$ is the magnitude of the charge on either plate (the capacitor as a whole is neutral). Typical values: picofarads (pF) to millifarads (mF); 1 F is very large."),
        T(r"Capacitance is a property of the **shape** and **materials** of the capacitor. It does not depend on $Q$ or $V$: doubling $V$ doubles $Q$ and $C$ stays the same."),
        H("Parallel-plate capacitor"),
        T(r"Two parallel plates of area $A$ are a distance $d$ apart in a vacuum (air is almost the same). If the charge on a plate is $Q$, the electric field between the plates (from Lower Sixth) is uniform:"),
        F(r"E = \frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon_0} = \frac{Q}{\varepsilon_0 A}"),
        T(r"Here $\sigma = Q/A$ is the charge per unit area and $\varepsilon_0 = 8.85\times10^{-12}$ F/m is the **permittivity of free space**. The pd across the gap is $V = Ed$, so"),
        F(r"V = \frac{Qd}{\varepsilon_0 A} \;\Rightarrow\; C = \frac{Q}{V} = \frac{\varepsilon_0 A}{d}"),
        T(r"Capacitance is bigger for larger plates and a smaller gap. That is why practical capacitors use large foils rolled into a small can."),
        H("Dielectrics"),
        T(r"A **dielectric** is an insulating material placed between the plates. Its molecules become **polarised**: the electric field pulls the positive and negative charges slightly apart, so each molecule becomes a tiny dipole with its negative end towards the positive plate. The dipoles' ends facing the plates produce an electric field in the opposite direction to the field of the plates, which reduces the net field in the gap."),
        IMG("al-phu1-capacitor-dielectric.svg", alt="Two plates with a dielectric between them. Each molecule is drawn as a tall oval with its negative end towards the positive plate.", caption="Figure 1. A dielectric between the plates of a capacitor. The polarised molecules reduce the field inside."),
        D(r"The **relative permittivity** $\varepsilon_r$ of a material is the factor by which it multiplies the capacitance, compared with a vacuum. It has no unit.", title="Dielectric effect", formula=r"C = \frac{\varepsilon_0\varepsilon_r A}{d}",
          note=r"Some typical values of $\varepsilon_r$: air about 1.0006, paper about 3, mica about 6, pure water about 80. For a vacuum $\varepsilon_r = 1$. (Values depend on the source and conditions: check your textbook.)"),
        T(r"For the same charge $Q$ the field in the gap, and therefore the pd, is reduced by the factor $\varepsilon_r$. Since $C = Q/V$, $C$ increases by $\varepsilon_r$. A dielectric also lets the plates be placed very close without touching, which further increases $C$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a parallel-plate capacitor", intro=r"A capacitor has plates of area $0.020$ m² separated by $1.0$ mm of air. (a) Find its capacitance. (b) A sheet of dielectric with $\varepsilon_r = 5$ fills the gap. Find the new capacitance. (c) The capacitor is charged to $12$ V with the dielectric in place. Find the charge stored.", steps=[
            S("(a) Air", math=r"C = \frac{\varepsilon_0 A}{d} = \frac{(8.85\times10^{-12})(0.020)}{1.0\times10^{-3}} = 1.77\times10^{-10}\ \text{F} = 177\ \text{pF}"),
            S("(b) Dielectric", math=r"C' = \varepsilon_r C = 5 \times 177 = 885\ \text{pF}"),
            S("(c) Charge", math=r"Q = C'V = (8.85\times10^{-10})(12) = 1.06\times10^{-8}\ \text{C} = 10.6\ \text{nC}"),
        ]),
        H("Capacitors in parallel and in series"),
        D(r"In parallel, every capacitor has the **same pd**, and the charges add: $Q = Q_1 + Q_2 + \cdots$ Since $Q = CV$:", title="Parallel", formula=r"C_{\text{total}} = C_1 + C_2 + C_3 + \cdots",
          note="Joining capacitors in parallel is like making one with bigger plate area."),
        D(r"In series, every capacitor holds the **same charge** $Q$ (charge cannot cross the gaps; it only moves through the connecting wires), and the pds add: $V = V_1 + V_2 + \cdots$ Dividing by $Q$:", title="Series", formula=r"\frac{1}{C_{\text{total}}} = \frac{1}{C_1} + \frac{1}{C_2} + \frac{1}{C_3} + \cdots",
          note="Joining in series is like making the gap thicker, so the total is smaller than the smallest capacitor."),
        T(r"Capacitors combine **opposite** to resistors: reciprocals for series, a simple sum for parallel."),
        EX("Worked example 2: two capacitors in series", intro=r"A $4.0\ \mu$F and a $12\ \mu$F capacitor are in series across a $100$ V supply. Find the charge on each and the pd across each.", steps=[
            S("Total capacitance", math=r"\frac{1}{C} = \frac{1}{4.0} + \frac{1}{12} = \frac{4}{12} \;\Rightarrow\; C = 3.0\ \mu\text{F}"),
            S("Charge (same on both)", math=r"Q = CV = (3.0\times10^{-6})(100) = 3.0\times10^{-4}\ \text{C} = 300\ \mu\text{C}"),
            S("pd across each", math=r"V_1 = \frac{300}{4.0} = 75\ \text{V}, \qquad V_2 = \frac{300}{12} = 25\ \text{V}"),
        ], check=r"75 + 25 = 100\ \text{V}"),
        T(r"The smaller capacitor takes the larger share of the pd. This is why capacitors in series must be chosen with care: the voltage rating of the small one can be exceeded."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a dielectric inserted into an isolated capacitor", intro=r"A $100$ pF air capacitor is charged to $200$ V and then disconnected from the supply. A dielectric with $\varepsilon_r = 4$ is slid fully between the plates. Find the new pd.", steps=[
            S("Charge before", math=r"Q = CV = (100\times10^{-12})(200) = 2.0\times10^{-8}\ \text{C}"),
            S("The capacitor is isolated, so $Q$ stays constant. New capacitance", math=r"C' = 4 \times 100 = 400\ \text{pF}"),
            S("New pd", math=r"V' = \frac{Q}{C'} = \frac{2.0\times10^{-8}}{400\times10^{-12}} = 50\ \text{V}"),
        ]),
        TIP(r"When a dielectric is inserted, ask first: is the supply still connected? If yes, $V$ stays fixed and $Q$ changes. If not, $Q$ stays fixed and $V$ changes."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the series formula for parallel capacitors", r"Parallel capacitors simply add. In series, you add the reciprocals and then invert, so the total is smaller than the smallest."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting to invert", r"After adding $1/C_1 + 1/C_2$, the result is $1/C_{\text{total}}$. Always turn it over. For $3\ \mu$F and $6\ \mu$F in series the total is $2\ \mu$F, not $0.5$."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing units", r"Convert microfarads and picofarads to farads before using SI formulae with $\varepsilon_0$, or keep every capacitance in the same unit and track it."),
        QUIZ(r"Three capacitors of $2\ \mu$F, $3\ \mu$F and $6\ \mu$F are connected in series. What is the total capacitance?",
             [r"$11\ \mu$F", r"$1\ \mu$F", r"$2.2\ \mu$F", r"$0.5\ \mu$F"], 1,
             r"$\frac{1}{C} = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6} = \frac{3 + 2 + 1}{6} = 1$, so $C = 1\ \mu$F.",
             hint=r"Add the reciprocals, then invert.",
             feedback=[r"$11\ \mu$F is the total if they were in parallel.", "", r"Recheck the sum of reciprocals: it is exactly 1, so the total is not 2.2 µF.", r"Recheck the sum of reciprocals: it is exactly 1, so $C = 1\ \mu$F, not $0.5\ \mu$F."]),
        QUIZ(r"The plates of an air capacitor are moved twice as far apart (area unchanged). What happens to the capacitance?",
             [r"It doubles", r"It halves", r"It quadruples", r"It is unchanged"], 1,
             r"$C = \varepsilon_0A/d$, so doubling $d$ halves $C$.",
             hint=r"Look at the formula $C = \varepsilon_0 A/d$.",
             feedback=[r"$C$ is inversely proportional to $d$, so it decreases.", "", r"$C$ is not related to $d^2$.", r"Capacitance depends on the geometry, including $d$."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Define capacitance. A capacitor stores $4.8\ \mu$C when the pd across it is $12$ V. What is its capacitance?",
           r"Capacitance is the charge stored per unit pd across the capacitor, $C = Q/V$. $C = \dfrac{4.8\times10^{-6}}{12} = 4.0\times10^{-7}$ F $= 0.40\ \mu$F."),
        PQ(r"Plates of area $0.010$ m² are $0.50$ mm apart in air. Calculate the capacitance and the electric field between the plates when the pd is $12$ V.",
           r"$C = \dfrac{\varepsilon_0A}{d} = \dfrac{(8.85\times10^{-12})(0.010)}{0.50\times10^{-3}} = 1.77\times10^{-10}$ F $= 177$ pF. $E = \dfrac{V}{d} = \dfrac{12}{0.50\times10^{-3}} = 2.4\times10^{4}$ V/m."),
        PQ(r"Explain, using polarisation, why placing a dielectric between the plates of a charged, isolated capacitor reduces the pd across it.",
           r"The field of the plates polarises the molecules: positive charge shifts slightly towards the negative plate and negative charge towards the positive plate. The dipole ends next to the plates carry charge opposite to the plate charge and set up a field opposing the plates' field, so the net field in the gap falls. $V = Ed$, so the pd falls while the plate charge $Q$ stays the same; $C = Q/V$ increases."),
        PQ(r"A $2.0\ \mu$F capacitor is in series with a parallel pair of $3.0\ \mu$F and $6.0\ \mu$F capacitors. Find the total capacitance. If the combination is connected to $18$ V, find the pd across the $2.0\ \mu$F capacitor.",
           r"The parallel pair gives $3.0 + 6.0 = 9.0\ \mu$F. In series with $2.0\ \mu$F: $\dfrac{1}{C} = \dfrac{1}{2.0} + \dfrac{1}{9.0} = \dfrac{11}{18}$, so $C = 1.64\ \mu$F. Charge $Q = CV = (1.64\times10^{-6})(18) = 2.95\times10^{-5}$ C. The pd across the $2.0\ \mu$F capacitor is $\dfrac{Q}{C_1} = \dfrac{2.95\times10^{-5}}{2.0\times10^{-6}} = 14.7$ V (14.7 V ≈ 18 × 9/11)."),
        TIP(r"Capacitor calculations (series, parallel, a dielectric inserted) are common in Paper 1 and Paper 2. In Paper 3 you may measure capacitance using a circuit with a known resistor and a stopwatch."),
        SUM(r"$C = Q/V$ (farad); it depends on geometry and materials, not on $Q$ or $V$.",
            r"Parallel plates: $C = \varepsilon_0\varepsilon_rA/d$; a dielectric polarises and reduces the field, so $C$ rises by $\varepsilon_r$.",
            r"Parallel: $C = C_1 + C_2 + \cdots$ with the same pd. Series: $1/C = 1/C_1 + 1/C_2 + \cdots$ with the same charge.",
            r"For a dielectric insertion decide whether $Q$ or $V$ is held constant."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. Energy stored in a capacitor
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Energy stored in a capacitor",
    title="Energy stored in a capacitor",
    slug="phu1-capacitor-energy",
    minutes=21,
    summary="Show that a charged capacitor stores energy E = 1/2 QV = 1/2 CV^2 = Q^2/2C, find it from a Q-V graph, and apply it to flash units, defibrillators and charge sharing.",
    objectives=[
        "Derive the energy stored from the area under a charge-pd graph",
        "Choose the correct form of E = 1/2 QV, 1/2 CV^2 or Q^2/2C for a problem",
        "Calculate the energy lost when charge is shared between capacitors",
        "Find the work done when the plate separation or dielectric of an isolated capacitor changes",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Energy stored in a capacitor", "Q-V graph", "Charge sharing", "Energy density", "Power from a capacitor"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A camera flash fires within about a thousandth of a second. A hospital defibrillator gives a patient's heart a short, strong pulse. Neither could use a battery directly: a battery cannot deliver energy that fast. Instead, they charge a capacitor slowly and release its stored energy suddenly. In this lesson you will calculate how much energy is stored."),
        H("Where does the energy come from?"),
        T(r"To charge a capacitor, you must move charge from one plate to the other against the pd that has already built up. Early charge moves easily, because the pd is small. Later charge is harder to move, because the pd is bigger. A small charge $\Delta Q$ moved when the pd is $V$ needs work $V\,\Delta Q$."),
        T(r"For a capacitor, $V = Q/C$, so a graph of $V$ against $Q$ is a straight line through the origin with gradient $1/C$. The work done to raise the charge from $0$ to $Q$ is the **area under the graph**, a triangle."),
        D(r"The energy stored in a capacitor charged to charge $Q$ at pd $V$ is the area of the triangle under the $V$-$Q$ graph:", title="Energy stored", formula=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}QV = \tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 = \frac{Q^2}{2C}",
          note=r"Using $Q = CV$ you can switch between the three forms. Choose the one that matches the quantities you know."),
        KEY("Which form should I use?", "Look at what is given and what stays constant.", cards=[
            [r"$\frac{1}{2}CV^2$", r"When the capacitor is connected to a fixed pd (a battery)."],
            [r"$\frac{Q^2}{2C}$", r"When the charge is fixed (an isolated capacitor)."],
            [r"$\frac{1}{2}QV$", r"When you know the charge and the pd, as read from a graph."]]),
        T(r"Note the factor $\tfrac{1}{2}$. The average pd while charging is half the final pd, so the energy is not $QV$. The missing half in a battery circuit is discussed in the RC charging lesson."),
        EX("Worked example 1: energy in a smoothing capacitor and a flash unit", intro=r"(a) A $470\ \mu$F capacitor is charged to $12$ V. Find the energy stored. (b) A camera flash uses a $1500\ \mu$F capacitor charged to $300$ V and discharges it in $2.0$ ms. Find the energy and the average power.", steps=[
            S("(a) Energy", math=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(470\times10^{-6})(12)^2 = 3.4\times10^{-2}\ \text{J} = 34\ \text{mJ}"),
            S("(b) Energy", math=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}(1500\times10^{-6})(300)^2 = 67.5\ \text{J}"),
            S("(b) Average power", math=r"P = \frac{E}{t} = \frac{67.5}{2.0\times10^{-3}} = 3.4\times10^{4}\ \text{W} = 34\ \text{kW}"),
        ]),
        T(r"The flash lamp's power of about 34 kW is far beyond what a small battery could supply, but the energy is only 67.5 J, about the energy to lift a 7 kg object by 1 m. This is the key advantage of a capacitor: it can deliver **energy very quickly**, while a battery stores much more energy but releases it slowly."),
        IMG("al-phu1-rc-curves.svg", alt="Two graphs of potential difference against time: charging rises from zero towards the supply value; discharging falls from the supply value towards zero.", caption="Figure 1. Charging and discharging are not instant. How fast a capacitor gives up its energy depends on the resistance in the circuit (next lessons)."),
        H("Charge sharing: where does the energy go?"),
        T(r"Connect a charged capacitor to an uncharged one. Charge flows from the first to the second until both have the same pd. Charge is conserved, but **energy is not**: some is lost as heat in the connecting wires and as a small spark or electromagnetic radiation."),
        EX("Worked example 2: sharing charge", intro=r"A $4.0\ \mu$F capacitor is charged to $50$ V and then connected, by wires of negligible resistance, across an uncharged $6.0\ \mu$F capacitor. Find the final pd and the energy lost.", steps=[
            S("Initial charge", math=r"Q = CV = (4.0\times10^{-6})(50) = 2.0\times10^{-4}\ \text{C}"),
            S("The charge is shared; the capacitors are now in parallel", math=r"C_{\text{total}} = 10\ \mu\text{F},\quad V = \frac{Q}{C_{\text{total}}} = \frac{2.0\times10^{-4}}{10\times10^{-6}} = 20\ \text{V}"),
            S("Energy before", math=r"E_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}(4.0\times10^{-6})(50)^2 = 5.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{J}"),
            S("Energy after", math=r"E_2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(10\times10^{-6})(20)^2 = 2.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{J}"),
            S("Energy lost", math=r"E_1 - E_2 = 3.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{J}\ (60\%\ \text{of the original})"),
        ]),
        H("Work done on an isolated capacitor"),
        T(r"If a capacitor is isolated, its charge is fixed and $E = Q^2/2C$. Pulling the plates apart lowers $C$, so $E$ rises. The extra energy is the work you do pulling against the attraction between the plates. Sliding a dielectric in does the opposite: $C$ rises and the stored energy falls, because the dielectric is pulled in."),
        EX("Worked example 3: pulling the plates apart", intro=r"A $100$ pF air capacitor is charged to $200$ V and isolated. The plate separation is doubled. Find the new pd and the work done.", steps=[
            S("Initial charge and energy", math=r"Q = (100\times10^{-12})(200) = 2.0\times10^{-8}\ \text{C},\quad E_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}QV = 2.0\times10^{-6}\ \text{J}"),
            S("Doubling $d$ halves $C$; $Q$ is fixed", math=r"C' = 50\ \text{pF},\quad V' = \frac{Q}{C'} = 400\ \text{V}"),
            S("New energy", math=r"E_2 = \frac{Q^2}{2C'} = \frac{(2.0\times10^{-8})^2}{2(50\times10^{-12})} = 4.0\times10^{-6}\ \text{J}"),
            S("Work done by you", math=r"W = E_2 - E_1 = 2.0\times10^{-6}\ \text{J}"),
        ]),
        T(r"**Energy density.** Dividing the energy $\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2$ by the volume $Ad$ of the gap gives the energy per unit volume, $\tfrac{1}{2}\varepsilon_0E^2$ for an air-filled capacitor. The energy is stored in the electric field itself. (This extension is not needed for most questions: check your syllabus.)"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using QV without the half", r"The energy is $\tfrac{1}{2}QV$ because the pd rises from $0$ to $V$ as the capacitor charges. Using $QV$ doubles the answer."),
        MISTAKE("Expecting energy to be conserved when capacitors are connected", r"Charge is conserved when capacitors are joined. Energy is not: some is lost as heat and radiation. Always find the final pd from the charge, then find the final energy separately."),
        MISTAKE(r"Using $\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2$ when $V$ changes", r"If the capacitor is isolated, $V$ changes when $C$ changes, so use $Q^2/2C$ with the fixed $Q$."),
        QUIZ(r"A $2000\ \mu$F capacitor is charged to $10$ V. How much energy does it store?",
             [r"$0.10$ J", r"$0.20$ J", r"$20$ J", r"$100$ J"], 0,
             r"$E = \tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(2.0\times10^{-3})(100) = 0.10$ J.",
             hint=r"$2000\ \mu\text{F} = 2.0\times10^{-3}$ F.",
             feedback=["", r"This is $CV^2$ without the $\tfrac{1}{2}$.", r"The capacitance was not converted from microfarads to farads correctly.", r"This is not obtained from the data given."]),
        QUIZ(r"Two identical capacitors, one charged to $V$ and one uncharged, are connected together. What fraction of the initial energy remains?",
             [r"All of it", r"One half", r"One quarter", r"None"], 1,
             r"The final pd is $V/2$ and the total capacitance is $2C$, so $E_f = \tfrac{1}{2}(2C)(V/2)^2 = \tfrac{1}{4}CV^2$, which is half of $E_i = \tfrac{1}{2}CV^2$.",
             hint=r"Find the final pd first from charge conservation.",
             feedback=[r"Energy is lost as heat and radiation when charge flows through the connecting wires.", "", r"A quarter of $CV^2$ is half of the initial energy $\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2$.", r"Half the energy remains stored."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Sketch a graph of pd against charge for a capacitor and show how it gives the energy stored.",
           r"The graph is a straight line through the origin: $V = Q/C$, gradient $1/C$. The work done adding a small charge $\Delta Q$ at pd $V$ is $V\Delta Q$, the area of a thin strip. The total work from $0$ to $Q$ is the area of the triangle: $E = \tfrac{1}{2}QV$."),
        PQ(r"A defibrillator uses a $100\ \mu$F capacitor charged to $2.0$ kV. Calculate the energy stored. If it is released in $5.0$ ms, find the average power.",
           r"$E = \tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(100\times10^{-6})(2000)^2 = 200$ J. $P = E/t = 200/(5.0\times10^{-3}) = 4.0\times10^{4}$ W $= 40$ kW."),
        PQ(r"A $2.0\ \mu$F capacitor charged to $60$ V is connected across an uncharged $3.0\ \mu$F capacitor. Find the final pd and the energy lost.",
           r"$Q = (2.0\times10^{-6})(60) = 1.2\times10^{-4}$ C. Final $V = \dfrac{Q}{5.0\times10^{-6}} = 24$ V. $E_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}(2.0\times10^{-6})(60)^2 = 3.6\times10^{-3}$ J. $E_2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(5.0\times10^{-6})(24)^2 = 1.44\times10^{-3}$ J. Lost: $2.16\times10^{-3}$ J."),
        PQ(r"A $100$ pF air capacitor, isolated and charged to $200$ V, has a dielectric of $\varepsilon_r = 4$ inserted. Show that the stored energy falls, and say where the energy goes.",
           r"Before: $E_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 = 2.0\times10^{-6}$ J. $Q = 2.0\times10^{-8}$ C stays constant; $C' = 400$ pF, so $E_2 = \dfrac{Q^2}{2C'} = \dfrac{(2.0\times10^{-8})^2}{2(400\times10^{-12})} = 5.0\times10^{-7}$ J, a quarter of the original. The dielectric is pulled into the gap by the field, so the field does work on it as it moves in; the energy transfers to the kinetic energy of the dielectric (and then to heat if it is brought to rest)."),
        TIP(r"The three forms of capacitor energy are often tested in Paper 1 with a change (plates moved, dielectric inserted, capacitors connected). In Paper 2, expect energy-loss calculations."),
        SUM(r"The energy stored is the area under the $V$-$Q$ graph: $E = \tfrac{1}{2}QV = \tfrac{1}{2}CV^2 = Q^2/2C$.",
            r"Use $\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2$ at fixed $V$ and $Q^2/2C$ at fixed $Q$.",
            r"When charged and uncharged capacitors are joined, charge is conserved but some energy is lost as heat and radiation.",
            r"Capacitors deliver energy very quickly (flash units, defibrillators), so the power can be very large."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6. RC discharging
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Charging and discharging an RC circuit",
    title="Discharging a capacitor through a resistor",
    slug="phu1-rc-discharging",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Derive Q = Q0 e^(-t/RC) for a discharging capacitor, define the time constant and half-life, and use logarithms to find RC from measurements.",
    objectives=[
        "Derive the exponential decay of charge, pd and current when a capacitor discharges through a resistor",
        "Define and use the time constant tau = RC and the half-life t = RC ln 2",
        "Calculate charge, pd, current and time at any stage of a discharge",
        "Use a graph of ln V against t to find RC and the capacitance",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Exponential decay", "Time constant", "Half-life of discharge", "Natural logarithm graph", "Leakage"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Switch off a TV in a hall and the screen takes a moment to fade. Unplug a phone charger and a small LED may glow for a second before going dark. In both cases a charged capacitor is draining through a resistance. Does it drain at a steady rate? No. The larger the pd, the larger the current, so the capacitor discharges quickly at first and then more slowly. The result is an **exponential decay**, the same mathematics you will meet in radioactive decay."),
        H("The equation of a discharging capacitor"),
        T(r"A capacitor of capacitance $C$, charged to $Q_0$ (pd $V_0 = Q_0/C$), is connected across a resistor $R$ at time $t = 0$. At a later time, the charge on the capacitor is $Q$, the pd across it is $V = Q/C$ and the current is $I$."),
        T(r"The pd across the capacitor drives the current through the resistor: $V = IR$. The current is the rate at which the capacitor loses charge, so $I = -\dfrac{dQ}{dt}$. Hence"),
        F(r"\frac{Q}{C} = -R\frac{dQ}{dt} \;\Rightarrow\; \frac{dQ}{dt} = -\frac{Q}{RC}"),
        T(r"The rate of loss of charge is proportional to the charge remaining. This differential equation has the solution"),
        D(r"The charge, pd and current all fall exponentially from their initial values:", title="Discharge equations", formula=r"Q = Q_0e^{-t/RC},\qquad V = V_0e^{-t/RC},\qquad I = I_0e^{-t/RC}",
          note=r"$I_0 = V_0/R$ is the initial current. The same exponent applies to $Q$, $V$ and $I$, because $Q = CV$ and $V = IR$."),
        T(r"Check by differentiating: $\dfrac{dQ}{dt} = -\dfrac{1}{RC}Q_0e^{-t/RC} = -\dfrac{Q}{RC}$, which matches the differential equation."),
        H("The time constant and the half-life"),
        D(r"The **time constant** is $\tau = RC$. It is the time for the charge (or pd or current) to fall to $1/e = 0.368$ (about 37%) of its initial value. Unit: seconds ($\Omega \times$ F = s).", title="Time constant", formula=r"\tau = RC"),
        T(r"After $t = \tau$ the quantity is $e^{-1} = 0.368$ of its start; after $2\tau$ it is $e^{-2} = 0.135$; after $5\tau$ it is $e^{-5} = 0.0067$, less than 1%, so after about $5\tau$ the capacitor is effectively empty. The **half-life** is the time to fall to one half, found from $e^{-t_{1/2}/RC} = \tfrac{1}{2}$:"),
        F(r"t_{1/2} = RC\ln 2 = 0.693\,RC"),
        IMG("al-phu1-rc-curves.svg", alt="Charging and discharging curves of pd against time for R = 20 kilohm and C = 500 microfarad, so the time constant is 10 s. The discharge curve falls to 37 per cent at 10 s.", caption="Figure 1. The right-hand curve is the discharge. A larger $RC$ means a slower decay."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a discharge calculation", intro=r"A $500\ \mu$F capacitor charged to $12$ V discharges through a $20\ \text{k}\Omega$ resistor. Find (a) the time constant, (b) the initial current, (c) the pd after $15$ s, (d) the time for the pd to fall to $3.0$ V.", steps=[
            S("(a) Time constant", math=r"\tau = RC = (20\times10^{3})(500\times10^{-6}) = 10\ \text{s}"),
            S("(b) Initial current", math=r"I_0 = \frac{V_0}{R} = \frac{12}{20\times10^{3}} = 6.0\times10^{-4}\ \text{A} = 0.60\ \text{mA}"),
            S("(c) pd after 15 s", math=r"V = 12e^{-15/10} = 12(0.223) = 2.68\ \text{V}"),
            S("(d) Take natural logs of $3.0 = 12e^{-t/10}$", math=r"\ln\tfrac{12}{3.0} = \frac{t}{10} \;\Rightarrow\; t = 10\ln 4 = 13.9\ \text{s}"),
        ], check=r"12e^{-13.86/10} = 12e^{-1.386} = 3.00\ \text{V}"),
        TIP(r"Use natural logarithms ($\ln$ on your calculator) to take $e$ out of an exponent. Be careful: $\ln$ is not the same as $\log_{10}$."),
        H("Finding RC from measurements: the logarithm graph"),
        T(r"In the laboratory you measure $V$ at several times with a voltmeter and a stopwatch. Taking natural logarithms of $V = V_0e^{-t/RC}$ gives a straight-line equation:"),
        F(r"\ln V = \ln V_0 - \frac{t}{RC}"),
        T(r"Compare with $y = c + mx$: a graph of $\ln V$ (vertical) against $t$ (horizontal) is a straight line with gradient $-\dfrac{1}{RC}$ and intercept $\ln V_0$. Using a straight line is better than reading a curve because errors show up as scatter, and the gradient is measured over a long baseline."),
        IMG("al-phu1-rc-log-graph.svg", alt="A straight line sloping down on axes of ln V against time, passing through ln 12 = 2.48 at zero and falling by 2.0 over 20 seconds, so its gradient is minus 0.10 per second.", caption="Figure 2. For $V_0 = 12$ V and $RC = 10$ s the logarithm graph is a straight line of gradient -0.10 per second."),
        EX("Worked example 2: finding a capacitance", intro=r"A student discharges an unknown capacitor through a $100\ \text{k}\Omega$ resistor. The pd falls from $6.0$ V at $t = 0$ to $2.2$ V at $t = 50$ s. Find the capacitance.", steps=[
            S("Take logs of the ratio", math=r"\ln\frac{V_0}{V} = \frac{t}{RC} \;\Rightarrow\; \ln\frac{6.0}{2.2} = 1.003"),
            S("Solve for $RC$", math=r"RC = \frac{50}{1.003} = 49.8\ \text{s}"),
            S("Capacitance", math=r"C = \frac{49.8}{100\times10^{3}} = 4.98\times10^{-4}\ \text{F} \approx 500\ \mu\text{F}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: leakage of a capacitor", intro=r"A $100\ \mu$F capacitor has an insulation (leakage) resistance of $10\ \text{M}\Omega$ in parallel with it. After being charged and disconnected, how long does it take the pd to fall to $37\%$ and to $1\%$?", steps=[
            S("Time constant", math=r"\tau = RC = (10\times10^{6})(100\times10^{-6}) = 1000\ \text{s} \approx 17\ \text{min}"),
            S("To 37%", text=r"This takes one time constant, about 17 minutes."),
            S("To 1%", math=r"t = \tau\ln 100 = 1000 \times 4.61 = 4.6\times10^{3}\ \text{s} \approx 77\ \text{min}"),
        ]),
        T(r"This is why a charged high-voltage capacitor can still give a shock minutes after switching off. Safe equipment includes a bleed resistor to discharge it faster."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Thinking the capacitor is empty after one time constant", r"After $\tau$ the pd is still 37% of the start. A capacitor is effectively empty only after about $5\tau$."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the time constant and the half-life", r"The time to fall to $\tfrac{1}{2}$ is $0.693\,RC$, not $RC$. The time to fall to $1/e$ is $RC$."),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong units for RC", r"Use ohms and farads to get seconds. With $\text{k}\Omega$ and $\mu$F, the product is in milliseconds: $1\ \text{k}\Omega \times 1\ \mu\text{F} = 1$ ms."),
        QUIZ(r"A capacitor discharges through a resistor with time constant $4.0$ s. By what factor has the charge fallen after $8.0$ s?",
             [r"Halved", r"Fallen to $e^{-2} = 0.135$", r"Fallen to $e^{-8} = 3.4\times10^{-4}$", r"Fallen to zero"], 1,
             r"$t/\tau = 2$, so $Q = Q_0e^{-2} = 0.135\,Q_0$.",
             hint=r"Find $t/RC$ first.",
             feedback=[r"Halving would take $0.693\tau = 2.8$ s, not twice $\tau$.", "", r"You used $t$ without dividing by $RC$. The exponent is $-t/RC = -2$.", r"An exponential decay never reaches zero exactly; it approaches zero."]),
        QUIZ(r"A graph of $\ln V$ against $t$ for a discharging capacitor has gradient $-0.25\ \text{s}^{-1}$. The resistance is $40\ \text{k}\Omega$. What is the capacitance?",
             [r"$10\ \mu$F", r"$100\ \mu$F", r"$4.0\ \mu$F", r"$1.0\ \text{mF}$"], 1,
             r"Gradient $= -1/RC$, so $RC = 4.0$ s and $C = 4.0/(40\times10^{3}) = 1.0\times10^{-4}$ F $= 100\ \mu$F.",
             hint=r"The gradient is $-1/RC$.",
             feedback=[r"Find $RC = 1/0.25 = 4.0$ s first, then divide by $R = 40\ \text{k}\Omega$; this answer is a factor of 10 too small.", "", r"$4.0$ is the value of $RC$ in seconds. You must still divide by $R$ to get $C$.", r"Check the power of ten: $4.0\ \text{s}/(40\times10^{3}\ \Omega) = 1.0\times10^{-4}$ F, not $10^{-3}$ F."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Starting from $V = IR$ and $I = -dQ/dt$, show that the charge on a discharging capacitor obeys $dQ/dt = -Q/RC$, and state its solution.",
           r"The pd across $C$ equals the pd across $R$: $\dfrac{Q}{C} = IR = -R\dfrac{dQ}{dt}$. So $\dfrac{dQ}{dt} = -\dfrac{Q}{RC}$. The solution is $Q = Q_0e^{-t/RC}$, since differentiating it gives $-Q/RC$."),
        PQ(r"A $2200\ \mu$F capacitor charged to $9.0$ V discharges through a $4.7\ \text{k}\Omega$ resistor. Calculate (a) the time constant, (b) the pd after $10$ s.",
           r"(a) $\tau = RC = (4.7\times10^{3})(2200\times10^{-6}) = 10.3$ s. (b) $V = 9.0e^{-10/10.34} = 9.0 \times 0.380 = 3.4$ V."),
        PQ(r"A capacitor discharges through a resistor. The pd halves every $6.9$ s. Find the time constant. How long does it take for the pd to fall to $10\%$?",
           r"$t_{1/2} = RC\ln 2$, so $RC = \dfrac{6.9}{0.693} = 10$ s. To fall to $10\%$: $e^{-t/10} = 0.10$, so $t = 10\ln 10 = 23$ s."),
        PQ(r"Describe how you would measure the time constant of an $RC$ circuit in the laboratory and use the results to find $RC$.",
           r"Charge the capacitor from a supply, then switch it to discharge through the resistor. Record the voltmeter reading at regular times with a stopwatch (or use a data logger). Plot $\ln V$ against $t$. The graph should be a straight line with gradient $-1/RC$, so $RC = -1/\text{gradient}$. Repeat the readings to reduce random error, and note that the voltmeter has a finite resistance that adds a parallel discharge path (use a high-resistance meter)."),
        TIP(r"The decay equations, the time constant and the $\ln V$-$t$ graph appear often in Paper 2 and in practical Paper 3 skills. Learn to turn an exponential into a straight line."),
        SUM(r"Discharge: $Q = Q_0e^{-t/RC}$, and $V$ and $I$ decay in the same way; the rate of loss of charge is proportional to the charge.",
            r"Time constant $\tau = RC$: the quantity falls to 37% in $\tau$; half-life $t_{1/2} = 0.693\,RC$; effectively empty after about $5\tau$.",
            r"$\ln V = \ln V_0 - t/RC$: a straight-line graph with gradient $-1/RC$.",
            r"Use ohms with farads for seconds, and natural logarithms for time calculations."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7. RC charging
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Charging and discharging an RC circuit",
    title="Charging a capacitor through a resistor",
    slug="phu1-rc-charging",
    minutes=23,
    summary="Derive Q = Q0(1 - e^(-t/RC)) for charging, find the pd across C and R and the current at any time, and show where the energy from the battery goes.",
    objectives=[
        "Write and use the charging equations for charge, pd across the capacitor and current",
        "Explain the shape of the charging curves using the pd across the resistor",
        "Calculate the time to reach a given pd, and the pd or current at a given time",
        "Show that half of the energy supplied by the battery is dissipated as heat, whatever R is",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Charging equation", "Time constant", "Final charge Q0 = CV0", "Energy supplied and dissipated"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A security light in a compound stays on for a short time after movement is detected, then switches off. A delay circuit in it uses a capacitor charging through a resistor. When the pd across the capacitor rises to a set value, the light turns off. The time delay is set by the resistor and capacitor values. In this lesson you will see exactly how the pd across a charging capacitor rises."),
        H("The charging equation"),
        T(r"A capacitor $C$, initially uncharged, is connected in series with a resistor $R$ to a cell of emf $V_0$ (negligible internal resistance) at time $t = 0$. At time $t$ the capacitor has charge $Q$, pd $V_C = Q/C$, and the current is $I$. The pd across the resistor is $V_R = IR$."),
        T(r"The cell's emf is shared between the two components at every instant:"),
        F(r"V_0 = IR + \frac{Q}{C}, \qquad I = \frac{dQ}{dt}"),
        T(r"At $t = 0$ the capacitor has no charge, so all of $V_0$ is across $R$ and the initial current is $I_0 = V_0/R$. As charge builds up, $V_C$ rises, $V_R$ falls, and the current falls. When the capacitor is full, $V_C = V_0$ and the current is zero. The solution of the equation (you may check by substitution) is"),
        D(r"With $Q_0 = CV_0$ the final charge:", title="Charging equations", formula=r"Q = Q_0\left(1 - e^{-t/RC}\right),\quad V_C = V_0\left(1 - e^{-t/RC}\right),\quad I = I_0e^{-t/RC}",
          note=r"The pd across the resistor is $V_R = V_0e^{-t/RC}$. Note that $V_C + V_R = V_0$ at all times. The current decays exponentially, just as in discharge."),
        T(r"**Check.** Differentiating $Q$ gives $I = \dfrac{dQ}{dt} = \dfrac{Q_0}{RC}e^{-t/RC} = \dfrac{V_0}{R}e^{-t/RC}$, which agrees. Also $IR + Q/C = V_0e^{-t/RC} + V_0(1 - e^{-t/RC}) = V_0$."),
        IMG("al-phu1-rc-curves.svg", alt="Charging curve of pd across the capacitor rising from zero towards the supply pd, reaching 63 per cent after one time constant of 10 s; beside it the discharging curve.", caption="Figure 1. The left curve is the charging pd across the capacitor. It reaches 63% of the final value in one time constant."),
        KEY("The same time constant", r"The time constant $\tau = RC$ governs charging as well as discharging.", cards=[
            [r"After $\tau$", r"$V_C = 63\%$ of $V_0$; the current is $37\%$ of $I_0$."],
            [r"After $2\tau$", r"$V_C = 86\%$ of $V_0$; the current is $14\%$ of $I_0$."],
            [r"After $5\tau$", r"$V_C = 99.3\%$ of $V_0$; the capacitor is effectively full."]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: a charging circuit", intro=r"A $500\ \mu$F capacitor is charged through a $20\ \text{k}\Omega$ resistor from a $12$ V supply. Find (a) the initial current, (b) the pd across the capacitor after $10$ s and after $20$ s, (c) the time to reach $9.0$ V.", steps=[
            S("(a) Initial current", math=r"I_0 = \frac{12}{20\times10^{3}} = 0.60\ \text{mA},\quad RC = 10\ \text{s}"),
            S("(b) After 10 s, which is one time constant", math=r"V_C = 12\left(1 - e^{-1}\right) = 12(0.632) = 7.59\ \text{V}"),
            S("(b) After 20 s", math=r"V_C = 12\left(1 - e^{-2}\right) = 12(0.865) = 10.4\ \text{V}"),
            S("(c) Solve $9.0 = 12(1 - e^{-t/10})$", math=r"e^{-t/10} = 1 - 0.75 = 0.25 \;\Rightarrow\; t = 10\ln 4 = 13.9\ \text{s}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a timer circuit", intro=r"A $6.0$ V supply charges a $470\ \mu$F capacitor through $47\ \text{k}\Omega$. A switch circuit turns on when the pd across the capacitor reaches $4.0$ V. Find the delay, and the current at that moment.", steps=[
            S("Time constant", math=r"RC = (47\times10^{3})(470\times10^{-6}) = 22.1\ \text{s}"),
            S("Set $4.0 = 6.0(1 - e^{-t/RC})$", math=r"e^{-t/RC} = 1 - \tfrac{4.0}{6.0} = \tfrac{1}{3} \;\Rightarrow\; t = RC\ln 3 = 22.1 \times 1.0986 = 24.3\ \text{s}"),
            S("Current: pd across $R$ is $6.0 - 4.0 = 2.0$ V", math=r"I = \frac{2.0}{47\times10^{3}} = 4.3\times10^{-5}\ \text{A} = 43\ \mu\text{A}"),
        ]),
        TIP(r"For a charging problem, it often helps to work with $V_R = V_0 - V_C$: the resistor's pd and the current follow $e^{-t/RC}$, so $V_R$ is just like the discharging case."),
        H("Energy in the charging circuit"),
        T(r"The cell moves a total charge $Q_0 = CV_0$ through its emf $V_0$, so it supplies energy $Q_0V_0 = CV_0^2$. The capacitor stores only $\tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2$. So exactly **half** of the energy from the cell is dissipated as heat in the resistor."),
        F(r"E_{\text{supplied}} = CV_0^2,\qquad E_{\text{stored}} = \tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2,\qquad E_{\text{heat}} = \tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2"),
        T(r"This is true for **any** value of $R$. A larger resistance makes charging slower but the same energy is lost as heat in total. (You can confirm by adding up $I^2R\,\Delta t$ over the whole charging time: $\int_0^\infty I_0^2e^{-2t/RC}R\,dt = \tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2$.)"),
        EX("Worked example 3: energy balance", intro=r"A $500\ \mu$F capacitor is charged fully from a $12$ V supply through a resistor. Find the energy supplied by the supply, the energy stored, and the energy dissipated.", steps=[
            S("Final charge", math=r"Q_0 = CV_0 = (500\times10^{-6})(12) = 6.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{C}"),
            S("Supplied", math=r"E = Q_0V_0 = (6.0\times10^{-3})(12) = 0.072\ \text{J}"),
            S("Stored", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(500\times10^{-6})(144) = 0.036\ \text{J}"),
            S("Dissipated in the resistor", math=r"0.072 - 0.036 = 0.036\ \text{J}"),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the discharge formula for charging", r"In discharge the pd falls as $V_0e^{-t/RC}$. In charging, the pd across the capacitor rises as $V_0(1 - e^{-t/RC})$; it is the pd across the resistor, and the current, that follow $e^{-t/RC}$."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking the capacitor charges to the supply pd in time RC", r"After $RC$ the capacitor has only 63% of the final pd. It needs about $5RC$ to be 99% charged."),
        MISTAKE("Saying the resistor 'stores' half of the energy", r"A resistor does not store energy; it converts it to heat. Half of the supplied energy leaves the circuit as heat."),
        QUIZ(r"An uncharged capacitor is connected to a $10$ V supply through a resistor. After a time equal to one time constant, what is the pd across the resistor?",
             [r"$3.7$ V", r"$6.3$ V", r"$10$ V", r"$0$ V"], 0,
             r"$V_C = 10(1 - e^{-1}) = 6.3$ V, so $V_R = 10 - 6.3 = 3.7$ V.",
             hint=r"$V_R = V_0 - V_C$.",
             feedback=["", r"This is the pd across the capacitor, not the resistor.", r"This is the pd across the resistor at $t = 0$.", r"The pd across the resistor is zero only when charging is complete."]),
        QUIZ(r"The resistance in a charging $RC$ circuit is doubled, with the same supply and capacitor. What happens to the total energy dissipated as heat in the resistor?",
             [r"It doubles", r"It halves", r"It is unchanged", r"It quadruples"], 2,
             r"The heat is always $\tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2$, independent of $R$. Charging is slower, but the same total energy is converted to heat.",
             hint=r"Energy supplied minus energy stored.",
             feedback=[r"A larger $R$ gives a smaller current, so the power is lower; the total heat does not depend on $R$.", r"The energy supplied and the energy stored do not depend on $R$.", "", r"No quadratic dependence on $R$ appears in the energy balance."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A capacitor is charged through a resistor from a cell. Explain why the charging current falls with time, using the pds across the capacitor and the resistor.",
           r"The cell's emf is shared: $V_0 = V_C + V_R$. At the start, $V_C = 0$, so $V_R = V_0$ and the current is largest. As charge accumulates, $V_C$ rises, so $V_R = V_0 - V_C$ falls. The current is $I = V_R/R$, so it falls. When $V_C = V_0$ the current is zero."),
        PQ(r"A $100\ \mu$F capacitor is charged from a $9.0$ V supply through a $25\ \text{k}\Omega$ resistor. Calculate the pd across the capacitor after $2.5$ s, and the time taken to reach $6.0$ V.",
           r"$RC = (25\times10^{3})(100\times10^{-6}) = 2.5$ s. After $2.5$ s: $V_C = 9.0(1 - e^{-1}) = 5.7$ V. To reach $6.0$ V: $6.0 = 9.0(1 - e^{-t/2.5})$, so $e^{-t/2.5} = \tfrac{1}{3}$, $t = 2.5\ln 3 = 2.7$ s."),
        PQ(r"Show that when a capacitor $C$ is charged fully through a resistor from a supply of emf $V_0$, the energy dissipated in the resistor is $\tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2$.",
           r"The charge moved through the supply is $Q_0 = CV_0$, so the energy supplied is $Q_0V_0 = CV_0^2$. The energy stored in the capacitor is $\tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2$. By conservation of energy, the energy dissipated is $CV_0^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}CV_0^2$."),
        PQ(r"Sketch, on the same axes, how the charging current and the pd across the capacitor vary with time for an $RC$ circuit, and mark the time constant on the time axis.",
           r"The current starts at $I_0 = V_0/R$ and decays exponentially towards zero, falling to $0.37I_0$ at $t = RC$. The pd across the capacitor starts at zero and rises with decreasing gradient towards $V_0$, reaching $0.63V_0$ at $t = RC$. The gradient of the pd graph at any time is proportional to the current at that time."),
        TIP(r"The charging equation is tested in Paper 2 calculations and in practical Paper 3 work (measuring the pd across a capacitor over time). Keep $V_C$, $V_R$ and $I$ separate in your working."),
        SUM(r"Charging: $V_0 = IR + Q/C$; $Q = Q_0(1 - e^{-t/RC})$, $V_C = V_0(1 - e^{-t/RC})$, $I = I_0e^{-t/RC}$.",
            r"After $RC$ the capacitor is at 63% of its final pd; after $5RC$ it is effectively full.",
            r"The current and the pd across the resistor decay; $V_C + V_R = V_0$ throughout.",
            r"The supply provides $CV_0^2$; half is stored and half is dissipated as heat, whatever $R$ is."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8. Force on moving charges
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Force on moving charges and the Hall effect",
    title="The magnetic force on moving charges and currents",
    slug="phu1-force-on-moving-charges",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use F = Bqv sin(theta) and F = BIL sin(theta), find the direction with the left-hand rule, and analyse circular motion, velocity selectors and helical paths in a magnetic field.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate the magnetic force on a moving charge and on a current-carrying wire",
        "Find the direction of the force with Fleming's left-hand rule, allowing for the sign of the charge",
        "Derive r = mv/(qB) and the period of circular motion, and explain why the period does not depend on speed",
        "Apply the results to a velocity selector and to a charged particle entering a field at an angle",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Magnetic flux density B", "Force on a moving charge", "Force on a current", "Circular motion in a magnetic field", "Velocity selector"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"In an old cathode-ray TV, a beam of electrons was bent by coils to paint the picture on the screen. In a hospital, protons are steered round a ring by magnets to make medical isotopes. In both cases a **magnetic field** pushes on **moving charges**. The magnetic force never speeds a charge up or slows it down; it only turns it. This lesson shows how to calculate that force and the path it produces."),
        H("The force on a moving charge"),
        D(r"A charge $q$ moving with velocity $v$ in a magnetic field of flux density $B$ experiences a force of magnitude", title="Magnetic force on a moving charge",
          formula=r"F = Bqv\sin\theta",
          note=r"$\theta$ is the angle between the velocity and the field. The force is zero when $\theta = 0$ (moving along the field) and greatest, $F = Bqv$, when $\theta = 90^\circ$."),
        T(r"The unit of $B$ is the **tesla** (T): one tesla is the flux density that gives a force of 1 N on a charge of 1 C moving at 1 m/s at right angles to the field. Typical values: Earth's field about $5\times10^{-5}$ T; a small bar magnet about $10^{-2}$ T; a hospital MRI scanner several tesla."),
        T(r"The force is **perpendicular to both** the velocity and the field. Use **Fleming's left-hand rule**: first finger along the **F**ield, second finger along the **c**urrent (the direction a **positive** charge moves), thumb gives the force. For a **negative** charge such as an electron, the conventional current points opposite to its velocity, so the force is reversed."),
        T(r"Because the force is always at right angles to the velocity, it does no work and the particle's speed stays constant. Only its direction changes."),
        H("The force on a current-carrying wire"),
        T(r"A current $I$ in a wire of length $L$ is a stream of moving charges. If $N$ charges pass in time $t$, $Q = It$ and each travels at speed $v$ so $L = vt$: the total force on the charges in the length $L$ is $F = BQv\sin\theta = BIL\sin\theta$."),
        D(r"A straight wire of length $L$ carrying current $I$ at angle $\theta$ to a field $B$ feels a force", title="Force on a current", formula=r"F = BIL\sin\theta"),
        EX("Worked example 1: a wire in a field", intro=r"A $0.40$ m length of wire carries $5.0$ A at right angles to a uniform field of $0.30$ T. What is the force? What is it when the wire is turned to $30^\circ$ to the field?", steps=[
            S("At 90 degrees", math=r"F = BIL =(0.30)(5.0)(0.40) = 0.60\ \text{N}"),
            S("At 30 degrees", math=r"F = BIL\sin 30^{\circ} = 0.60 \times 0.50 = 0.30\ \text{N}"),
        ]),
        H("Circular motion in a magnetic field"),
        IMG("al-phu1-force-circular.svg", alt="A positive charge moving to the right at the bottom of a circle, with the field into the page shown as crosses and an arrow for the force pointing up towards the centre.", caption="Figure 1. A positive charge in a field into the page. The force F points to the centre at every point, so the path is a circle."),
        T(r"If the charge enters the field at right angles ($\theta = 90^\circ$), the force $Bqv$ is constant in size and always points to the centre. It supplies the centripetal force:"),
        F(r"Bqv = \frac{mv^2}{r} \;\Rightarrow\; r = \frac{mv}{qB}"),
        T(r"A faster or heavier particle makes a bigger circle; a stronger field or larger charge makes a smaller one. The time for one circuit is"),
        F(r"T = \frac{2\pi r}{v} = \frac{2\pi m}{qB}, \qquad f = \frac{qB}{2\pi m}"),
        T(r"The period does **not** depend on the speed. A faster particle goes on a larger circle but travels it just as fast in proportion. This is the idea behind the cyclotron, a particle accelerator in which charged particles spiral outwards while a radio-frequency voltage of fixed frequency $f$ accelerates them each half-turn."),
        EX("Worked example 2: electron in a magnetic field", intro=r"An electron ($m = 9.11\times10^{-31}$ kg, $e = 1.60\times10^{-19}$ C) moves at $2.0\times10^{7}$ m/s at right angles to a field of $0.50$ mT. Find the force, the radius of its path and the period.", steps=[
            S("Force", math=r"F = Bev = (5.0\times10^{-4})(1.60\times10^{-19})(2.0\times10^{7}) = 1.6\times10^{-15}\ \text{N}"),
            S("Radius", math=r"r = \frac{mv}{eB} = \frac{(9.11\times10^{-31})(2.0\times10^{7})}{(1.60\times10^{-19})(5.0\times10^{-4})} = 0.23\ \text{m}"),
            S("Period", math=r"T = \frac{2\pi m}{eB} = \frac{2\pi(9.11\times10^{-31})}{(1.60\times10^{-19})(5.0\times10^{-4})} = 7.2\times10^{-8}\ \text{s}"),
        ], check=r"\frac{2\pi r}{v} = \frac{2\pi(0.2278)}{2.0\times10^{7}} = 7.2\times10^{-8}\ \text{s}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a proton accelerated and then bent", intro=r"A proton ($m = 1.67\times10^{-27}$ kg) is accelerated from rest through a pd of $1.0$ kV and enters a field of $0.10$ T at right angles. Find its speed and the radius of its path.", steps=[
            S("Energy gained from the pd", math=r"eV = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 \;\Rightarrow\; v = \sqrt{\frac{2eV}{m}} = \sqrt{\frac{2(1.60\times10^{-19})(1000)}{1.67\times10^{-27}}} = 4.4\times10^{5}\ \text{m/s}"),
            S("Radius", math=r"r = \frac{mv}{eB} = \frac{(1.67\times10^{-27})(4.38\times10^{5})}{(1.60\times10^{-19})(0.10)} = 4.6\times10^{-2}\ \text{m}"),
        ]),
        H("A velocity selector"),
        T(r"Suppose a beam of charged particles passes through a region with an electric field $E$ and a magnetic field $B$ at right angles to each other and to the beam. The electric force is $qE$ in one direction and the magnetic force is $Bqv$ in the opposite direction. Particles with speed $v$ such that the forces balance go straight through:"),
        F(r"qE = Bqv \;\Rightarrow\; v = \frac{E}{B}"),
        T(r"The charge and mass cancel, so the selector lets through only particles of a single speed. For $E = 2.0\times10^{4}$ V/m and $B = 0.050$ T, $v = 4.0\times10^{5}$ m/s. Mass spectrometers use a selector followed by a region of field $B$ where $r = mv/qB$ separates different masses."),
        H("Entering at an angle: the helix"),
        T(r"If the velocity makes an angle $\theta$ to the field, split it into a component $v\cos\theta$ along $B$ (no force) and a component $v\sin\theta$ at right angles (circular motion). The result is a **helix** (spiral): radius $r = \dfrac{mv\sin\theta}{qB}$ and a **pitch** (distance advanced per turn) of $v\cos\theta \times T$. For example, an electron at $4.0\times10^{6}$ m/s at $60^\circ$ to a field of $2.0$ mT has $r = 9.9$ mm and a pitch of $3.6$ cm."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Getting the direction wrong for negative charges", r"Fleming's left-hand rule uses conventional current. For an electron, the current direction is opposite to its velocity, so the force is reversed compared with a proton moving the same way."),
        MISTAKE("Saying the magnetic force does work", r"The force is perpendicular to the velocity, so no work is done and the speed is constant. The kinetic energy is unchanged."),
        MISTAKE("Using $B$ in millitesla or microtesla without converting", r"Write $0.50$ mT as $5.0\times10^{-4}$ T before using the formulae."),
        QUIZ(r"A proton and an alpha particle (charge $2e$, mass about $4m_p$) enter a magnetic field at right angles with the same speed. What is the ratio of the radius of the alpha path to that of the proton path?",
             [r"1", r"2", r"4", r"$\tfrac{1}{2}$"], 1,
             r"$r = mv/qB$. For the alpha particle $m$ is 4 times as great and $q$ is 2 times, so $r$ is $4/2 = 2$ times as great.",
             hint=r"$r \propto m/q$ at the same speed.",
             feedback=[r"The mass-to-charge ratios are not equal.", "", r"You used mass only. The charge is also double.", r"This inverts the ratio; a heavier particle has a larger radius."]),
        QUIZ(r"A charged particle moves along the direction of a uniform magnetic field. What happens to its path?",
             [r"It moves in a circle", r"It moves in a straight line at constant speed", r"It speeds up along the field", r"It moves in a helix"], 1,
             r"With $\theta = 0$, $F = Bqv\sin 0^\circ = 0$. There is no force, so it continues in a straight line.",
             hint=r"What is $\sin 0^\circ$?",
             feedback=[r"A circle needs the velocity to be at $90^\circ$ to the field.", "", r"A magnetic force never changes the speed.", r"A helix needs a component of velocity across the field."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Derive an expression for the radius of the circular path of a particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ moving at speed $v$ at right angles to a magnetic field $B$.",
           r"The magnetic force $Bqv$ is perpendicular to the velocity and provides the centripetal force: $Bqv = \dfrac{mv^2}{r}$. Hence $r = \dfrac{mv}{qB}$."),
        PQ(r"An electron is accelerated from rest through $2.0$ kV and enters a uniform field of $1.0$ mT at right angles. Find its speed and the radius of its path. ($m = 9.11\times10^{-31}$ kg, $e = 1.60\times10^{-19}$ C.)",
           r"$\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 = eV$, so $v = \sqrt{\dfrac{2(1.60\times10^{-19})(2000)}{9.11\times10^{-31}}} = 2.65\times10^{7}$ m/s. $r = \dfrac{mv}{eB} = \dfrac{(9.11\times10^{-31})(2.65\times10^{7})}{(1.60\times10^{-19})(1.0\times10^{-3})} = 0.15$ m."),
        PQ(r"Explain why the period of a charged particle moving in a circle in a uniform magnetic field does not depend on its speed, and find the frequency for a proton in a $1.5$ T field.",
           r"$T = \dfrac{2\pi r}{v} = \dfrac{2\pi}{v}\cdot\dfrac{mv}{qB} = \dfrac{2\pi m}{qB}$: the speed cancels. A faster particle has a larger radius in proportion. $f = \dfrac{qB}{2\pi m} = \dfrac{(1.60\times10^{-19})(1.5)}{2\pi(1.67\times10^{-27})} = 2.3\times10^{7}$ Hz (23 MHz)."),
        PQ(r"Ions of different speeds pass through crossed fields $E = 5.0\times10^{3}$ V/m and $B = 0.020$ T. Find the speed of the ions that pass undeflected, and say what happens to faster ions.",
           r"$v = E/B = 5.0\times10^{3}/0.020 = 2.5\times10^{5}$ m/s. For faster ions the magnetic force $Bqv$ exceeds the electric force $qE$, so they are deflected in the direction of the magnetic force."),
        TIP(r"Circular motion of charges in fields, the velocity selector and $F = BIL$ are standard in Paper 1 and Paper 2. In Paper 2, draw the direction of the force on a diagram before calculating."),
        SUM(r"$F = Bqv\sin\theta$ on a moving charge and $F = BIL\sin\theta$ on a current; direction from the left-hand rule (reverse for negative charges).",
            r"The force is perpendicular to the velocity: no work, constant speed.",
            r"At $90^\circ$: circular path with $r = mv/qB$ and $T = 2\pi m/qB$, independent of speed.",
            r"Crossed $E$ and $B$ fields select speed $v = E/B$; at other angles the path is a helix."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9. The Hall effect
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Force on moving charges and the Hall effect",
    title="The Hall effect and the Hall probe",
    slug="phu1-hall-effect",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Explain the Hall pd across a current-carrying slab in a magnetic field, derive V_H = BI/(nte), and use it to find carrier density, the sign of carriers and the flux density.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain how the Hall pd arises from the magnetic force on the charge carriers",
        "Derive V_H = BI/(nte) and use it in calculations",
        "Use the polarity of the Hall pd to identify the sign of the charge carriers",
        "Explain why semiconductors give much larger Hall pds than metals and how a Hall probe measures B",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Hall effect", "Hall pd", "Charge carrier density n", "Hall probe", "Metals and semiconductors"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"How do you measure a magnetic field with a small electronic device? Phones use a tiny sensor to detect the field of a magnet in the cover; vehicles use one to count wheel turns; a laboratory uses a **Hall probe** to measure $B$. All depend on one effect discovered by Edwin Hall in 1879: a magnetic field across a current-carrying conductor produces a small pd at right angles to both."),
        H("How the Hall pd arises"),
        T(r"Consider a flat slab of conductor carrying a current $I$ along its length. It has width $w$ and thickness $t$, and the magnetic field $B$ is at right angles to the large face, along the thickness. The charge carriers (electrons in a metal) drift with speed $v$. The magnetic force $Bqv$ pushes them towards one edge of the slab."),
        IMG("al-phu1-hall-effect.svg", alt="A rectangular slab with current to the right, electrons drifting left, magnetic field out of the page shown as dots, negative charge collecting along the bottom edge and positive along the top edge, with a voltmeter across the width.", caption="Figure 1. Hall effect in a metal slab. The magnetic force pushes electrons towards the bottom edge, which becomes negative; the top edge is left positive."),
        T(r"Charge builds up on the two edges. This produces a transverse electric field $E_H$ across the width, which pushes on the carriers in the opposite direction to the magnetic force. The build-up stops when the two forces balance, and the carriers then travel straight along the slab again. The pd between the edges is the **Hall pd** $V_H = E_Hw$."),
        D(r"The Hall pd appears across the width of a conductor carrying a current in a magnetic field at right angles to the current. It is produced by the magnetic force on the moving charge carriers and is balanced by the electric force from the charge that builds up on the edges.", title="Hall effect"),
        H("Deriving the Hall pd"),
        T(r"At equilibrium the electric force equals the magnetic force on a carrier of charge $e$ moving at drift speed $v$:"),
        F(r"eE_H = Bev \;\Rightarrow\; E_H = Bv"),
        T(r"The current in the slab is $I = nAve$, where $n$ is the number of charge carriers per unit volume and $A = wt$ is the cross-sectional area. Hence $v = \dfrac{I}{nwte}$. The Hall pd is"),
        F(r"V_H = E_Hw = Bvw = B\cdot\frac{I}{nwte}\cdot w = \frac{BI}{nte}"),
        D(r"For carriers of charge magnitude $e$ and number density $n$, in a slab of thickness $t$ (measured along the field):", title="Hall pd", formula=r"V_H = \frac{BI}{nte}",
          note=r"For a given slab and current, $V_H$ is directly proportional to $B$. The width $w$ does not appear."),
        TIP(r"$t$ is the thickness along the direction of the field. Take care: it is not the width across which you measure $V_H$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a copper strip", intro=r"A copper strip of thickness $0.10$ mm carries $10$ A in a field of $0.50$ T. For copper $n = 8.5\times10^{28}\ \text{m}^{-3}$. Find the Hall pd.", steps=[
            S("Formula", math=r"V_H = \frac{BI}{nte}"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"V_H = \frac{(0.50)(10)}{(8.5\times10^{28})(1.0\times10^{-4})(1.60\times10^{-19})}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"V_H = 3.7\times10^{-6}\ \text{V} = 3.7\ \mu\text{V}"),
        ]),
        T(r"A few microvolts is very small and hard to measure. The reason is that metals have an enormous number of free electrons, so each moves slowly, and the magnetic force is small."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a semiconductor Hall probe", intro=r"A semiconductor slab of thickness $0.50$ mm has $n = 5.0\times10^{21}\ \text{m}^{-3}$. It carries $1.0$ mA in a field of $0.10$ T. Find the Hall pd.", steps=[
            S("Substitute", math=r"V_H = \frac{(0.10)(1.0\times10^{-3})}{(5.0\times10^{21})(5.0\times10^{-4})(1.60\times10^{-19})}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"V_H = 2.5\times10^{-4}\ \text{V} = 0.25\ \text{mV}"),
        ]),
        T(r"The pd is about 70 times larger even with a current 10 000 times smaller, because $n$ is about $10^{7}$ times smaller. This is why Hall probes are made from semiconductors."),
        H("The Hall probe"),
        T(r"A **Hall probe** is a thin slice of semiconductor with a fixed current $I$ passed along it. Since $V_H = \dfrac{BI}{nte}$, a voltmeter reading $V_H$ is proportional to the component of $B$ at right angles to the slice. The probe is calibrated in a known field, then used to map fields. Turning the probe changes the reading, and it is zero when the field lies in the plane of the slice. The probe must be held with its face at right angles to the field to read the full value."),
        H("What the sign of the Hall pd tells you"),
        T(r"In the slab of Figure 1, electrons move left and are pushed to the bottom edge. A **positive** charge carrier, a hole, in a p-type semiconductor, moves right (the same direction as the conventional current) and is pushed to the **same** edge by the magnetic force, because both the velocity direction and the charge sign are reversed. So holes make the bottom edge positive, and electrons make it negative. The polarity of $V_H$ therefore shows the **sign of the charge carriers**. Hall measurements gave the first evidence that some materials conduct by positive carriers."),
        EX("Worked example 3: finding the carrier density", intro=r"A metal strip of thickness $0.20$ mm carries $5.0$ A in a field of $0.40$ T. The Hall pd is $1.04\ \mu$V. Calculate $n$.", steps=[
            S("Rearrange", math=r"n = \frac{BI}{V_Hte}"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"n = \frac{(0.40)(5.0)}{(1.04\times10^{-6})(2.0\times10^{-4})(1.60\times10^{-19})}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"n = 6.0\times10^{28}\ \text{m}^{-3}"),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the width instead of the thickness", r"In $V_H = BI/nte$ the length $t$ is the dimension of the slab along $B$. The width across which $V_H$ is measured cancels."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that the Hall pd needs a transverse field", r"There is no Hall pd if the field is parallel to the current, because there is no force on the carriers. The field must have a component at right angles to the current and to the Hall pd direction."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking a larger $n$ gives a larger Hall pd", r"$V_H \propto 1/n$. Metals have large $n$ and give tiny Hall pds; semiconductors have small $n$ and give large ones."),
        QUIZ(r"Why is a semiconductor, rather than a metal, used for a Hall probe?",
             [r"It has more free electrons per unit volume", r"It has fewer free charge carriers per unit volume, so the Hall pd is larger", r"It is a better conductor", r"It is not affected by temperature"], 1,
             r"$V_H = BI/nte$ is inversely proportional to $n$. A semiconductor has a much smaller $n$, so the pd is much larger and easier to measure.",
             hint=r"Which quantity appears in the denominator of $V_H = BI/nte$?",
             feedback=[r"More electrons would give a smaller Hall pd.", "", r"A metal conducts better, but conduction is not what makes the Hall pd large.", r"The Hall voltage of semiconductors varies with temperature, so the probe must be calibrated."]),
        QUIZ(r"The current through a Hall probe is doubled and the flux density is halved. What happens to the Hall pd?",
             [r"It doubles", r"It halves", r"It is unchanged", r"It quadruples"], 2,
             r"$V_H \propto BI$. Doubling $I$ and halving $B$ leaves the product, and $V_H$, unchanged.",
             hint=r"$V_H$ is proportional to the product $BI$.",
             feedback=[r"You applied only the change in current.", r"You applied only the change in field.", "", r"There is no squared term in $V_H = BI/nte$."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Explain how the Hall pd arises across a slab carrying a current in a magnetic field, and why it reaches a steady value.",
           r"The magnetic force $Bqv$ on the moving charge carriers pushes them towards one edge of the slab, so that edge gains charge of one sign and the opposite edge is left with the opposite sign. This sets up a transverse electric field and a pd. The electric field exerts a force on each carrier opposite to the magnetic force. Charge builds up until the electric force equals the magnetic force, when carriers are no longer deflected and the Hall pd stays steady."),
        PQ(r"Derive $V_H = BI/nte$ for a slab of thickness $t$ and width $w$.",
           r"At equilibrium $eE_H = Bev$, so $E_H = Bv$. $V_H = E_Hw = Bvw$. With $I = nAve = nwtve$, $v = \dfrac{I}{nwte}$. Hence $V_H = \dfrac{BIw}{nwte} = \dfrac{BI}{nte}$."),
        PQ(r"A Hall probe of thickness $0.20$ mm has $n = 1.0\times10^{22}\ \text{m}^{-3}$ and carries a current of $2.0$ mA. When placed in a magnetic field the Hall pd is $0.50$ mV. Calculate the flux density.",
           r"$B = \dfrac{V_Hnte}{I} = \dfrac{(5.0\times10^{-4})(1.0\times10^{22})(2.0\times10^{-4})(1.60\times10^{-19})}{2.0\times10^{-3}} = 0.080$ T."),
        PQ(r"In a Hall experiment, reversing the direction of $B$ reverses the polarity of $V_H$. Explain this, and say what the polarity can tell you about the material.",
           r"The magnetic force is reversed when $B$ is reversed, so the carriers are pushed to the opposite edge. Since $V_H$'s sign depends on which edge collects which sign of charge, it reverses. For a fixed current and field direction, the polarity depends on the sign of the charge carriers (negative electrons or positive holes), so it can identify whether the material is n-type or p-type."),
        TIP(r"The derivation of $V_H = BI/nte$ is a classic Paper 2 question. In Paper 3 you may use a Hall probe to measure $B$ near a coil or magnet."),
        SUM(r"The magnetic force deflects the moving charge carriers to one edge of the slab, creating a transverse Hall pd.",
            r"At equilibrium $eE_H = Bev$, giving $V_H = BI/nte$ with $t$ the thickness along $B$.",
            r"$V_H \propto 1/n$: semiconductors give large Hall pds, metals very small ones; $V_H \propto B$ for a fixed current, so a Hall probe measures $B$.",
            r"The polarity of $V_H$ shows the sign of the charge carriers."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 10. Biot-Savart
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Magnetic fields of currents",
    title="The Biot-Savart law: fields of straight wires and loops",
    slug="phu1-biot-savart",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use the Biot-Savart law dB = (mu0/4pi) I dl sin(theta)/r^2 to derive the field of a long straight wire and of a circular coil, and add fields by superposition.",
    objectives=[
        "State the Biot-Savart law and use the right-hand grip rule for direction",
        "Derive B = mu0 I / 2 pi r for a long straight wire",
        "Derive and use the field at the centre and on the axis of a circular coil",
        "Add the fields of several currents as vectors",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Biot-Savart law", "Permeability of free space", "Field of a straight wire", "Field of a circular coil", "Superposition of fields"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"In 1820 a Danish teacher, Hans Christian Oersted, noticed a compass needle swing when a wire nearby carried a current. This showed for the first time that **currents produce magnetic fields**. Today you use the same fact whenever you switch on an electric bell, a loudspeaker or a relay. To design them, we need to know the size of the field. The **Biot-Savart law** gives it, point by point, for any shape of wire."),
        H("The Biot-Savart law"),
        T(r"Divide the wire into tiny lengths $dl$. Each carries current $I$ and contributes a small field $dB$ at a point $P$ that is at distance $r$ from the element:"),
        D(r"A small length $dl$ of wire carrying current $I$ produces at a point a distance $r$ away a flux density", title="Biot-Savart law", formula=r"dB = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\,\frac{I\,dl\,\sin\theta}{r^2}",
          note=r"$\theta$ is the angle between the direction of the current element and the line joining it to the point. $\mu_0 = 4\pi\times10^{-7}\ \text{T m A}^{-1}$ is the **permeability of free space**. The total field is the vector sum of all the $dB$ contributions."),
        T(r"The field is inversely proportional to $r^2$, like gravity and the electric field of a point charge. It is zero along the line of the current element ($\theta = 0$) and greatest at right angles to it."),
        T(r"**Direction.** The right-hand grip rule: point the thumb of your right hand along the (conventional) current; the fingers curl in the direction of the field lines. For a long straight wire the field lines are circles around the wire, in planes at right angles to it."),
        H("The long straight wire"),
        T(r"Take a very long wire and a point $P$ at perpendicular distance $d$ from it. For an element at angle $\theta$ the distance to $P$ is $r = d/\sin\theta$, and the position along the wire is $l = -d\cot\theta$, so $dl = \dfrac{d\,d\theta}{\sin^2\theta}$. Every element contributes in the same direction, so the magnitudes add:"),
        F(r"B = \frac{\mu_0 I}{4\pi}\int_0^{\pi}\frac{\sin\theta}{r^2}\,dl = \frac{\mu_0 I}{4\pi}\int_0^{\pi}\frac{\sin^3\theta}{d^2}\cdot\frac{d}{\sin^2\theta}\,d\theta = \frac{\mu_0 I}{4\pi d}\int_0^{\pi}\sin\theta\,d\theta"),
        T(r"The integral is $\int_0^\pi\sin\theta\,d\theta = 2$. Hence"),
        D(r"For a long straight wire carrying current $I$, the flux density at perpendicular distance $r$ is", title="Field of a long straight wire", formula=r"B = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi r}",
          note=r"$B \propto 1/r$ (see Figure 1). The factor $\mu_0/2\pi = 2\times10^{-7}\ \text{T m A}^{-1}$ is worth remembering."),
        IMG("al-phu1-wire-field.svg", alt="Concentric anticlockwise field circles around a wire with current out of the page, and a graph of flux density against distance from the wire falling as 1 over r from about 50 microtesla near the wire to 5 microtesla at 0.2 m.", caption="Figure 1. The field of a straight wire carrying 5.0 A: circles around the wire, and a graph of B against r."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a wire and the Earth's field", intro=r"A long straight wire carries $5.0$ A. Find the flux density at $0.10$ m from it. Compare it with Earth's field, which is of order $5\times10^{-5}$ T. (Earth's field varies with place: check your textbook.)", steps=[
            S("Formula", math=r"B = \frac{\mu_0I}{2\pi r} = \frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(5.0)}{2\pi(0.10)}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"B = 1.0\times10^{-5}\ \text{T} = 10\ \mu\text{T}"),
            S("Compare", text="This is about a fifth of the Earth's field, so a compass near the wire is noticeably deflected."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: two wires", intro=r"Two long parallel wires are $0.20$ m apart and carry $5.0$ A in **opposite** directions. Find the flux density at the midpoint between them.", steps=[
            S("Each wire is $0.10$ m from the midpoint", math=r"B_1 = B_2 = \frac{\mu_0I}{2\pi r} = 1.0\times10^{-5}\ \text{T}"),
            S("Directions: for opposite currents the two fields point the **same way** between the wires, so they add", math=r"B = 1.0\times10^{-5} + 1.0\times10^{-5} = 2.0\times10^{-5}\ \text{T} = 20\ \mu\text{T}"),
            S("If the currents were in the **same** direction", text="the fields would be opposite at the midpoint and the resultant would be zero."),
        ]),
        H("A circular coil"),
        T(r"Take a circular loop of radius $R$ carrying current $I$. Every element $dl$ is at right angles to the line to the centre ($\theta = 90^\circ$) and at distance $R$ from it, and all the fields at the centre point along the axis. Adding up all the elements, $\sum dl = 2\pi R$:"),
        F(r"B_{\text{centre}} = \frac{\mu_0 I}{4\pi}\cdot\frac{2\pi R}{R^2} = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2R}"),
        T(r"For a coil of $N$ close turns, multiply by $N$: $B = \dfrac{\mu_0NI}{2R}$. The field direction along the axis follows from the grip rule: the fingers follow the current round the loop and the thumb points along the field."),
        T(r"**On the axis**, at a distance $x$ from the centre, each element is at distance $r = \sqrt{R^2 + x^2}$ from the point. The components perpendicular to the axis cancel in pairs; the axial component of each is $dB\sin\varphi$ where $\sin\varphi = R/r$. Adding up around the loop:"),
        F(r"B_{\text{axis}} = \frac{\mu_0 I}{4\pi}\cdot\frac{2\pi R}{r^2}\cdot\frac{R}{r} = \frac{\mu_0 I R^2}{2\left(R^2 + x^2\right)^{3/2}}"),
        T(r"For $x = 0$ this gives $\mu_0I/2R$. For a coil of $N$ turns multiply by $N$. The field falls rapidly along the axis."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a flat coil", intro=r"A flat circular coil has $50$ turns of radius $0.10$ m and carries $2.0$ A. Find the flux density at its centre and on the axis at a distance equal to the radius from the centre.", steps=[
            S("Centre", math=r"B = \frac{\mu_0NI}{2R} = \frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(50)(2.0)}{2(0.10)} = 6.3\times10^{-4}\ \text{T}"),
            S("On the axis at a distance $x = R$, the factor $(R^2 + x^2)^{3/2}$ becomes $2^{3/2}R^3$", math=r"B = \frac{B_{\text{centre}}}{2^{3/2}} = \frac{6.28\times10^{-4}}{2.83} = 2.2\times10^{-4}\ \text{T}"),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the number of turns", r"A coil of $N$ turns has $N$ times the field of one loop. Include $N$ in $B = \mu_0NI/2R$."),
        MISTAKE("Adding fields as numbers", r"Fields from different wires are vectors. Use the grip rule to find the direction of each, then add or subtract."),
        MISTAKE("Using the straight-wire formula inside or very near the end of a short wire", r"$B = \mu_0I/2\pi r$ is for a long wire, where the distance to the point is small compared with the wire length."),
        QUIZ(r"The distance from a long straight wire is tripled. By what factor does the flux density change?",
             [r"It is multiplied by 3", r"It is multiplied by $\tfrac{1}{3}$", r"It is multiplied by $\tfrac{1}{9}$", r"It is multiplied by 9"], 1,
             r"For a long straight wire, $B \propto 1/r$ (not $1/r^2$), so tripling $r$ divides $B$ by 3.",
             hint=r"Which power of $r$ appears in $B = \mu_0I/2\pi r$?",
             feedback=[r"The field falls with distance.", "", r"That is for an inverse-square law, such as a point source. For a long wire it is $1/r$.", r"The field gets weaker, not stronger, as you move away."]),
        QUIZ(r"A circular coil has $N$ turns of radius $R$ carrying current $I$. The radius is doubled and the current is doubled. What happens to the flux density at the centre?",
             [r"It is doubled", r"It is unchanged", r"It is halved", r"It is quadrupled"], 1,
             r"$B = \mu_0NI/2R$. Doubling $I$ doubles $B$; doubling $R$ halves $B$. The two changes cancel.",
             hint=r"Look at $I/R$.",
             feedback=[r"You applied only the change in current.", "", r"You applied only the change in radius.", r"Neither change enters as a square."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"State the Biot-Savart law, and use it to show that the magnetic flux density at the centre of a circular coil of $N$ turns, radius $R$ and current $I$ is $\mu_0NI/2R$.",
           r"The field $dB$ at a point distance $r$ from a current element $I\,dl$ is $dB = \dfrac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\dfrac{I\,dl\sin\theta}{r^2}$. At the centre of a loop each element is at $r = R$ and $\theta = 90^\circ$, and all $dB$ point along the axis. Summing $dl$ round the circumference ($2\pi R$): $B = \dfrac{\mu_0 I}{4\pi}\cdot\dfrac{2\pi R}{R^2} = \dfrac{\mu_0 I}{2R}$ for one turn; for $N$ turns multiply by $N$."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the flux density $5.0$ cm from a long straight wire carrying $12$ A.",
           r"$B = \dfrac{\mu_0I}{2\pi r} = \dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(12)}{2\pi(0.050)} = 4.8\times10^{-5}$ T."),
        PQ(r"A circular coil of $100$ turns and radius $4.0$ cm carries $0.50$ A. Find the flux density at its centre.",
           r"$B = \dfrac{\mu_0NI}{2R} = \dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(100)(0.50)}{2(0.040)} = 7.9\times10^{-4}$ T."),
        PQ(r"Two long parallel wires, $0.10$ m apart, carry currents of $4.0$ A and $6.0$ A in the **same** direction. Find the flux density at a point midway between them, and state its direction relative to the field of the $6.0$ A wire.",
           r"Each wire is $0.050$ m from the midpoint. $B_4 = \dfrac{\mu_0(4.0)}{2\pi(0.050)} = 1.6\times10^{-5}$ T and $B_6 = \dfrac{\mu_0(6.0)}{2\pi(0.050)} = 2.4\times10^{-5}$ T. For currents in the same direction the fields are opposite at the midpoint, so the resultant is $2.4\times10^{-5} - 1.6\times10^{-5} = 8.0\times10^{-6}$ T, in the direction of the field of the $6.0$ A wire."),
        TIP(r"Examination questions usually give you $B = \mu_0I/2\pi r$ and $B = \mu_0NI/2R$ in use, with superposition of two fields. Draw the field direction of each wire before you add."),
        SUM(r"Biot-Savart: $dB = \dfrac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\dfrac{I\,dl\sin\theta}{r^2}$; $\mu_0 = 4\pi\times10^{-7}$ T m A$^{-1}$; direction by the right-hand grip rule.",
            r"Long straight wire: $B = \mu_0I/2\pi r$, falling as $1/r$.",
            r"Circular coil: $B = \mu_0NI/2R$ at the centre, and $\dfrac{\mu_0NIR^2}{2(R^2 + x^2)^{3/2}}$ on the axis.",
            r"Fields from several currents add as vectors."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 11. Ampere's law, solenoids, parallel wires
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Magnetic fields of currents",
    title="Ampere's law, solenoids and the force between parallel wires",
    slug="phu1-ampere-solenoid",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use Ampere's law to find the field of a long wire, a solenoid and a toroid, and calculate the force between two parallel current-carrying wires, the basis of the old definition of the ampere.",
    objectives=[
        "State Ampere's law and apply it to symmetric current distributions",
        "Derive B = mu0 n I inside a long solenoid and use it, including an iron core",
        "Find the field inside and outside a straight wire of finite radius and for a toroid",
        "Calculate the force per unit length between parallel wires and explain how it defines the ampere",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Ampere's law", "Amperian loop", "Solenoid", "Toroid", "Force between parallel currents", "The ampere"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Every electric motor, door bell relay and loudspeaker has a **coil**. A long coil, a **solenoid**, gives a strong, uniform field inside it, which is why it appears in so many machines. The Biot-Savart law could find its field, but only after a long integration. **Ampere's law** does it in a few lines, whenever the situation has enough symmetry."),
        H("Ampere's law"),
        D(r"Around any closed loop, the sum of the product $B_\parallel\,\Delta l$ (the component of $B$ along the loop times a small length of the loop) equals $\mu_0$ times the total current passing through the loop:", title="Ampere's law", formula=r"\oint \mathbf{B}\cdot d\mathbf{l} = \mu_0 I_{\text{enclosed}}",
          note=r"The loop is an imaginary 'Amperian loop' chosen to follow a path where $B$ is constant in size and along the path, or perpendicular to it, so that the integral is simple. Direction of the current relative to the loop follows the right-hand grip rule."),
        T(r"The law is always true, but it is useful for finding $B$ only when symmetry lets you take $B$ out of the integral."),
        H("A long straight wire"),
        T(r"Take a circular loop of radius $r$ centred on the wire, in a plane at right angles to it. By symmetry $B$ has the same size all round the loop and points along it. So $\oint \mathbf{B}\cdot d\mathbf{l} = B(2\pi r) = \mu_0I$:"),
        F(r"B = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi r}"),
        T(r"This is the same result as the Biot-Savart integration. Now let the wire be a solid cylinder of radius $a$ carrying a uniform current density. **Inside** ($r < a$), the loop encloses only the fraction $\dfrac{\pi r^2}{\pi a^2}$ of the current, so $B(2\pi r) = \mu_0I\dfrac{r^2}{a^2}$:"),
        F(r"B_{\text{inside}} = \frac{\mu_0 I r}{2\pi a^2}\quad(r < a),\qquad B_{\text{outside}} = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi r}\quad(r > a)"),
        T(r"The field rises in proportion to $r$ inside the wire, reaches its maximum at the surface $r = a$, and then falls as $1/r$. For $I = 10$ A and $a = 2.0$ mm: at $r = 1.0$ mm, $B = 0.50$ mT; at the surface, $B = 1.0$ mT; at $r = 4.0$ mm, $B = 0.50$ mT again."),
        H("The long solenoid"),
        T(r"A **solenoid** is a long coil of wire wound closely in a helix. Let it have $n$ turns per metre and carry current $I$. For a long solenoid the field inside is uniform and along the axis; outside it is very small and can be taken as zero. Choose a rectangular Amperian loop of length $l$ with one long side inside the solenoid and the other outside."),
        IMG("al-phu1-solenoid-ampere.svg", alt="Cross-section of a long solenoid with current out of the page along the top wires and into the page along the bottom wires, a uniform field to the right inside, and a dashed rectangular loop with one long side inside and the other outside.", caption="Figure 1. The Amperian loop for a long solenoid. It encloses $N = nl$ turns."),
        T(r"Only the side inside contributes ($B\,l$): the side outside has $B \approx 0$, and the two short sides are perpendicular to $B$. The loop encloses $nl$ turns, so $Bl = \mu_0(nl)I$:"),
        D(r"The flux density inside a long solenoid of $n$ turns per metre carrying current $I$ is", title="Field in a solenoid", formula=r"B = \mu_0 n I = \frac{\mu_0 N I}{L}",
          note=r"$N$ is the total number of turns and $L$ is the length. The field does not depend on the solenoid's radius. With a core of relative permeability $\mu_r$ (iron, for example) the field is $B = \mu_0\mu_r nI$ (see your textbook for the limits of this for ferromagnetic materials)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a solenoid", intro=r"A solenoid is $0.50$ m long and has $400$ turns. It carries $1.5$ A. Find the flux density inside it.", steps=[
            S("Turns per metre", math=r"n = \frac{400}{0.50} = 800\ \text{m}^{-1}"),
            S("Flux density", math=r"B = \mu_0nI = (4\pi\times10^{-7})(800)(1.5) = 1.5\times10^{-3}\ \text{T}"),
        ]),
        T(r"Ends of a solenoid: at the centre of one end the field is about half of the central value. A short coil, or the region near the ends, is not described by this formula."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a toroid", intro=r"A **toroid** is a solenoid bent into a ring. It has $500$ turns on a ring of mean radius $0.10$ m and carries $2.0$ A. Find the flux density in the ring.", steps=[
            S("Ampere's law for a loop along the ring, which encloses $N$ turns", text=r"$B(2\pi r) = \mu_0NI$"),
            S("Solve", math=r"B = \frac{\mu_0NI}{2\pi r} = \frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(500)(2.0)}{2\pi(0.10)} = 2.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{T}"),
        ]),
        H("Force between two parallel wires"),
        T(r"Wire 1 (current $I_1$) produces a field $B_1 = \dfrac{\mu_0I_1}{2\pi d}$ at the position of wire 2, at distance $d$. Wire 2 (current $I_2$) lies at right angles to this field, so the force on a length $L$ of it is $F = B_1I_2L$:"),
        D(r"The force per unit length between two long parallel wires a distance $d$ apart carrying currents $I_1$ and $I_2$ is", title="Force between parallel currents", formula=r"\frac{F}{L} = \frac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d}",
          note=r"Currents in the **same** direction attract; currents in **opposite** directions repel. The forces on the two wires are equal and opposite (Newton's third law)."),
        T(r"**The ampere.** Before 2019 the ampere was defined using this force: one ampere is the constant current which, in two infinitely long straight parallel wires one metre apart in a vacuum, would produce a force of $2\times10^{-7}$ N per metre of length. Put $I_1 = I_2 = 1$ A and $d = 1$ m in the formula: $F/L = \dfrac{4\pi\times10^{-7}}{2\pi} = 2\times10^{-7}$ N/m. Since 2019 the ampere is defined by fixing the value of the elementary charge, but the force relation is still true to very high accuracy. (Check your textbook for which definition it uses.)"),
        EX("Worked example 3: two cables", intro=r"Two long parallel cables $5.0$ cm apart carry $20$ A and $30$ A in the same direction. Find the force per unit length, and say whether it attracts or repels.", steps=[
            S("Formula", math=r"\frac{F}{L} = \frac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d} = \frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(20)(30)}{2\pi(0.050)}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"\frac{F}{L} = 2.4\times10^{-3}\ \text{N/m}"),
            S("Direction", text="Currents in the same direction attract each other."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong turns per metre", r"In $B = \mu_0nI$, $n$ is turns **per metre**. If you have $N$ turns on a length $L$, $n = N/L$."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking parallel currents repel because 'like' charges repel", r"Parallel currents in the **same** direction attract. This is the opposite of what you might expect from like electric charges."),
        MISTAKE("Using the long-solenoid formula for a short coil", r"$B = \mu_0nI$ requires a length much greater than the radius. For a short coil use the coil formulae of the previous lesson."),
        QUIZ(r"A long solenoid has $n$ turns per metre. The current is doubled and the number of turns per metre is halved (by stretching). What happens to the flux density?",
             [r"It is doubled", r"It is unchanged", r"It is halved", r"It is quadrupled"], 1,
             r"$B = \mu_0nI$. $n \to n/2$ and $I \to 2I$, so $B$ is unchanged.",
             hint=r"$B$ is proportional to the product $nI$.",
             feedback=[r"You applied only the change in current.", "", r"You applied only the change in turns per metre.", r"Neither change enters as a square."]),
        QUIZ(r"Two parallel wires carry currents in opposite directions. What is the force between them?",
             [r"They attract each other", r"They repel each other", r"There is no force", r"They attract at short distances and repel at long ones"], 1,
             r"Opposite currents repel; the same direction attracts.",
             hint=r"Currents in the same direction attract.",
             feedback=[r"This is the case for currents in the same direction.", "", r"Each wire is in the magnetic field of the other, so there is a force.", r"The force depends on $1/d$ and its direction does not change with distance."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Use Ampere's law to derive the flux density inside a long solenoid with $n$ turns per metre carrying a current $I$. State the assumptions.",
           r"Take a rectangular Amperian loop of length $l$, one side inside the solenoid along the axis, the other outside. Assume the field is uniform and along the axis inside, and zero outside (long solenoid). The sides inside and outside contribute $Bl$ and $0$, and the short sides contribute nothing as $B$ is perpendicular to them. The loop encloses $nl$ turns, so $Bl = \mu_0nlI$, giving $B = \mu_0nI$."),
        PQ(r"A solenoid of length $0.40$ m and $800$ turns carries $2.5$ A. Find the flux density in the middle. What would the flux density be if a soft iron core of relative permeability $200$ filled it (assuming the simple relation $B = \mu_0\mu_rnI$ holds)?",
           r"$n = 800/0.40 = 2000$ m$^{-1}$. $B = \mu_0nI = (4\pi\times10^{-7})(2000)(2.5) = 6.3\times10^{-3}$ T. With the core, $B = 200 \times 6.3\times10^{-3} = 1.26$ T."),
        PQ(r"A straight conductor of radius $2.0$ mm carries a uniform current of $10$ A. Find the flux density at distances of $1.0$ mm, $2.0$ mm and $4.0$ mm from its axis.",
           r"Inside, $B = \dfrac{\mu_0Ir}{2\pi a^2}$: at $r = 1.0$ mm, $B = \dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(10)(1.0\times10^{-3})}{2\pi(2.0\times10^{-3})^2} = 5.0\times10^{-4}$ T. At the surface, $B = \dfrac{\mu_0I}{2\pi a} = 1.0\times10^{-3}$ T. Outside at $r = 4.0$ mm, $B = \dfrac{\mu_0I}{2\pi r} = 5.0\times10^{-4}$ T."),
        PQ(r"Two long parallel wires, $2.0$ cm apart, carry $15$ A and $15$ A in opposite directions. Calculate the force per metre between them. Is it attractive or repulsive?",
           r"$\dfrac{F}{L} = \dfrac{\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\pi d} = \dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(15)(15)}{2\pi(0.020)} = 2.3\times10^{-3}$ N/m. Opposite currents repel."),
        TIP(r"Solenoids and the force between parallel wires are examined in Paper 1 and Paper 2. Remember that $n$ is per metre, and draw the two fields at each wire to find the direction of the force."),
        SUM(r"Ampere's law: $\oint \mathbf{B}\cdot d\mathbf{l} = \mu_0I_{\text{enclosed}}$; use a loop whose symmetry gives a simple integral.",
            r"Long wire: $B = \mu_0I/2\pi r$ outside and $\mu_0Ir/2\pi a^2$ inside; solenoid: $B = \mu_0nI$; toroid: $B = \mu_0NI/2\pi r$.",
            r"Parallel wires: $F/L = \mu_0I_1I_2/2\pi d$; same direction attract, opposite direction repel.",
            r"The old definition of the ampere used $2\times10^{-7}$ N/m at 1 m for 1 A in each wire."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 12. Electromagnetic induction
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Electromagnetic induction",
    title="Faraday's and Lenz's laws and induced emf",
    slug="phu1-electromagnetic-induction",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Define magnetic flux and flux linkage, use Faraday's law and Lenz's law to find induced emfs and their directions, and apply them to moving rods and rotating-coil generators.",
    objectives=[
        "Define magnetic flux and flux linkage and calculate them",
        "State Faraday's and Lenz's laws and use them to find size and direction of an induced emf",
        "Derive and use emf = BLv for a conductor moving through a field",
        "Describe a simple AC generator and find the peak emf NBA omega",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Magnetic flux", "Flux linkage", "Faraday's law", "Lenz's law", "Motional emf", "AC generator"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"The dynamo on a bicycle wheel powers the lamp, with no battery. A power station near a dam generates the electricity that reaches your house. A phone charging pad charges without wires. All of them use **electromagnetic induction**, discovered by Michael Faraday in 1831: a changing magnetic field makes an emf in a conductor. In this lesson you will learn the laws that tell us how big it is and in which direction the current flows."),
        H("Magnetic flux and flux linkage"),
        D(r"The **magnetic flux** $\Phi$ through a surface of area $A$, in a uniform field $B$, is the product of the area and the component of $B$ at right angles to it. Unit: the **weber** (Wb), $1\ \text{Wb} = 1\ \text{T m}^2$.", title="Magnetic flux", formula=r"\Phi = BA\cos\theta",
          note=r"$\theta$ is the angle between $B$ and the **normal** (the perpendicular) to the surface. The flux is greatest ($BA$) when the field is at right angles to the surface ($\theta = 0$) and zero when it is parallel to the surface ($\theta = 90^\circ$)."),
        D(r"For a coil of $N$ turns, each turn is linked by the flux $\Phi$. The **flux linkage** is $N\Phi$.", title="Flux linkage", formula=r"\text{flux linkage} = N\Phi = NBA\cos\theta"),
        H("Faraday's law and Lenz's law"),
        D(r"**Faraday's law:** the magnitude of the induced emf is equal to the rate of change of flux linkage. **Lenz's law:** the direction of the induced emf (and current) is such that it opposes the change that produces it.", title="Faraday's and Lenz's laws", formula=r"\mathcal{E} = -N\frac{d\Phi}{dt}",
          note=r"The minus sign expresses Lenz's law. In calculations of size, use the magnitude: $\mathcal{E} = N\,\Delta\Phi/\Delta t$ for a steady rate of change."),
        T(r"Flux can change in three ways: the field $B$ changes, the area $A$ changes, or the angle $\theta$ changes. Any one is enough."),
        T(r"**Why Lenz's law?** It is conservation of energy. Push the north pole of a magnet towards a coil. If the induced current made the coil attract the magnet, the magnet would speed up and the coil would produce even more current, creating energy from nothing. Instead the induced current makes the near face of the coil a north pole, which **repels** the magnet. You must do work to push the magnet in, and that work becomes electrical energy."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a changing field", intro=r"A coil of $200$ turns and area $5.0\times10^{-3}$ m² has its axis along a field that falls uniformly from $0.80$ T to zero in $0.020$ s. Find the induced emf.", steps=[
            S("Change in flux linkage", math=r"\Delta(N\Phi) = NA\,\Delta B = (200)(5.0\times10^{-3})(0.80) = 0.80\ \text{Wb}"),
            S("Emf", math=r"\mathcal{E} = \frac{\Delta(N\Phi)}{\Delta t} = \frac{0.80}{0.020} = 40\ \text{V}"),
            S("Direction (Lenz)", text="The induced current opposes the fall, so it produces a field in the same direction as the original field."),
        ]),
        H("A conductor moving through a field"),
        T(r"A straight rod of length $L$ moves at speed $v$ at right angles to a uniform field $B$ and to its own length. In a time $t$ it sweeps out an area $\Delta A = Lvt$, so the flux it cuts is $\Delta\Phi = B\Delta A = BLvt$. By Faraday's law:"),
        D(r"The emf induced in a straight conductor of length $L$ moving at speed $v$ at right angles to a field $B$ is", title="Motional emf", formula=r"\mathcal{E} = BLv",
          note=r"If the velocity makes an angle to the field the formula becomes $BLv\sin\theta$. The direction of the current is given by **Fleming's right-hand rule** (or by Lenz's law)."),
        T(r"You can also see this from the magnetic force on the free electrons in the rod. They are pushed along the rod, and charge builds up at the ends until the electric force balances the magnetic force: $eE = Bev$, so $\mathcal{E} = EL = BLv$."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a rod on rails", intro=r"A rod $0.50$ m long is pulled at $4.0$ m/s along rails in a field of $0.20$ T at right angles to the plane of the rails. The circuit has a total resistance of $2.0\ \Omega$. Find the emf, the current, the force needed to move the rod at constant speed, and the power supplied.", steps=[
            S("Emf", math=r"\mathcal{E} = BLv = (0.20)(0.50)(4.0) = 0.40\ \text{V}"),
            S("Current", math=r"I = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{R} = \frac{0.40}{2.0} = 0.20\ \text{A}"),
            S("Magnetic force on the rod, opposing the motion (Lenz)", math=r"F = BIL = (0.20)(0.20)(0.50) = 0.020\ \text{N}"),
            S("Power supplied by the person pulling", math=r"P = Fv = (0.020)(4.0) = 0.080\ \text{W}"),
        ], check=r"I^2R = (0.20)^2(2.0) = 0.080\ \text{W}"),
        T(r"The mechanical power $Fv$ equals the electrical power $I^2R$. This confirms conservation of energy, and shows why Lenz's law must be true."),
        H("The AC generator"),
        T(r"A coil of $N$ turns and area $A$ rotates at constant angular speed $\omega$ in a uniform field $B$. The angle between $B$ and the coil's normal is $\theta = \omega t$, so the flux linkage is"),
        F(r"N\Phi = NBA\cos\omega t \;\Rightarrow\; \mathcal{E} = -\frac{d(N\Phi)}{dt} = NBA\omega\sin\omega t"),
        IMG("al-phu1-coil-emf.svg", alt="Two sinusoidal curves against time over two periods. The flux curve is a cosine that starts at its maximum; the emf curve is a sine that starts at zero, so the emf is largest when the flux is zero.", caption="Figure 1. Flux and induced emf for a rotating coil. The emf is largest when the flux is changing fastest, which is when the flux is zero."),
        D(r"The peak emf of a rotating-coil generator is $\mathcal{E}_0 = NBA\omega = 2\pi fNBA$. The emf is a sine wave of frequency $f$, and it is largest when the coil's plane is parallel to the field and zero when the coil is perpendicular to the field.", title="Peak emf of a generator"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a small generator", intro=r"A coil has $100$ turns of area $0.020$ m² and rotates at $50$ Hz in a field of $0.050$ T. Find the peak emf, and the emf $2.0$ ms after the coil is perpendicular to the field.", steps=[
            S("Angular speed", math=r"\omega = 2\pi f = 2\pi(50) = 314\ \text{rad/s}"),
            S("Peak emf", math=r"\mathcal{E}_0 = NBA\omega = (100)(0.050)(0.020)(314) = 31\ \text{V}"),
            S("At $t = 2.0$ ms", math=r"\mathcal{E} = 31.4\sin(314 \times 0.0020) = 31.4\sin(0.628) = 18.5\ \text{V}"),
        ]),
        H("Eddy currents"),
        T(r"A changing flux also induces currents in solid pieces of metal. These **eddy currents** flow in loops inside the metal and heat it. Lenz's law means they oppose the change: a magnet swinging over a copper plate is slowed down (used in electromagnetic braking). To reduce the waste heating in transformer cores, the iron is made of thin insulated layers (laminations)."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Saying the emf depends on the flux itself", r"The emf depends on the **rate of change** of the flux linkage. A large steady flux gives no emf; a small, rapidly changing flux can give a large one."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the turns", r"For a coil, the emf is $N\,\Delta\Phi/\Delta t$. Do not leave out $N$."),
        MISTAKE("Getting the direction by the 'same as the change' idea", r"The induced current opposes the **change** in flux. If the flux is increasing, the induced field is opposite to the original; if it is decreasing, the induced field is in the same direction."),
        QUIZ(r"A coil is rotated so that its normal changes from parallel to the field to perpendicular to the field. The flux through it",
             [r"increases from zero to $BA$", r"decreases from $BA$ to zero", r"stays at $BA$", r"is zero throughout"], 1,
             r"$\Phi = BA\cos\theta$. As $\theta$ goes from $0^\circ$ to $90^\circ$, $\cos\theta$ falls from 1 to 0.",
             hint=r"$\theta$ is the angle between $B$ and the normal.",
             feedback=[r"The flux is greatest when the normal is parallel to the field.", "", r"The angle changes, so the flux changes.", r"The flux is zero only when the field is parallel to the surface."]),
        QUIZ(r"A bar magnet is dropped through a long copper tube and falls much more slowly than a non-magnetic bar of the same size. The explanation is",
             [r"air resistance in the tube", r"eddy currents induced in the tube oppose the magnet's motion", r"copper attracts magnets", r"the magnet loses its magnetism"], 1,
             r"As the magnet moves, the flux through the tube changes and eddy currents flow. By Lenz's law their fields oppose the motion, slowing the magnet.",
             hint=r"Think about Lenz's law and the induced currents in the tube.",
             feedback=[r"Air resistance is the same for a non-magnetic bar; the difference is due to the magnet.", "", r"Copper is not ferromagnetic and does not attract magnets.", r"The magnet keeps its magnetism."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"State Faraday's law and Lenz's law, and explain how Lenz's law is an example of the conservation of energy.",
           r"Faraday: the induced emf is equal to the rate of change of flux linkage. Lenz: the induced emf is in a direction such that it opposes the change that causes it. If the induced current aided the change, the effect would reinforce its own cause and energy would be created without any work being done. Because it opposes, work must be done against the opposing force, and this work becomes electrical energy, so energy is conserved."),
        PQ(r"A circular coil of $50$ turns and radius $4.0$ cm is placed with its plane at right angles to a field that increases uniformly from $0.10$ T to $0.50$ T in $0.20$ s. Find the induced emf.",
           r"$A = \pi r^2 = \pi(0.040)^2 = 5.03\times10^{-3}$ m². $\mathcal{E} = N\dfrac{A\,\Delta B}{\Delta t} = (50)\dfrac{(5.03\times10^{-3})(0.40)}{0.20} = 0.50$ V."),
        PQ(r"An aircraft with a wingspan of $40$ m flies horizontally at $250$ m/s. The vertical component of Earth's field is $4.0\times10^{-5}$ T. Find the pd between the wing tips.",
           r"$\mathcal{E} = BLv = (4.0\times10^{-5})(40)(250) = 0.40$ V."),
        PQ(r"A coil of $200$ turns and area $4.0\times10^{-3}$ m² rotates at $25$ Hz in a field of $0.30$ T. Calculate the peak emf, and state the position of the coil when the emf is zero.",
           r"$\omega = 2\pi(25) = 157$ rad/s. $\mathcal{E}_0 = NBA\omega = (200)(0.30)(4.0\times10^{-3})(157) = 37.7$ V. The emf is zero when the flux through the coil is at its maximum, that is, when the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field."),
        TIP(r"Induction questions in Paper 1 usually test Lenz's law (direction) and ratios, while Paper 2 asks for emf calculations, graphs of emf against time and explanations in terms of flux linkage. Always say 'rate of change of flux linkage' in explanations."),
        SUM(r"Flux $\Phi = BA\cos\theta$ (weber); flux linkage $N\Phi$.",
            r"Faraday: $\mathcal{E} = -N\,d\Phi/dt$. Lenz: the induced current opposes the change that causes it (conservation of energy).",
            r"A straight conductor moving at right angles to a field: $\mathcal{E} = BLv$.",
            r"Rotating-coil generator: $\mathcal{E} = NBA\omega\sin\omega t$, peak $2\pi fNBA$; zero emf when the flux is greatest."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 13. Self and mutual inductance
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Self and mutual inductance",
    title="Self-inductance, mutual inductance and energy in an inductor",
    slug="phu1-self-mutual-inductance",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Define self-inductance L and mutual inductance M, find the induced emf, derive L for a solenoid and the energy 1/2 L I^2, analyse current growth in an LR circuit and use the ideal transformer.",
    objectives=[
        "Define self-inductance and the henry, and use E = -L dI/dt",
        "Derive L = mu0 N^2 A / l for a long solenoid and the energy stored (1/2) L I^2",
        "Describe and use the growth of current in an LR circuit, with time constant L/R",
        "Define mutual inductance and apply it to an ideal transformer",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Self-inductance", "Henry", "Back emf", "Energy stored in an inductor", "LR time constant", "Mutual inductance", "Transformer"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Switch off a heavy machine or a fluorescent light and a small spark may jump across the switch contacts. The current was suddenly stopped, but the coil in the machine tried to keep it flowing. A coil resists a **change** in its own current, because the changing current creates a changing flux in the same coil, which induces an emf. This property is called **self-inductance**."),
        H("Self-inductance"),
        T(r"When a current $I$ flows in a coil of $N$ turns, it creates a flux $\Phi$ through each turn. The flux linkage $N\Phi$ is proportional to $I$:"),
        D(r"The **self-inductance** $L$ of a coil is the flux linkage per unit current. Unit: the **henry** (H): $1\ \text{H} = 1\ \text{Wb A}^{-1} = 1\ \text{V s A}^{-1}$.", title="Self-inductance", formula=r"L = \frac{N\Phi}{I}"),
        T(r"If the current changes, Faraday's law gives the induced emf, which by Lenz's law opposes the change (a **back emf**):"),
        D(r"The emf induced in a coil of self-inductance $L$ when the current changes at rate $dI/dt$ is", title="Induced emf", formula=r"\mathcal{E} = -L\frac{dI}{dt}",
          note="One henry is the self-inductance of a coil in which an emf of 1 V is induced when the current changes at 1 A per second."),
        T(r"**A solenoid.** For a long solenoid of $N$ turns, length $l$ and cross-sectional area $A$: $B = \mu_0\dfrac{N}{l}I$, so $\Phi = BA$ and"),
        F(r"L = \frac{N\Phi}{I} = \frac{\mu_0N^2A}{l}"),
        T(r"$L$ depends on the square of the number of turns and on the geometry. An iron core multiplies $L$ by $\mu_r$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a solenoid", intro=r"A solenoid has $1000$ turns, length $0.40$ m and cross-sectional area $4.0\times10^{-4}$ m². Find its inductance. The current in it falls uniformly from $2.0$ A to zero in $10$ ms. Find the induced emf.", steps=[
            S("Inductance", math=r"L = \frac{\mu_0N^2A}{l} = \frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(1000)^2(4.0\times10^{-4})}{0.40} = 1.26\times10^{-3}\ \text{H} = 1.26\ \text{mH}"),
            S("Rate of change of current", math=r"\frac{dI}{dt} = \frac{-2.0}{0.010} = -200\ \text{A/s}"),
            S("Induced emf", math=r"\mathcal{E} = -L\frac{dI}{dt} = (1.26\times10^{-3})(200) = 0.25\ \text{V}"),
            S("Direction", text="The emf is in the direction of the current, trying to keep the falling current flowing."),
        ]),
        H("Energy stored in an inductor"),
        T(r"To increase the current, the supply must push against the back emf. When the current is $I$ and is rising at $dI/dt$, the power supplied to the inductor is $P = I\cdot L\dfrac{dI}{dt}$. The energy to take the current from $0$ to $I$ is"),
        F(r"W = \int_0^{I}LI'\,dI' = \tfrac{1}{2}LI^2"),
        D(r"The energy stored in the magnetic field of an inductor carrying current $I$ is", title="Energy in an inductor", formula=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}LI^2",
          note=r"Compare with $\tfrac{1}{2}CV^2$ for a capacitor. The energy is stored in the magnetic field, and is released if the current is broken, which causes the spark at a switch."),
        EX("Worked example 2: energy", intro=r"Find the energy stored in the solenoid of Worked example 1 when it carries $2.0$ A.", steps=[
            S("Energy", math=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}LI^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(1.26\times10^{-3})(2.0)^2 = 2.5\times10^{-3}\ \text{J}"),
        ]),
        H("Current growth in an LR circuit"),
        T(r"Connect a coil of inductance $L$ and resistance $R$ to a cell of emf $V$. The cell's emf is shared between the resistance and the back emf: $V = IR + L\dfrac{dI}{dt}$. At the start $I = 0$, so $L\dfrac{dI}{dt} = V$ and the current rises at rate $V/L$. As $I$ grows, $IR$ takes more of the emf and the rise slows. The solution is"),
        D(r"The current grows exponentially to its final value $I_0 = V/R$ with time constant $L/R$:", title="Growth of current", formula=r"I = I_0\left(1 - e^{-Rt/L}\right),\qquad \tau = \frac{L}{R}",
          note=r"The curve has the same shape as capacitor charging. After one time constant, the current has reached 63% of its final value. When the supply is switched off through a resistor, the current decays as $I_0e^{-Rt/L}$."),
        IMG("al-phu1-lr-growth.svg", alt="Graph of current against time in milliseconds rising from zero towards 0.48 amperes, reaching 63 per cent of the final value at 20 milliseconds.", caption="Figure 1. Current growth in an inductor of 0.50 H with R = 25 ohm and V = 12 V: the time constant L/R is 20 ms."),
        EX("Worked example 3: an LR circuit", intro=r"A coil of inductance $0.50$ H and resistance $25\ \Omega$ is connected to a $12$ V supply. Find the time constant, the final current, the initial rate of rise of current, and the current after $40$ ms.", steps=[
            S("Time constant", math=r"\tau = \frac{L}{R} = \frac{0.50}{25} = 0.020\ \text{s}"),
            S("Final current", math=r"I_0 = \frac{V}{R} = \frac{12}{25} = 0.48\ \text{A}"),
            S("Initial rate of rise", math=r"\frac{dI}{dt} = \frac{V}{L} = \frac{12}{0.50} = 24\ \text{A/s}"),
            S("Current after 40 ms, which is two time constants", math=r"I = 0.48\left(1 - e^{-2}\right) = 0.48(0.8647) = 0.415\ \text{A}"),
        ]),
        H("Mutual inductance and the transformer"),
        T(r"Place two coils close together. A changing current $I_p$ in the **primary** coil creates a changing flux, part of which links the **secondary** coil and induces an emf there. This is **mutual inductance**."),
        D(r"The **mutual inductance** $M$ of two coils is the flux linkage in the secondary per unit current in the primary. The emf induced in the secondary is", title="Mutual inductance", formula=r"M = \frac{N_s\Phi_s}{I_p},\qquad \mathcal{E}_s = -M\frac{dI_p}{dt}",
          note="Unit: the henry. Like self-inductance, it depends on the geometry of the coils and the material between them."),
        EX("Worked example 4: mutual inductance", intro=r"Two coils have mutual inductance $25$ mH. The current in the primary falls uniformly from $4.0$ A to zero in $20$ ms. Find the emf induced in the secondary.", steps=[
            S("Rate of change", math=r"\frac{dI_p}{dt} = \frac{-4.0}{0.020} = -200\ \text{A/s}"),
            S("Emf", math=r"\mathcal{E}_s = M\left|\frac{dI_p}{dt}\right| = (25\times10^{-3})(200) = 5.0\ \text{V}"),
        ]),
        T(r"A **transformer** has two coils wound on a common iron core, so that nearly all the flux links both. The same flux changes through each turn of both coils, so the emfs are in proportion to the number of turns. For an **ideal** transformer (no losses):"),
        F(r"\frac{V_s}{V_p} = \frac{N_s}{N_p},\qquad V_pI_p = V_sI_s"),
        T(r"For a step-down transformer from $230$ V to $12$ V with $1150$ turns on the primary, the secondary needs $N_s = 1150 \times \dfrac{12}{230} = 60$ turns. If it delivers $24$ W at $12$ V, the secondary current is $2.0$ A. Real transformers lose energy through coil resistance, eddy currents in the core, and incomplete flux linkage. Grid companies use high voltages for transmission because, for a given power, a higher voltage means a smaller current and less $I^2R$ loss in the lines."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Thinking inductance opposes current itself", r"A coil opposes a **change** in current. A steady current in a coil with no resistance has no back emf at all."),
        MISTAKE("Using the stored-energy formula for a transformer", r"$\tfrac{1}{2}LI^2$ is the energy stored in one coil's field. A transformer works by mutual induction and AC; there is no steady storage of energy in the ideal case."),
        MISTAKE("Using DC in a transformer", r"A transformer needs a changing flux. With a steady direct current there is no change of flux, so no emf is induced in the secondary."),
        QUIZ(r"The current in a coil of inductance $0.20$ H rises uniformly from $0$ to $3.0$ A in $0.050$ s. What is the magnitude of the induced emf?",
             [r"$0.60$ V", r"$12$ V", r"$30$ V", r"$0.033$ V"], 1,
             r"$\mathcal{E} = L\dfrac{dI}{dt} = 0.20 \times \dfrac{3.0}{0.050} = 12$ V.",
             hint=r"Find $dI/dt$ first.",
             feedback=[r"This is $L \times \Delta I$; the time interval is missing.", "", r"This is $L\times\Delta I/\Delta t$ with the wrong power of ten.", r"You divided by $L$ and $\Delta I$ the wrong way round."]),
        QUIZ(r"The energy stored in an inductor is $E$ when the current is $I$. What is the energy when the current is $2I$?",
             [r"$2E$", r"$4E$", r"$E/2$", r"$E$"], 1,
             r"$E = \tfrac{1}{2}LI^2$ is proportional to $I^2$, so doubling $I$ multiplies the energy by 4.",
             hint=r"The energy depends on $I^2$.",
             feedback=[r"This treats $E$ as proportional to $I$.", "", r"The energy increases with current.", r"The energy depends on the current."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Define self-inductance and the henry. Show that the emf induced in a coil when the current changes is $\mathcal{E} = -L\,dI/dt$.",
           r"Self-inductance is the flux linkage per unit current, $L = N\Phi/I$. One henry is one weber per ampere. The induced emf is $-d(N\Phi)/dt$ (Faraday). Since $N\Phi = LI$ with $L$ constant, $\mathcal{E} = -\dfrac{d(LI)}{dt} = -L\dfrac{dI}{dt}$. The minus sign shows that the emf opposes the change (Lenz)."),
        PQ(r"A long solenoid has $500$ turns, a length of $0.25$ m and a cross-sectional area of $3.0\times10^{-4}$ m². Calculate its self-inductance and the energy stored when the current is $1.5$ A.",
           r"$L = \dfrac{\mu_0N^2A}{l} = \dfrac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(500)^2(3.0\times10^{-4})}{0.25} = 3.8\times10^{-4}$ H. $E = \tfrac{1}{2}LI^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(3.77\times10^{-4})(1.5)^2 = 4.2\times10^{-4}$ J."),
        PQ(r"A coil of inductance $2.0$ H and resistance $10\ \Omega$ is connected to a $20$ V supply. Find the time constant, the final current, and the time for the current to reach $1.5$ A.",
           r"$\tau = L/R = 0.20$ s. $I_0 = V/R = 2.0$ A. For $1.5$ A: $1.5 = 2.0(1 - e^{-t/0.20})$, so $e^{-t/0.20} = 0.25$ and $t = 0.20\ln 4 = 0.28$ s."),
        PQ(r"An ideal transformer steps $230$ V down to $12$ V. The primary has $1150$ turns and the secondary delivers $2.0$ A. Find the number of secondary turns and the primary current.",
           r"$N_s = N_p\dfrac{V_s}{V_p} = 1150\times\dfrac{12}{230} = 60$. Power in equals power out: $V_pI_p = V_sI_s$, so $I_p = \dfrac{12\times2.0}{230} = 0.10$ A."),
        TIP(r"Inductance is examined in Paper 2 (derivations of $L$ and $\tfrac{1}{2}LI^2$, LR curves) and in Paper 1 as short conceptual questions. Compare each result with the capacitor equivalent to help your memory."),
        SUM(r"Self-inductance $L = N\Phi/I$ (henry); induced emf $\mathcal{E} = -L\,dI/dt$ opposes the change in current; solenoid $L = \mu_0N^2A/l$.",
            r"Energy stored in an inductor: $E = \tfrac{1}{2}LI^2$.",
            r"LR circuit: $I = I_0(1 - e^{-Rt/L})$ with $\tau = L/R$ and $I_0 = V/R$.",
            r"Mutual inductance $M = N_s\Phi_s/I_p$, $\mathcal{E}_s = -M\,dI_p/dt$; ideal transformer $V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p$ and $V_pI_p = V_sI_s$."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 14. Alternating current and rms
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Alternating current and rms values",
    title="Alternating current, peak values and rms values",
    slug="phu1-ac-rms",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Describe sinusoidal AC with peak value, frequency and angular frequency, derive the rms value I0/sqrt(2) from mean power, and read values from oscilloscope traces.",
    objectives=[
        "Write the equations of a sinusoidal current and pd and state their period, frequency and angular frequency",
        "Define the root-mean-square (rms) value and derive I_rms = I0/sqrt 2",
        "Calculate power in a resistor from rms or peak values",
        "Read peak value and frequency from an oscilloscope trace",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Alternating current", "Peak value", "Frequency and period", "Mean power", "Root-mean-square value"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"The electricity in a house in Yaounde or Bamenda is **alternating current**. The label on an electric kettle might say '220 V, 50 Hz'. The current in the wire does not flow steadily in one direction: it changes direction 100 times every second. Why do we quote 220 V, when the pd actually swings up to about 311 V? The answer is the **root-mean-square (rms) value**, which is the key idea of this lesson."),
        H("Describing an alternating current"),
        T(r"The output of a generator is a **sinusoidal** pd. We write it as"),
        F(r"V = V_0\sin\omega t,\qquad I = I_0\sin\omega t,\qquad \omega = 2\pi f = \frac{2\pi}{T}"),
        KEY("The quantities", "These words are used for any oscillation.", cards=[
            [r"Peak value $V_0$, $I_0$", "The greatest value in either direction (the amplitude)."],
            [r"Period $T$", "The time for one complete cycle, in seconds."],
            [r"Frequency $f$", r"Cycles per second, $f = 1/T$, in hertz (Hz)."],
            [r"Angular frequency $\omega$", r"$\omega = 2\pi f$, in radians per second."]]),
        T(r"For a 50 Hz supply, $T = 20$ ms and $\omega = 100\pi = 314$ rad/s."),
        H("Why we need an average: mean power"),
        T(r"The mean (average) value of a sine wave over one cycle is **zero**, because the positive and negative halves cancel. This is no use for describing heating, because a resistor heats the same whichever way the current flows. Heating depends on the **square** of the current, and the square of a sine wave is never negative."),
        T(r"The instantaneous power in a resistor $R$ is $P = I^2R = I_0^2R\sin^2\omega t$. The average value of $\sin^2\omega t$ over a cycle is $\tfrac{1}{2}$. (This follows from $\sin^2\theta = \tfrac{1}{2}(1 - \cos 2\theta)$, and the average of $\cos 2\theta$ is zero.) So the mean power is"),
        F(r"\bar{P} = \tfrac{1}{2}I_0^2R"),
        D(r"The **root-mean-square (rms)** value of an alternating current is the value of the steady direct current that would dissipate the same power in a resistor. Equating $I_{\text{rms}}^2R = \tfrac{1}{2}I_0^2R$:", title="rms value", formula=r"I_{\text{rms}} = \frac{I_0}{\sqrt{2}} = 0.707\,I_0,\qquad V_{\text{rms}} = \frac{V_0}{\sqrt{2}}",
          note=r"The name describes the calculation: take the **square** of the current, find its **mean** over a cycle, then take the **root**. This relation applies to a sinusoidal waveform only."),
        IMG("al-phu1-ac-rms.svg", alt="Left graph: a sine wave of pd with peak 311 volts and a dashed line at its rms value 220 volts. Right graph: the power in a resistor, which oscillates between zero and its peak, with a dashed line at half the peak.", caption="Figure 1. A 220 V rms, 50 Hz supply. The power oscillates at twice the supply frequency, with a mean of half the peak."),
        T(r"Unless stated otherwise, the values of AC currents and voltages in a question, and the numbers on meters and appliances, are rms values. So for a $220$ V rms supply the peak value is $220\sqrt{2} = 311$ V. The mean power in a resistor is"),
        F(r"\bar{P} = I_{\text{rms}}V_{\text{rms}} = I_{\text{rms}}^2R = \frac{V_{\text{rms}}^2}{R}"),
        EX("Worked example 1: a heater", intro=r"A heater is marked 2.0 kW, 220 V (rms). Find the rms current, the resistance, the peak current and the peak power.", steps=[
            S("rms current", math=r"I_{\text{rms}} = \frac{P}{V_{\text{rms}}} = \frac{2000}{220} = 9.09\ \text{A}"),
            S("Resistance", math=r"R = \frac{V_{\text{rms}}^2}{P} = \frac{220^2}{2000} = 24.2\ \Omega"),
            S("Peak current", math=r"I_0 = \sqrt{2}\,I_{\text{rms}} = 12.9\ \text{A}"),
            S("Peak power", math=r"P_{\text{peak}} = I_0^2R = (12.9)^2(24.2) = 4.0\ \text{kW} = 2\bar{P}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: when does the pd reach 220 V?", intro=r"The pd of a mains supply is $V = 311\sin(100\pi t)$ volts. After how long, starting from $V = 0$, does it first reach $220$ V?", steps=[
            S("Set $V = 220$", math=r"\sin(100\pi t) = \frac{220}{311} = 0.707"),
            S("Solve for the angle", math=r"100\pi t = \frac{\pi}{4} \;\Rightarrow\; t = \frac{1}{400} = 2.5\times10^{-3}\ \text{s}"),
            S("Meaning", text="The instantaneous value equals the rms value one eighth of the way through a cycle ($t = T/8 = 2.5$ ms)."),
        ]),
        H("Reading an oscilloscope trace"),
        T(r"An oscilloscope shows the pd against time. The **vertical** scale (volts per division) gives the peak value and the **horizontal** scale (time per division) gives the period."),
        EX("Worked example 3: an oscilloscope trace", intro=r"A trace of an alternating pd has a peak-to-trough height of $6.0$ divisions at a setting of $2.0$ V/div. One full cycle spans $4.0$ divisions at $5.0$ ms/div. Find the peak value, the rms value and the frequency.", steps=[
            S("Peak value is half the peak-to-trough height", math=r"V_0 = \tfrac{1}{2}(6.0)(2.0) = 6.0\ \text{V}"),
            S("rms value", math=r"V_{\text{rms}} = \frac{6.0}{\sqrt{2}} = 4.2\ \text{V}"),
            S("Period", math=r"T = (4.0)(5.0) = 20\ \text{ms} \;\Rightarrow\; f = \frac{1}{0.020} = 50\ \text{Hz}"),
        ]),
        T(r"**Other waveforms.** The $1/\sqrt{2}$ factor is for a sine wave. For a half-wave rectified sine wave (only the positive half cycles pass), the mean of $I^2$ is $\tfrac{1}{2}\times\tfrac{1}{2}I_0^2$ over a whole cycle, so $I_{\text{rms}} = I_0/2$. For a 10 V peak half-wave signal, $V_{\text{rms}} = 5.0$ V, compared with $7.07$ V for the full sine wave."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the peak value in power formulae", r"$P = I^2R$ gives the **mean** power only when $I$ is the rms current. With peak values, use $\bar{P} = \tfrac{1}{2}I_0^2R$."),
        MISTAKE("Reading peak-to-peak as the amplitude", r"On an oscilloscope the peak value is **half** of the height from the lowest point to the highest point."),
        MISTAKE("Saying the average current of an AC supply is the rms value", r"The mean of a sine wave over a cycle is zero. The rms value is the square root of the mean of the square."),
        QUIZ(r"An alternating current has a peak value of $5.0$ A. What steady direct current would produce the same mean heating in a resistor?",
             [r"$2.5$ A", r"$3.5$ A", r"$5.0$ A", r"$7.1$ A"], 1,
             r"$I_{\text{rms}} = I_0/\sqrt{2} = 5.0/1.414 = 3.5$ A.",
             hint=r"That is the definition of the rms value.",
             feedback=[r"This halves the peak value; the correct factor is $1/\sqrt{2}$.", "", r"The peak value is reached only for an instant. The heating of a steady $5.0$ A would be twice as great.", r"You multiplied by $\sqrt{2}$ instead of dividing."]),
        QUIZ(r"A $60$ W lamp is designed for a $220$ V rms supply. What is the peak pd across it?",
             [r"$155$ V", r"$220$ V", r"$311$ V", r"$440$ V"], 2,
             r"$V_0 = \sqrt{2}\,V_{\text{rms}} = 1.414 \times 220 = 311$ V.",
             hint=r"The rated voltage is the rms value.",
             feedback=[r"This divides by $\sqrt{2}$ instead of multiplying.", r"This is the rms value.", "", r"This is $2V_{\text{rms}}$, not $\sqrt{2}V_{\text{rms}}$."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Explain what is meant by the rms value of an alternating current, and show that for a sinusoidal current it is $I_0/\sqrt{2}$.",
           r"The rms value is the steady direct current that dissipates the same mean power in a resistor. The power is $I_0^2R\sin^2\omega t$, whose mean over a cycle is $\tfrac{1}{2}I_0^2R$. Equating to $I_{\text{rms}}^2R$ gives $I_{\text{rms}}^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}I_0^2$, so $I_{\text{rms}} = I_0/\sqrt{2}$."),
        PQ(r"An alternating pd is given by $V = 170\sin(120\pi t)$ volts. State the peak value, the rms value, the frequency and the period.",
           r"Peak $= 170$ V. rms $= 170/\sqrt{2} = 120$ V. $\omega = 120\pi$, so $f = \omega/2\pi = 60$ Hz and $T = 1/60 = 0.0167$ s $= 16.7$ ms."),
        PQ(r"A resistor of $50\ \Omega$ is connected to a supply of $230$ V rms. Find the mean power dissipated and the peak current.",
           r"$\bar{P} = \dfrac{V_{\text{rms}}^2}{R} = \dfrac{230^2}{50} = 1058$ W $\approx 1.06$ kW. $I_{\text{rms}} = \dfrac{230}{50} = 4.6$ A, so $I_0 = \sqrt{2}(4.6) = 6.5$ A."),
        PQ(r"The display of an oscilloscope shows a sine wave whose height from trough to crest is $5.0$ cm. The y-gain is $4.0$ V/cm and the time base is $2.0$ ms/cm. One cycle takes $5.0$ cm along the screen. Find the rms voltage and the frequency.",
           r"Peak $= \tfrac{1}{2}(5.0)(4.0) = 10$ V; rms $= 10/\sqrt{2} = 7.1$ V. Period $= (5.0)(2.0) = 10$ ms, so $f = 1/0.010 = 100$ Hz."),
        TIP(r"Paper 1 often includes a trace on a screen to read peak value and frequency, and Paper 2 asks for mean power from rms values. In Paper 3 you may measure the frequency of an AC signal with an oscilloscope."),
        SUM(r"A sinusoidal pd: $V = V_0\sin\omega t$ with $\omega = 2\pi f = 2\pi/T$.",
            r"The mean power in a resistor is $\tfrac{1}{2}I_0^2R$; the rms value is the equivalent direct current: $I_{\text{rms}} = I_0/\sqrt{2}$, $V_{\text{rms}} = V_0/\sqrt{2}$ (sine waves only).",
            r"Meter readings and appliance ratings are rms values; mains of 220 V rms has a peak of 311 V.",
            r"On an oscilloscope, peak = half of peak-to-peak; period from the time base gives the frequency."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 15. Reactance, impedance and phasors in series RLC
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Series RLC circuits and resonance",
    title="Reactance, impedance and phasors in a series RLC circuit",
    slug="phu1-rlc-impedance",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Find the reactance of inductors and capacitors, draw phasor diagrams for a series RLC circuit, calculate impedance, phase angle, current and power, and explain the power factor.",
    objectives=[
        "State the phase relationships between current and pd for R, L and C",
        "Calculate inductive reactance X_L = omega L and capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(omega C)",
        "Use a phasor diagram to find the impedance Z and phase angle of a series RLC circuit",
        "Calculate the mean power and power factor of an AC circuit",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Reactance", "Impedance", "Phase angle", "Phasor diagram", "Power factor"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A fluorescent tube, an electric motor and a phone charger all contain coils and capacitors as well as resistors. In a DC circuit only resistance limits the current. In an AC circuit, a coil and a capacitor also oppose the current, in a way that depends on the **frequency**. In this lesson you will find out how much they oppose it, and how their effects combine."),
        H("The phase of current and pd"),
        KEY("Phase relationships", r"Take the current $I = I_0\sin\omega t$ as the reference.", cards=[
            [r"Resistor", r"$V_R$ is **in phase** with $I$: $V_R = IR$."],
            [r"Inductor", r"$V_L$ **leads** $I$ by $90^\circ$ ($\tfrac{1}{4}$ cycle)."],
            [r"Capacitor", r"$V_C$ **lags** $I$ by $90^\circ$."]]),
        T(r"**Inductor.** The back emf is $-L\,dI/dt$, so the pd across the inductor is $V_L = L\,dI/dt = LI_0\omega\cos\omega t = \omega LI_0\sin(\omega t + 90^\circ)$. It leads the current by $90^\circ$ and has peak value $\omega LI_0$."),
        T(r"**Capacitor.** The charge $Q = CV$, so the current is $I = C\,dV_C/dt$. For $I = I_0\sin\omega t$, integrating gives $V_C = \dfrac{I_0}{\omega C}\sin(\omega t - 90^\circ)$. It lags the current by $90^\circ$ and has peak value $I_0/\omega C$."),
        H("Reactance"),
        D(r"**Reactance** is the ratio of the peak (or rms) pd to the peak (or rms) current for an inductor or capacitor. The unit is the ohm. Reactance changes with frequency.", title="Reactance", formula=r"X_L = \omega L = 2\pi fL,\qquad X_C = \frac{1}{\omega C} = \frac{1}{2\pi fC}",
          note=r"An inductor passes low frequencies more easily than high ones ($X_L$ rises with $f$). A capacitor passes high frequencies more easily ($X_C$ falls as $f$ rises). At $f = 0$ (direct current), a capacitor has infinite reactance (it blocks DC)."),
        T(r"Unlike resistance, reactance does not dissipate energy. The inductor and capacitor store energy in each quarter-cycle and give it back in the next."),
        EX("Worked example 1: reactance values", intro=r"Find the reactance of a $0.20$ H inductor and of a $50\ \mu$F capacitor at $50$ Hz, and then at $500$ Hz.", steps=[
            S("Inductor at 50 Hz", math=r"X_L = 2\pi(50)(0.20) = 63\ \Omega"),
            S("Inductor at 500 Hz", math=r"X_L = 2\pi(500)(0.20) = 628\ \Omega\ (\text{ten times larger})"),
            S("Capacitor at 50 Hz", math=r"X_C = \frac{1}{2\pi(50)(50\times10^{-6})} = 64\ \Omega"),
            S("Capacitor at 500 Hz", math=r"X_C = 6.4\ \Omega\ (\text{ten times smaller})"),
        ]),
        H("Series RLC and impedance"),
        T(r"In a series circuit the same current flows through all components. The pds across the components are not in phase, so we add them as **phasors**: arrows whose length is the peak (or rms) value and whose direction shows the phase. With the current phasor pointing to the right, $V_R$ points right, $V_L$ points up and $V_C$ points down."),
        IMG("al-phu1-phasor-rlc.svg", alt="Phasor diagram with VR to the right, VL up, VC down, the difference VL minus VC drawn up from the end of VR, and the total V as the hypotenuse making the phase angle with the current direction.", caption="Figure 1. Phasor diagram for R = 100 ohm, L = 0.40 H and C = 40 microfarad at 50 Hz, drawn for a current of 1.0 A."),
        T(r"Since $V_L$ and $V_C$ are in opposite directions, they partly cancel. Their resultant, $V_L - V_C$, is at right angles to $V_R$. By Pythagoras:"),
        F(r"V = \sqrt{V_R^2 + (V_L - V_C)^2} = I\sqrt{R^2 + (X_L - X_C)^2}"),
        D(r"The **impedance** $Z$ of a circuit is the ratio of the pd across it to the current in it (both rms or both peak). For a series RLC circuit,", title="Impedance", formula=r"Z = \sqrt{R^2 + (X_L - X_C)^2},\qquad \tan\varphi = \frac{X_L - X_C}{R}",
          note=r"$\varphi$ is the phase angle by which the supply pd **leads** the current. If $X_L > X_C$ the circuit is mainly inductive and $V$ leads $I$ ($\varphi > 0$). If $X_C > X_L$ it is mainly capacitive and $V$ lags $I$ ($\varphi < 0$)."),
        EX("Worked example 2: R and L in series", intro=r"A $40\ \Omega$ resistor and a $0.20$ H inductor are in series across a $24$ V rms, $50$ Hz supply. Find the impedance, the current and the phase angle.", steps=[
            S("Reactance", math=r"X_L = 2\pi(50)(0.20) = 62.8\ \Omega"),
            S("Impedance", math=r"Z = \sqrt{40^2 + 62.8^2} = 74.5\ \Omega"),
            S("Current", math=r"I = \frac{V}{Z} = \frac{24}{74.5} = 0.32\ \text{A}"),
            S("Phase angle", math=r"\tan\varphi = \frac{62.8}{40} \;\Rightarrow\; \varphi = 57.5^{\circ}\ (V\ \text{leads}\ I)"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: full series RLC", intro=r"A $100\ \Omega$ resistor, a $0.40$ H inductor and a $40\ \mu$F capacitor are in series across $220$ V rms at $50$ Hz. Find $Z$, the current, the pd across each component, the phase angle and the mean power.", steps=[
            S("Reactances", math=r"X_L = 2\pi(50)(0.40) = 125.7\ \Omega,\quad X_C = \frac{1}{2\pi(50)(40\times10^{-6})} = 79.6\ \Omega"),
            S("Impedance", math=r"Z = \sqrt{100^2 + (125.7 - 79.6)^2} = \sqrt{100^2 + 46.1^2} = 110\ \Omega"),
            S("Current", math=r"I = \frac{220}{110.1} = 2.0\ \text{A}"),
            S("pds across components", math=r"V_R = 200\ \text{V},\quad V_L = IX_L = 251\ \text{V},\quad V_C = IX_C = 159\ \text{V}"),
            S("Phase angle", math=r"\tan\varphi = \frac{46.1}{100} \;\Rightarrow\; \varphi = 24.7^{\circ}"),
            S("Mean power", math=r"\bar{P} = I^2R = (2.0)^2(100) = 4.0\times10^{2}\ \text{W}"),
        ], check=r"V_{\text{rms}}I\cos\varphi = (220)(2.0)\cos 24.7^{\circ} = 4.0\times10^{2}\ \text{W}"),
        T(r"Notice that $V_L = 251$ V and $V_C = 159$ V are individually **larger** than the supply pd of $220$ V. This is possible because they are out of phase and partly cancel."),
        H("Power and power factor"),
        T(r"Only the resistor dissipates energy. The mean power is $\bar{P} = I_{\text{rms}}^2R$. Since $R = Z\cos\varphi$, this is also"),
        F(r"\bar{P} = V_{\text{rms}}I_{\text{rms}}\cos\varphi"),
        D(r"The factor $\cos\varphi = R/Z$ is the **power factor**. It is 1 for a purely resistive circuit and 0 for a pure inductor or capacitor.", title="Power factor", formula=r"\text{power factor} = \cos\varphi = \frac{R}{Z}"),
        T(r"A factory with many motors (which are inductive) has a low power factor. It draws more current for the same useful power, which wastes energy in the supply cables. Electricity companies sometimes charge extra, and the factory adds capacitors to correct it. In Example 3 the power factor is $100/110 = 0.91$."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Adding the pds as numbers", r"$V \neq V_R + V_L + V_C$. The pds are not in phase. Use phasors and Pythagoras."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up which of V and I leads", r"Remember it as **CIVIL**: in a **C**apacitor, **I** leads **V**; in an inductor (**L**), **V** leads **I**."),
        MISTAKE("Using $R$ instead of $Z$ for the current", r"The current is $V/Z$, not $V/R$, when there are reactances."),
        QUIZ(r"An inductor of $0.10$ H is connected to a $50$ Hz supply. What is its reactance?",
             [r"$5.0\ \Omega$", r"$31\ \Omega$", r"$314\ \Omega$", r"$0.10\ \Omega$"], 1,
             r"$X_L = 2\pi fL = 2\pi(50)(0.10) = 31.4\ \Omega$.",
             hint=r"$X_L = 2\pi fL$.",
             feedback=[r"This is $fL$; the $2\pi$ is missing.", "", r"This is $\omega$ without multiplying by $L$.", r"This is the value of $L$, not the reactance."]),
        QUIZ(r"The pd across a capacitor in an AC circuit",
             [r"leads the current by $90^\circ$", r"is in phase with the current", r"lags the current by $90^\circ$", r"is $180^\circ$ out of phase with the current"], 2,
             r"For a capacitor the current leads the pd by $90^\circ$, so the pd lags the current by $90^\circ$ (CIVIL).",
             hint=r"Remember CIVIL.",
             feedback=[r"This is the inductor: V leads I.", r"This is the resistor.", "", r"The pd across a capacitor is $90^\circ$ out of phase with the current, not $180^\circ$."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A $2.0\ \mu$F capacitor is connected across a $230$ V rms, $50$ Hz supply. Find its reactance and the rms current.",
           r"$X_C = \dfrac{1}{2\pi fC} = \dfrac{1}{2\pi(50)(2.0\times10^{-6})} = 1.59\times10^{3}\ \Omega$. $I = \dfrac{V}{X_C} = \dfrac{230}{1592} = 0.14$ A."),
        PQ(r"A series circuit contains $R = 30\ \Omega$ and an inductor of reactance $40\ \Omega$ at the supply frequency. Find the impedance, the phase angle, and the power factor. If the supply is $100$ V rms, find the mean power dissipated.",
           r"$Z = \sqrt{30^2 + 40^2} = 50\ \Omega$. $\tan\varphi = 40/30$, so $\varphi = 53^\circ$. Power factor $= R/Z = 0.60$. $I = 100/50 = 2.0$ A and $\bar{P} = I^2R = 4.0 \times 30 = 120$ W (check: $VI\cos\varphi = 100 \times 2.0 \times 0.60 = 120$ W)."),
        PQ(r"A series circuit has $R = 60\ \Omega$ and a capacitor with $X_C = 80\ \Omega$ and no inductance. State whether $V$ leads or lags $I$ and find the phase angle.",
           r"$\tan\varphi = \dfrac{X_L - X_C}{R} = \dfrac{0 - 80}{60}$, so $\varphi = -53^\circ$. The negative sign shows that the supply pd **lags** the current by $53^\circ$ (the circuit is capacitive)."),
        PQ(r"Explain why a circuit with a pure inductor connected to an AC supply has a zero mean power consumption, even though a current flows.",
           r"The current lags the pd by $90^\circ$, so the instantaneous power $VI$ is positive for a quarter-cycle (energy is stored in the magnetic field) and negative for the next quarter-cycle (energy is returned to the supply). Over a whole cycle the mean power is zero. Equivalently $\bar{P} = VI\cos 90^\circ = 0$."),
        TIP(r"Impedance and phasor questions are in Paper 2, and short conceptual items on reactance and phase are in Paper 1. Draw the phasor diagram before calculating; it prevents sign errors."),
        SUM(r"Resistor: $V$ in phase with $I$. Inductor: $V$ leads $I$ by $90^\circ$. Capacitor: $V$ lags $I$ by $90^\circ$ (CIVIL).",
            r"$X_L = \omega L$ (rises with $f$) and $X_C = 1/\omega C$ (falls with $f$); both in ohms and neither dissipates energy.",
            r"Series RLC: $Z = \sqrt{R^2 + (X_L - X_C)^2}$, $\tan\varphi = (X_L - X_C)/R$, $I = V/Z$.",
            r"Mean power $\bar{P} = I^2R = VI\cos\varphi$, with power factor $\cos\varphi = R/Z$."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 16. Resonance in series RLC
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Series RLC circuits and resonance",
    title="Resonance in a series RLC circuit",
    slug="phu1-rlc-resonance",
    minutes=23,
    summary="Find the resonant frequency f0 = 1/(2 pi sqrt(LC)), describe the current, impedance and phase at resonance, and use the Q factor and bandwidth to explain tuning and sharpness.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain resonance in a series RLC circuit and derive f0 = 1/(2 pi sqrt(LC))",
        "State the impedance, current and phase angle at resonance",
        "Define the Q factor and relate it to the sharpness of the resonance curve and the bandwidth",
        "Apply resonance to the tuning of a radio receiver",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Resonant frequency", "Impedance at resonance", "Q factor", "Bandwidth", "Tuning circuit"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Many radio stations broadcast at the same time, yet your radio plays only one. Turning the dial changes a capacitor in a circuit so that only the signal of the wanted station produces a large current. This selection by frequency is called **resonance**. You met it in mechanics as a driven oscillation. In a series RLC circuit, the energy swings between the magnetic field of the inductor and the electric field of the capacitor."),
        H("Resonant frequency"),
        T(r"In a series circuit, $Z = \sqrt{R^2 + (X_L - X_C)^2}$. At low frequency $X_C$ is large and $X_L$ small; at high frequency it is the other way round. At one frequency $X_L = X_C$. The reactances cancel, $Z$ is at its minimum value, and the current is at its maximum."),
        D(r"Resonance occurs when $X_L = X_C$. Then $\omega_0 L = \dfrac{1}{\omega_0 C}$, which gives", title="Resonant frequency", formula=r"\omega_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}},\qquad f_0 = \frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC}}"),
        KEY("At resonance", r"Take the supply pd to be $V$ (rms).", cards=[
            ["Impedance", r"$Z = R$, its minimum value."],
            ["Current", r"$I = V/R$, its maximum value."],
            ["Phase", r"$V$ and $I$ are in phase ($\varphi = 0$); power factor 1."],
            ["Component pds", r"$V_L$ and $V_C$ are equal and opposite and can each be much larger than $V$."]]),
        IMG("al-phu1-resonance-curve.svg", alt="Two curves of current against frequency for a series RLC circuit, both peaking at 50.3 hertz. The curve for 2 ohm reaches 1.0 ampere and is sharp; the curve for 10 ohm reaches 0.2 ampere and is broad.", caption="Figure 1. Resonance curves for L = 0.10 H and C = 100 microfarad with a 2.0 V supply. More resistance gives a lower and broader peak."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a resonant circuit", intro=r"A series circuit has $L = 0.10$ H, $C = 100\ \mu$F and $R = 2.0\ \Omega$, with a $2.0$ V rms supply of variable frequency. Find the resonant frequency, the current at resonance and the pd across the capacitor at resonance.", steps=[
            S("Resonant frequency", math=r"f_0 = \frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{(0.10)(100\times10^{-6})}} = \frac{1}{2\pi(3.16\times10^{-3})} = 50.3\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Current at resonance", math=r"I = \frac{V}{R} = \frac{2.0}{2.0} = 1.0\ \text{A}"),
            S("Reactance of $C$", math=r"X_C = \frac{1}{\omega_0C} = \frac{1}{(316.2)(100\times10^{-6})} = 31.6\ \Omega"),
            S("pd across $C$", math=r"V_C = IX_C = 31.6\ \text{V}"),
        ]),
        T(r"The pd across the capacitor (31.6 V) is about sixteen times the supply pd. This is **voltage magnification**, and it is safe only because the pds across $L$ and $C$ cancel each other. It is why high-voltage resonance can damage equipment."),
        H("The Q factor and bandwidth"),
        D(r"The **Q factor** (quality factor) of a series resonant circuit measures how sharp the resonance is:", title="Q factor", formula=r"Q = \frac{\omega_0L}{R} = \frac{1}{\omega_0CR} = \frac{f_0}{\Delta f}",
          note=r"$\Delta f$ is the **bandwidth**: the width of the resonance curve between the two frequencies at which the power is half of its maximum value, that is, where the current is $I_{\max}/\sqrt{2}$. At resonance $V_L = V_C = QV$."),
        T(r"A small resistance gives a large $Q$: a tall, narrow peak that is selective. A large resistance gives a small $Q$, a low, wide peak. For Example 1, $Q = \dfrac{\omega_0L}{R} = \dfrac{(316)(0.10)}{2.0} = 15.8$ and the bandwidth is $\Delta f = f_0/Q = 50.3/15.8 = 3.2$ Hz. If $R$ is $10\ \Omega$, $Q = 3.2$ and $\Delta f = 16$ Hz."),
        T(r"The power in the circuit is $I^2R$, so the half-power points are where $I = I_{\max}/\sqrt{2}$. The impedance there is $\sqrt{2}R$, which means $|X_L - X_C| = R$."),
        H("Tuning a radio"),
        T(r"An antenna picks up a mixture of signals of many frequencies, each inducing a tiny pd in the tuning circuit. The circuit gives a large current only for the signal whose frequency equals $f_0$. Changing the variable capacitor changes $f_0$ and selects a different station. The bandwidth must be narrow enough to reject neighbouring stations but wide enough to pass the sound information carried by the signal."),
        EX("Worked example 2: tuning a radio", intro=r"A tuning circuit has $L = 250\ \mu$H. What capacitance gives a resonant frequency of $600$ kHz? What range of frequency is covered when $C$ varies from $100$ pF to $500$ pF?", steps=[
            S("Rearrange", math=r"C = \frac{1}{(2\pi f_0)^2L} = \frac{1}{(2\pi \times 6.0\times10^{5})^2(250\times10^{-6})} = 2.8\times10^{-10}\ \text{F} = 281\ \text{pF}"),
            S("Frequency for $100$ pF", math=r"f = \frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{(250\times10^{-6})(100\times10^{-12})}} = 1.01\times10^{6}\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Frequency for $500$ pF", math=r"f = 4.5\times10^{5}\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Range", text="From about 450 kHz to 1.0 MHz."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: finding a component", intro=r"A $40\ \mu$F capacitor is to be used with an inductor to resonate at $50$ Hz. What inductance is required?", steps=[
            S("Rearrange the resonance formula for L", math=r"L = \frac{1}{(2\pi f_0)^2C} = \frac{1}{(2\pi \times 50)^2(40\times10^{-6})} = 0.25\ \text{H}"),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the square root or the factor 2 pi", r"$f_0 = 1/(2\pi\sqrt{LC})$. Using $1/\sqrt{LC}$ gives the angular frequency $\omega_0$, not $f_0$."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking resonance means zero impedance", r"At resonance $Z = R$, not zero. Only the **reactance** is zero."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up Q and bandwidth", r"A **higher** $Q$ means a **narrower** bandwidth ($\Delta f = f_0/Q$)."),
        QUIZ(r"At the resonant frequency of a series RLC circuit, which statement is correct?",
             [r"The current is a minimum", r"The impedance is equal to $R$ and the pd is in phase with the current", r"The pds across $L$ and $C$ are in phase", r"The supply pd leads the current by $90^\circ$"], 1,
             r"At resonance $X_L = X_C$, so $Z = R$, the current is a maximum and the supply pd is in phase with it.",
             hint=r"What happens to $X_L - X_C$?",
             feedback=[r"The current is a maximum at resonance.", "", r"The pds across $L$ and $C$ are in antiphase (opposite) and cancel.", r"A $90^\circ$ lead occurs for a pure inductor, not at resonance."]),
        QUIZ(r"The resistance of a series resonant circuit is increased. What happens to the Q factor and the bandwidth?",
             [r"$Q$ rises and the bandwidth narrows", r"$Q$ falls and the bandwidth widens", r"$Q$ falls and the bandwidth narrows", r"Neither changes"], 1,
             r"$Q = \omega_0L/R$ falls as $R$ rises, and $\Delta f = f_0/Q$ increases.",
             hint=r"$Q = \omega_0L/R$.",
             feedback=[r"This is what happens when $R$ is decreased.", "", r"A smaller $Q$ gives a wider bandwidth, not a narrower one.", r"$Q$ contains $R$, so it changes."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Show that the resonant frequency of a series LC circuit is $f_0 = \dfrac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC}}$.",
           r"Resonance occurs when $X_L = X_C$: $\omega_0L = \dfrac{1}{\omega_0C}$, so $\omega_0^2 = \dfrac{1}{LC}$ and $\omega_0 = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{LC}}$. Using $\omega_0 = 2\pi f_0$, $f_0 = \dfrac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC}}$."),
        PQ(r"A series circuit of $L = 25$ mH, $C = 1.0\ \mu$F and $R = 5.0\ \Omega$ is connected to a $10$ V rms supply of variable frequency. Find the resonant frequency, the current at resonance and the $Q$ factor.",
           r"$f_0 = \dfrac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{(25\times10^{-3})(1.0\times10^{-6})}} = 1.0\times10^{3}$ Hz. $I = V/R = 10/5.0 = 2.0$ A. $Q = \dfrac{\omega_0L}{R} = \dfrac{(6325)(0.025)}{5.0} = 32$."),
        PQ(r"Sketch the graph of current against frequency for a series RLC circuit for two values of the resistance, and explain how $R$ changes the shape.",
           r"Both curves rise to a peak at $f_0 = 1/2\pi\sqrt{LC}$ and then fall, with the same peak frequency. The curve for the smaller $R$ has a taller, narrower peak ($I_{\max} = V/R$ is larger and the Q is larger); the curve for the larger $R$ has a lower, broader peak. Resistance does not change $f_0$ but damps the response."),
        PQ(r"A radio is tuned to a station at $900$ kHz using a coil of $200\ \mu$H. Calculate the capacitance, and explain why the circuit rejects a station at $1000$ kHz.",
           r"$C = \dfrac{1}{(2\pi f_0)^2L} = \dfrac{1}{(2\pi\times9.0\times10^{5})^2(200\times10^{-6})} = 1.56\times10^{-10}$ F $= 156$ pF. At $1000$ kHz the reactances $X_L$ and $X_C$ are no longer equal, so $Z$ is much greater than $R$ and the current from that station is small compared with the current at $900$ kHz."),
        TIP(r"Resonance and Q factor are Paper 2 topics, often with a resonance curve to interpret. State clearly that at resonance $Z = R$ and the current is maximum."),
        SUM(r"Resonance: $X_L = X_C$, $f_0 = 1/(2\pi\sqrt{LC})$.",
            r"At resonance $Z = R$ (minimum), $I = V/R$ (maximum), $V$ and $I$ in phase, and $V_L = V_C = QV$ in opposite directions.",
            r"$Q = \omega_0L/R = f_0/\Delta f$: low resistance gives a high Q, a tall narrow peak.",
            r"Tuning circuits select a station by changing $C$ (or $L$) so that $f_0$ equals the station frequency."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 17. Stress, strain and Young's modulus
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3,
    topic="Stress, strain and Young's modulus",
    title="Stress, strain and Young's modulus",
    slug="phu1-stress-strain-youngs-modulus",
    minutes=23,
    summary="Define tensile stress and strain, use Hooke's law and Young's modulus, read a stress-strain graph for a ductile metal, and describe an experiment to measure Young's modulus of a wire.",
    objectives=[
        "Define stress, strain and Young's modulus and use E = stress/strain",
        "Relate Young's modulus to the force constant: k = EA/L",
        "Interpret a stress-strain graph: proportional limit, elastic limit, yield point, ultimate tensile stress and fracture",
        "Describe an experiment to find Young's modulus of a metal wire",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Stress", "Strain", "Young's modulus", "Hooke's law", "Elastic and plastic behaviour", "Ultimate tensile stress"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A crane on a building site in Douala lifts a load on a steel cable. Will the cable stretch? Will it snap? Engineers answer these questions with two quantities that do not depend on the size of the sample: **stress** and **strain**. The ratio of the two is a property of the material."),
        H("Stress and strain"),
        D(r"**Tensile stress** is the force applied per unit cross-sectional area. Unit: pascal (Pa), $1\ \text{Pa} = 1\ \text{N/m}^2$.", title="Stress", formula=r"\sigma = \frac{F}{A}"),
        D(r"**Tensile strain** is the extension per unit original length. It has no unit (it may be quoted as a percentage).", title="Strain", formula=r"\varepsilon = \frac{\Delta L}{L}"),
        T(r"For a wire loaded within its limits, the extension is proportional to the load. This is **Hooke's law**: $F = kx$, where $k$ is the force constant (stiffness) of the sample. It holds up to the **limit of proportionality**."),
        D(r"The **Young modulus** $E$ of a material is the ratio of tensile stress to tensile strain, within the limit of proportionality. Unit: Pa.", title="Young modulus", formula=r"E = \frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon} = \frac{F/A}{\Delta L/L} = \frac{FL}{A\,\Delta L}",
          note=r"$E$ is a property of the material: a thick short wire and a thin long wire of the same steel have the same $E$ but different $k$. Typical values (check your textbook): steel about $2\times10^{11}$ Pa, copper about $1.2\times10^{11}$ Pa."),
        T(r"Comparing $F = kx$ with $E = FL/(A\,\Delta L)$ gives the force constant of a wire: $k = \dfrac{EA}{L}$. A thicker wire is stiffer; a longer wire is less stiff."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a steel wire", intro=r"A steel wire of length $2.0$ m and diameter $0.80$ mm supports a load of $100$ N. Find the stress, the extension and the strain. Take $E = 2.0\times10^{11}$ Pa.", steps=[
            S("Cross-sectional area", math=r"A = \pi r^2 = \pi(0.40\times10^{-3})^2 = 5.03\times10^{-7}\ \text{m}^2"),
            S("Stress", math=r"\sigma = \frac{F}{A} = \frac{100}{5.03\times10^{-7}} = 1.99\times10^{8}\ \text{Pa}"),
            S("Strain", math=r"\varepsilon = \frac{\sigma}{E} = \frac{1.99\times10^{8}}{2.0\times10^{11}} = 9.9\times10^{-4}"),
            S("Extension", math=r"\Delta L = \varepsilon L = (9.9\times10^{-4})(2.0) = 2.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{m} = 2.0\ \text{mm}"),
        ]),
        H("The stress-strain graph"),
        IMG("al-phu1-stress-strain.svg", alt="Stress-strain graph for a ductile metal: a steep straight line to about 250 megapascals, a flat yield region, a rise to a maximum of 400 megapascals, then a fall to fracture at 330 megapascals.", caption="Figure 1. Schematic stress-strain graph of a ductile metal such as mild steel. The initial straight part is drawn steeper than to scale."),
        KEY("Features of the graph", "Read the graph from the origin outwards.", cards=[
            ["Limit of proportionality", "End of the straight line: stress is proportional to strain. The gradient is $E$."],
            ["Elastic limit", "Beyond this, the material does not return to its original length when unloaded."],
            ["Yield point", "The material stretches a lot for little extra stress: it becomes plastic."],
            ["Ultimate tensile stress", "The maximum stress the material can bear. The wire then 'necks' and breaks."]]),
        T(r"A **ductile** material (copper, mild steel) can be drawn into wire: it shows a large plastic region. A **brittle** material (glass, cast iron, ceramics) breaks with little or no plastic deformation. A **plastic** deformation is permanent. A **stiff** material has a large $E$ (steep line); a **strong** material has a large breaking stress. These words are not the same."),
        EX("Worked example 2: choosing a rod", intro=r"A steel rod must support a load of $20$ kN with a stress that does not exceed $150$ MPa. Find the minimum cross-sectional area, and the diameter of a circular rod.", steps=[
            S("Area", math=r"A = \frac{F}{\sigma} = \frac{20\times10^{3}}{150\times10^{6}} = 1.33\times10^{-4}\ \text{m}^2 = 133\ \text{mm}^2"),
            S("Diameter", math=r"d = 2\sqrt{\frac{A}{\pi}} = 2\sqrt{\frac{1.33\times10^{-4}}{\pi}} = 1.30\times10^{-2}\ \text{m} = 13\ \text{mm}"),
        ]),
        H("Measuring Young's modulus of a wire"),
        T(r"Hang a long thin test wire from a firm support, with a second 'reference' wire beside it to cancel changes in temperature and sagging of the support. Add masses to the test wire in steps and measure the extension with a vernier scale or a micrometer. Measure the **diameter** with a micrometer at several points, and the **length** from the clamp to the marker with a metre rule. Plot **force against extension**."),
        T(r"The graph is a straight line through the origin up to the limit of proportionality. Its gradient is $\dfrac{F}{\Delta L} = \dfrac{EA}{L}$, so $E = \text{gradient}\times\dfrac{L}{A}$. The wire should be long (about 2 m) so that the extension is large enough to measure, and thin so that the stress is large for small loads. The largest source of error is the measurement of the diameter, because it appears squared in $A$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: from the gradient", intro=r"For a wire of length $2.0$ m and area $5.03\times10^{-7}$ m², the gradient of the force-extension graph is $5.0\times10^{4}$ N/m. Find $E$.", steps=[
            S("Use the gradient", math=r"E = \text{gradient}\times\frac{L}{A} = (5.0\times10^{4})\frac{2.0}{5.03\times10^{-7}} = 2.0\times10^{11}\ \text{Pa}"),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the diameter in place of the radius", r"$A = \pi r^2 = \pi d^2/4$. Halve the diameter before squaring."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing stiff and strong", r"Stiffness is the Young modulus (resistance to stretching). Strength is the stress at which the material breaks."),
        MISTAKE("Saying the elastic limit and the limit of proportionality are always the same", r"They are close for many metals but not identical. Rubber, for example, is elastic over a huge range but never obeys Hooke's law."),
        QUIZ(r"A wire is replaced by one of the same material and length but twice the diameter. The same load is applied. How does the extension change?",
             [r"It doubles", r"It halves", r"It becomes one quarter", r"It is unchanged"], 2,
             r"$\Delta L = FL/(AE)$, and $A$ is proportional to $d^2$. Doubling $d$ multiplies $A$ by 4, so the extension is divided by 4.",
             hint=r"The area depends on the square of the diameter.",
             feedback=[r"A thicker wire stretches less.", r"This takes account of $d$ but not of the square.", "", r"The extension depends on the area."]),
        QUIZ(r"Which statement about the Young modulus is correct?",
             [r"It depends on the length of the wire", r"It depends on the cross-sectional area of the wire", r"It is a property of the material", r"It is the force per unit extension"], 2,
             r"$E$ is stress divided by strain: both are defined per unit area and per unit length, so $E$ depends only on the material.",
             hint=r"Which quantities does the definition remove?",
             feedback=[r"Strain already divides by the original length.", r"Stress already divides by the area.", "", r"Force per unit extension is the force constant $k = EA/L$, which depends on the sample."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Define stress, strain and the Young modulus. State the condition under which the Young modulus is defined.",
           r"Stress is force per unit cross-sectional area; strain is extension per unit original length; the Young modulus is the ratio stress/strain. It is defined only up to the limit of proportionality, where stress is proportional to strain."),
        PQ(r"A copper wire of length $3.0$ m and cross-sectional area $1.5\times10^{-6}$ m² is stretched by a force of $60$ N. The Young modulus of copper is $1.2\times10^{11}$ Pa. Find the extension.",
           r"$\Delta L = \dfrac{FL}{AE} = \dfrac{(60)(3.0)}{(1.5\times10^{-6})(1.2\times10^{11})} = 1.0\times10^{-3}$ m $= 1.0$ mm."),
        PQ(r"Describe how you would use the graph from a Young modulus experiment to find $E$, and name one way to reduce the uncertainty in the result.",
           r"Plot force against extension; the gradient is $EA/L$, so $E = \text{gradient}\times L/A$, where $L$ is the length of wire measured with a metre rule and $A = \pi d^2/4$ with $d$ measured by a micrometer. To reduce uncertainty: measure the diameter at several places and in two perpendicular directions and use the mean, use a long thin wire so that the extension is large, and use the gradient of the line of best fit rather than single readings."),
        PQ(r"Sketch the stress-strain graph for a ductile metal and label the limit of proportionality, the yield point and the ultimate tensile stress. State what happens to the wire if it is unloaded after the yield point.",
           r"The graph is a straight line from the origin to the limit of proportionality (gradient $E$), then a curve through the elastic limit to the yield point, where strain grows quickly with little change in stress. It then rises to a maximum, the ultimate tensile stress, and falls as the wire necks until it breaks. If unloaded after yield, the wire does not return to its original length: it keeps a permanent (plastic) extension."),
        TIP(r"Stress-strain graphs, the Young modulus experiment and its uncertainties appear in Paper 1 and Paper 2 and as practical skills in Paper 3. Always convert diameters to metres and areas to m²."),
        SUM(r"Stress $\sigma = F/A$ (Pa); strain $\varepsilon = \Delta L/L$; Young modulus $E = \sigma/\varepsilon$ within the limit of proportionality.",
            r"Force constant of a wire: $k = EA/L$; $E$ is a material property, $k$ depends on the sample.",
            r"Stress-strain graph: proportional region (gradient $E$), elastic limit, yield, ultimate tensile stress, fracture; ductile and brittle materials differ.",
            r"Measure $E$ from the gradient of a force-extension graph: $E = \text{gradient}\times L/A$."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 18. Strain energy
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3,
    topic="Strain energy",
    title="Strain energy and elastic behaviour",
    slug="phu1-strain-energy",
    minutes=21,
    summary="Find the elastic strain energy as the area under a force-extension graph, E = 1/2 F x = 1/2 k x^2, the energy per unit volume 1/2 stress x strain, and interpret loading and unloading curves.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate strain energy from the area under a force-extension graph",
        "Use E = 1/2 F x, 1/2 k x^2 and the energy per unit volume 1/2 sigma epsilon = sigma^2/2E",
        "Explain the loading and unloading curves of a metal and of rubber, including hysteresis",
        "Solve energy problems involving stretched wires and cords",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Elastic strain energy", "Area under force-extension graph", "Energy per unit volume", "Hysteresis", "Elastic and plastic deformation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A catapult stores energy as you pull back the rubber, and releases it to launch a stone. A bow, a trampoline and a bungee cord all work in the same way. When a material is stretched, work is done on it. If the deformation is **elastic**, that work is stored in the material as **elastic strain energy** and can be recovered."),
        H("Energy from the force-extension graph"),
        T(r"As a wire is stretched, the force you must apply grows from zero to $F$. The work done in a small extension $\Delta x$ is $F\,\Delta x$, the area of a thin strip under the graph. So the total work done is the **area under the force-extension graph**."),
        IMG("al-phu1-strain-energy.svg", alt="Left: a force-extension graph that is a straight line through the origin with the triangle underneath shaded, labelled half F x. Right: a loading curve and a lower unloading curve for rubber, which form a loop.", caption="Figure 1. Energy as the area under a force-extension graph. For a straight line the area is a triangle; for rubber, the area of the loop is energy lost as heat."),
        D(r"For a material that obeys Hooke's law, $F = kx$, the area under the line is a triangle:", title="Elastic strain energy", formula=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}Fx = \tfrac{1}{2}kx^2 = \frac{F^2}{2k}",
          note=r"$x$ is the extension. The energy is stored only if the limit of proportionality (and the elastic limit) are not exceeded. The factor $\tfrac{1}{2}$ comes from the average force, $F/2$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a steel wire", intro=r"A steel wire of length $2.0$ m, area $5.03\times10^{-7}$ m², $E = 2.0\times10^{11}$ Pa, carries $100$ N, so its extension is $2.0$ mm. Find the strain energy stored.", steps=[
            S("Energy", math=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}Fx = \tfrac{1}{2}(100)(1.99\times10^{-3}) = 0.10\ \text{J}"),
            S("Compare with $mgh$", text="If the load fell through 2.0 mm it would lose 100 N times 0.002 m = 0.20 J, twice as much. Half the lost gravitational energy is stored in the wire; the rest goes to kinetic energy and then heat as the load oscillates and settles."),
        ]),
        H("Energy per unit volume"),
        T(r"Divide the energy by the volume of the wire, $V = AL$:"),
        F(r"\frac{E}{V} = \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}Fx}{AL} = \tfrac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{F}{A}\cdot\frac{x}{L} = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\sigma\varepsilon = \frac{\sigma^2}{2E}"),
        D(r"The elastic strain energy stored per unit volume is half the product of stress and strain, which is the area under the stress-strain graph:", title="Energy density", formula=r"\frac{E}{V} = \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma\varepsilon = \frac{\sigma^2}{2E}",
          note="Unit: J/m$^3$. Use this to compare materials without needing their sizes."),
        EX("Worked example 2: energy density", intro=r"For the wire in Example 1, find the energy per unit volume in two ways.", steps=[
            S("Volume", math=r"V = AL = (5.03\times10^{-7})(2.0) = 1.01\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}^3"),
            S("From the energy", math=r"\frac{E}{V} = \frac{0.0995}{1.01\times10^{-6}} = 9.9\times10^{4}\ \text{J/m}^3"),
            S("From stress and strain", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}\sigma\varepsilon = \tfrac{1}{2}(1.99\times10^{8})(9.95\times10^{-4}) = 9.9\times10^{4}\ \text{J/m}^3"),
        ]),
        H("Loading and unloading"),
        T(r"For a metal below the elastic limit, the unloading line follows the loading line back to the origin. All the energy stored is returned. Beyond the elastic limit (plastic deformation) the unloading line is parallel to the loading line but ends at a non-zero extension; the area between the lines is energy converted to heat in permanently deforming the metal."),
        T(r"**Rubber** is different. It is elastic over a large range, but the force does not follow Hooke's law, and the unloading curve lies **below** the loading curve. The area of the loop between them is the energy converted to heat in one cycle. This effect is called **hysteresis**. It is why a rubber band feels warm after repeated stretching, and why rubber is used in vehicle engine mounts, which absorb vibration energy."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a catapult", intro=r"A rubber cord (treated as obeying Hooke's law) has force constant $250$ N/m and is pulled back by $0.40$ m. A $50$ g stone is released. Find the energy stored and the maximum speed of the stone, ignoring losses.", steps=[
            S("Stored energy", math=r"E = \tfrac{1}{2}kx^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(250)(0.40)^2 = 20\ \text{J}"),
            S("All becomes kinetic energy", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 = 20 \;\Rightarrow\; v = \sqrt{\frac{2(20)}{0.050}} = 28\ \text{m/s}"),
            S("In practice", text="The speed is lower because some energy is lost to heat in the rubber (hysteresis) and to the motion of the cord itself."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using Fx without the half", r"The force rises from zero to $F$, so the work is $\tfrac{1}{2}Fx$. Using $Fx$ doubles the energy."),
        MISTAKE("Using the total extension when the wire is below its natural length", r"Energy $\tfrac{1}{2}kx^2$ depends on the **extension** from the natural length, not on the total length."),
        MISTAKE("Assuming all the work done is recoverable", r"Only the elastic part is returned. For plastic deformation and for rubber hysteresis some energy becomes heat."),
        QUIZ(r"A spring is stretched by $x$ and stores energy $E$. It is stretched to $3x$. How much energy does it store?",
             [r"$3E$", r"$6E$", r"$9E$", r"$E/3$"], 2,
             r"$E = \tfrac{1}{2}kx^2 \propto x^2$, so tripling $x$ multiplies $E$ by 9.",
             hint=r"Energy depends on $x^2$.",
             feedback=[r"This treats $E$ as proportional to $x$.", r"No factor 6 appears in $\tfrac{1}{2}kx^2$.", "", r"The stored energy increases with extension."]),
        QUIZ(r"The loading and unloading curves of a rubber band form a loop. What does the area of the loop represent?",
             [r"The energy stored in the band", r"The work done by the band on the surroundings", r"The energy converted to heat in the cycle", r"The Young modulus"], 2,
             r"Work done stretching is the area under the loading curve; the energy returned is the area under the unloading curve. The difference, the loop area, is dissipated as heat.",
             hint=r"Compare the work put in with the work got back.",
             feedback=[r"The stored energy is the area under the loading curve.", r"The loop area is the difference between the work done on the band and the work it does.", "", r"The Young modulus is a gradient, not an area."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Show that the elastic energy stored in a wire that obeys Hooke's law is $\tfrac{1}{2}Fx$, where $F$ is the final force and $x$ the extension.",
           r"The work done in a small extension is $F\,dx$, the area of a thin strip under the force-extension graph. The total work is the area under the graph from $0$ to $x$. For $F = kx$ this is a triangle of base $x$ and height $F$: area $= \tfrac{1}{2}Fx$. This is stored as elastic energy."),
        PQ(r"A spring of force constant $400$ N/m is stretched from an extension of $0.050$ m to $0.150$ m. Find the extra energy stored.",
           r"$E_2 - E_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}k(x_2^2 - x_1^2) = \tfrac{1}{2}(400)(0.150^2 - 0.050^2) = 200(0.0225 - 0.0025) = 4.0$ J."),
        PQ(r"A wire has a stress of $1.5\times10^{8}$ Pa and Young modulus $2.0\times10^{11}$ Pa. Find the strain energy stored per unit volume, and the total energy in $5.0\times10^{-6}$ m³ of the wire.",
           r"Energy per unit volume $= \dfrac{\sigma^2}{2E} = \dfrac{(1.5\times10^{8})^2}{2(2.0\times10^{11})} = 5.6\times10^{4}$ J/m³. Total $= (5.6\times10^{4})(5.0\times10^{-6}) = 0.28$ J."),
        PQ(r"Explain why a rubber band becomes warm when it is stretched and released many times.",
           r"The loading and unloading curves of rubber do not coincide: the unloading curve lies below the loading curve (hysteresis). The work done in stretching is greater than the energy recovered in the release, and the difference, the area of the loop, is converted to internal energy (heat) in the rubber each cycle."),
        TIP(r"Questions often ask you to find energy from the area under a force-extension graph, including graphs that are not straight lines. Count squares or use trapezia if the graph is not linear."),
        SUM(r"Strain energy is the area under the force-extension graph: $E = \tfrac{1}{2}Fx = \tfrac{1}{2}kx^2$ for Hooke's law.",
            r"Energy per unit volume $= \tfrac{1}{2}\sigma\varepsilon = \sigma^2/2E$ (area under the stress-strain graph).",
            r"Below the elastic limit all the energy is recovered; beyond it, or in rubber (hysteresis), some becomes heat.",
            r"The loop area between loading and unloading curves is energy dissipated per cycle."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 19. Surface tension and capillarity
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3,
    topic="Surface tension and capillarity",
    title="Surface tension, excess pressure and capillary rise",
    slug="phu1-surface-tension-capillarity",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Define surface tension as force per unit length and energy per unit area, find the excess pressure in drops and bubbles, and derive the rise of liquid in a capillary tube.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain surface tension in terms of molecular forces and define it as force per unit length and as surface energy per unit area",
        "Calculate forces on a film and the energy needed to enlarge a surface",
        "Derive and use the excess pressure 2 gamma/r (drop) and 4 gamma/r (soap bubble)",
        "Derive and use h = 2 gamma cos(theta)/(rho g r) for capillary rise and depression",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Surface tension", "Surface energy", "Excess pressure", "Angle of contact", "Capillary rise"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A small insect walks on the surface of a pond. Water climbs up the fibres of a cloth or the stem of a plant. Rain forms round drops rather than flat sheets. All of these come from the **surface tension** of liquids: the surface behaves like a stretched elastic skin."),
        H("Surface tension"),
        T(r"A molecule deep inside a liquid is pulled equally in all directions by its neighbours. A molecule at the surface has neighbours only below and beside it, so it is pulled inwards. To make the surface bigger, molecules must be brought from inside to the surface, and this needs work. The liquid therefore tries to have the **smallest possible surface area**, which is why drops are spherical."),
        D(r"The **surface tension** $\gamma$ of a liquid is the force acting along the surface, at right angles to a line of unit length drawn in the surface. Unit: N/m.", title="Surface tension (force definition)", formula=r"\gamma = \frac{F}{L}"),
        D(r"Equivalently, the surface tension is the work done (energy supplied) to increase the surface area by one unit, at constant temperature. Unit: J/m$^2$ (the same as N/m).", title="Surface tension (energy definition)", formula=r"\gamma = \frac{\Delta W}{\Delta A}",
          note=r"For water at about $20^\circ$C, $\gamma \approx 0.072$ N/m. It decreases as the temperature rises and when soap or detergent is dissolved (soapy water: about $0.025$ N/m). Values vary between sources: check your textbook."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a wire on a soap film", intro=r"A rectangular wire frame has a sliding wire of length $5.0$ cm. A soap film ($\gamma = 0.025$ N/m) is stretched across the frame. Find the force the film exerts on the wire, and the work done in pulling the wire $2.0$ cm to enlarge the film.", steps=[
            S("A film has **two** surfaces, so the length of edge is doubled", math=r"F = 2\gamma L = 2(0.025)(0.050) = 2.5\times10^{-3}\ \text{N}"),
            S("Work done", math=r"W = Fx = (2.5\times10^{-3})(0.020) = 5.0\times10^{-5}\ \text{J}"),
            S("Check using the energy definition", math=r"\Delta A = 2(0.050)(0.020) = 2.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{m}^2,\quad W = \gamma\Delta A = 0.025(2.0\times10^{-3}) = 5.0\times10^{-5}\ \text{J}"),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: blowing a bubble", intro=r"How much work is needed to blow a soap bubble of radius $2.0$ cm, taking $\gamma = 0.025$ N/m? (Ignore the work against atmospheric pressure.)", steps=[
            S("A bubble has two surfaces (inside and outside)", math=r"\Delta A = 2 \times 4\pi r^2 = 2 \times 4\pi(0.020)^2 = 1.0\times10^{-2}\ \text{m}^2"),
            S("Work", math=r"W = \gamma\Delta A = (0.025)(1.0\times10^{-2}) = 2.5\times10^{-4}\ \text{J}"),
        ]),
        H("Excess pressure in drops and bubbles"),
        T(r"The surface tension of a curved surface pulls inwards, so the pressure inside a drop or bubble is higher than the pressure outside. To find the **excess pressure** $\Delta p$ in a spherical drop of radius $r$, imagine that the drop grows by a small amount $\Delta r$. The work done by the excess pressure is $\Delta p \times 4\pi r^2\,\Delta r$. It increases the surface energy by $\gamma\times\Delta(4\pi r^2) = \gamma\,8\pi r\,\Delta r$. Equating them gives"),
        D(r"Excess pressure inside a **liquid drop** (one surface), and inside a **soap bubble** (two surfaces):", title="Excess pressure", formula=r"\Delta p = \frac{2\gamma}{r}\ \text{(drop)},\qquad \Delta p = \frac{4\gamma}{r}\ \text{(soap bubble)}",
          note=r"Smaller drops and bubbles have **greater** excess pressure. Two bubbles of different sizes joined by a tube: air flows from the smaller (higher pressure) into the larger one."),
        EX("Worked example 3: pressure in a drop and bubble", intro=r"Find the excess pressure in a water drop of radius $0.50$ mm ($\gamma = 0.072$ N/m) and in a soap bubble of radius $1.0$ cm ($\gamma = 0.025$ N/m).", steps=[
            S("Drop", math=r"\Delta p = \frac{2\gamma}{r} = \frac{2(0.072)}{0.50\times10^{-3}} = 288\ \text{Pa}"),
            S("Bubble", math=r"\Delta p = \frac{4\gamma}{r} = \frac{4(0.025)}{1.0\times10^{-2}} = 10\ \text{Pa}"),
        ]),
        H("Angle of contact and capillary rise"),
        T(r"Where a liquid meets a solid, its surface makes an **angle of contact** $\theta$, measured through the liquid. Water on clean glass has $\theta$ close to $0^\circ$: it **wets** the glass and its surface curves up at the wall (a concave meniscus). Mercury on glass has $\theta \approx 140^\circ$: it does not wet the glass, and its surface bulges up (a convex meniscus)."),
        IMG("al-phu1-capillary.svg", alt="Two narrow tubes dipped in liquids. In water the level inside the tube is higher than outside with a concave meniscus; in mercury it is lower than outside with a convex meniscus.", caption="Figure 1. Capillary rise of water and capillary depression of mercury in a narrow glass tube."),
        T(r"In a narrow tube of radius $r$, the surface tension acts along the circumference ($2\pi r$) at the angle $\theta$ to the wall, giving an upward force $2\pi r\gamma\cos\theta$. It supports the weight of the raised column of liquid, $\pi r^2h\rho g$:"),
        F(r"2\pi r\gamma\cos\theta = \pi r^2h\rho g \;\Rightarrow\; h = \frac{2\gamma\cos\theta}{\rho g r}"),
        D(r"The height $h$ of the liquid in a capillary tube above (or, if negative, below) the outside level is", title="Capillary rise", formula=r"h = \frac{2\gamma\cos\theta}{\rho g r}",
          note=r"Narrower tubes give a larger $h$. If $\theta > 90^\circ$, $\cos\theta$ is negative and the liquid is depressed. This ignores the small volume of liquid in the meniscus."),
        T(r"Capillary action draws water up through soil and the fine vessels of plants, and soaks into a towel. Dry soil with fine pores draws water up further than coarse sand."),
        EX("Worked example 4: capillary rise and depression", intro=r"Find the rise of water in a glass tube of radius $0.50$ mm ($\theta = 0^\circ$, $\gamma = 0.072$ N/m, $\rho = 1000$ kg/m³) and the change of level of mercury in a tube of radius $1.0$ mm ($\theta = 140^\circ$, $\gamma = 0.465$ N/m, $\rho = 13\,600$ kg/m³). Take $g = 10$ m/s².", steps=[
            S("Water", math=r"h = \frac{2(0.072)(1)}{(1000)(10)(0.50\times10^{-3})} = 2.88\times10^{-2}\ \text{m} = 2.9\ \text{cm (rise)}"),
            S("Mercury", math=r"h = \frac{2(0.465)\cos 140^{\circ}}{(13\,600)(10)(1.0\times10^{-3})} = \frac{2(0.465)(-0.766)}{136} = -5.2\times10^{-3}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Meaning of the sign", text="Mercury is depressed by about 5 mm below the outside level."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the second surface of a film or bubble", r"A soap film and a soap bubble have two surfaces, so the force, energy and excess pressure use $2\gamma$ and $4\gamma$ respectively, not $\gamma$ and $2\gamma$."),
        MISTAKE("Using diameter in the capillary formula", r"The radius $r$ of the **tube** appears in $h = 2\gamma\cos\theta/\rho gr$. Halve the diameter first."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking surface tension is a force between two molecules", r"It is a force **per unit length** along the surface, or an energy per unit area. It is a property of the whole surface."),
        QUIZ(r"The radius of a capillary tube is halved. How does the capillary rise of water change?",
             [r"It halves", r"It doubles", r"It quadruples", r"It is unchanged"], 1,
             r"$h \propto 1/r$, so halving $r$ doubles $h$.",
             hint=r"Where does $r$ appear in $h = 2\gamma\cos\theta/\rho gr$?",
             feedback=[r"A narrower tube gives a higher rise.", "", r"$h$ is inversely proportional to $r$, not $r^2$.", r"The rise depends on the radius."]),
        QUIZ(r"Two soap bubbles, one large and one small, are joined by a tube. What happens?",
             [r"Air flows from the large bubble into the small one", r"Air flows from the small bubble into the large one", r"Nothing: the pressures are equal", r"Both burst"], 1,
             r"The excess pressure is $4\gamma/r$, which is greater in the small bubble. Air flows to the lower pressure: the large bubble grows.",
             hint=r"Which bubble has the higher pressure?",
             feedback=[r"This would be right if the larger bubble had the higher pressure.", "", r"The pressures differ because the excess pressure depends on $1/r$.", r"There is no reason for them to burst."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Define surface tension in terms of force and in terms of energy, and show that the two definitions are equivalent.",
           r"Force definition: $\gamma = F/L$, the force per unit length at right angles to a line in the surface. Energy definition: $\gamma = \Delta W/\Delta A$. For a film on a frame, the surface tension force on a sliding wire of length $L$ (one surface) is $F = \gamma L$. If the wire moves a distance $x$ the work done is $Fx = \gamma Lx = \gamma\Delta A$, where $\Delta A = Lx$ is the area created. So $\gamma = \Delta W/\Delta A$."),
        PQ(r"Show that the excess pressure inside a spherical liquid drop of radius $r$ is $2\gamma/r$.",
           r"Let the radius grow by $\Delta r$. Work done by excess pressure $\Delta p$ on the surface: $\Delta p \times 4\pi r^2\Delta r$. Increase in surface energy: $\gamma\times 8\pi r\Delta r$. Equating: $\Delta p\,4\pi r^2 = 8\pi r\gamma$, so $\Delta p = 2\gamma/r$."),
        PQ(r"Calculate the height to which water rises in a glass capillary tube of internal diameter $0.60$ mm. ($\gamma = 0.072$ N/m, $\theta = 0^\circ$, $\rho = 1000$ kg/m³, $g = 10$ m/s².)",
           r"$r = 0.30$ mm. $h = \dfrac{2\gamma\cos\theta}{\rho gr} = \dfrac{2(0.072)}{(1000)(10)(0.30\times10^{-3})} = 4.8\times10^{-2}$ m $= 4.8$ cm."),
        PQ(r"A soap bubble of radius $1.5$ cm is blown with soap solution of surface tension $0.025$ N/m. Find the excess pressure inside it and the energy stored in its surfaces.",
           r"$\Delta p = \dfrac{4\gamma}{r} = \dfrac{4(0.025)}{0.015} = 6.7$ Pa. Energy $= \gamma\times 2\times 4\pi r^2 = (0.025)(2)(4\pi)(0.015)^2 = 1.4\times10^{-4}$ J."),
        TIP(r"Surface tension appears in Paper 1 (definitions, ratios) and Paper 2 (derivations of excess pressure and capillary rise, calculations). In Paper 3, you may use a capillary tube to measure $\gamma$ with a travelling microscope."),
        SUM(r"Surface tension $\gamma$ = force per unit length = energy per unit area (N/m = J/m²).",
            r"Excess pressure: drop $2\gamma/r$; soap bubble $4\gamma/r$ (two surfaces).",
            r"Capillary rise: $h = 2\gamma\cos\theta/\rho gr$; mercury, with $\theta > 90^\circ$, is depressed.",
            r"Films and bubbles have two surfaces: remember the factor 2."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 20. Viscosity, Stokes' law, Poiseuille
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3,
    topic="Viscosity, Stokes' law and Poiseuille's formula",
    title="Viscosity, Stokes' law and Poiseuille's formula",
    slug="phu1-viscosity-stokes-poiseuille",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Define the coefficient of viscosity, use Stokes' law for the drag on a small sphere and its terminal velocity, and use Poiseuille's formula for the flow of a viscous liquid through a narrow tube.",
    objectives=[
        "Define viscosity and the coefficient of viscosity and use F = eta A (dv/dx)",
        "Use Stokes' law F = 6 pi eta r v and find the terminal velocity of a sphere in a fluid",
        "Use Poiseuille's formula for volume flow rate through a tube and explain the r^4 dependence",
        "State when laminar flow is expected and why viscosity depends on temperature",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Viscosity", "Coefficient of viscosity", "Laminar flow", "Stokes' law", "Terminal velocity", "Poiseuille's formula"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Palm oil poured from a bottle flows more slowly than water, and honey more slowly than either. A fluid's resistance to flow is called **viscosity**. It is important in lubricating engines, in the flow of blood and in the settling of particles in water. In this lesson we first define it, then see how it limits the speed of a sphere falling through a fluid, and finally how it controls flow in a pipe."),
        H("Laminar flow and viscosity"),
        T(r"In **laminar** (streamline) flow the fluid moves in smooth layers. The layer touching a solid wall is at rest, and the speed increases layer by layer to the middle of the stream. Layers moving at different speeds drag on each other: the faster layer is slowed and the slower layer is dragged along. This internal friction is **viscosity**."),
        T(r"Imagine a liquid between a fixed plate and a parallel plate of area $A$, a distance $d$ away, moving at speed $v$. The velocity changes uniformly across the gap, with **velocity gradient** $v/d$. The force needed to keep the top plate moving is proportional to $A$ and to the gradient:"),
        D(r"The **coefficient of viscosity** $\eta$ of a fluid is defined by $F = \eta A\,\dfrac{dv}{dx}$, where $F$ is the tangential force between layers of area $A$ and $dv/dx$ is the velocity gradient at right angles to the flow. Unit: Pa s (= N s m$^{-2}$ = kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-1}$).", title="Coefficient of viscosity", formula=r"F = \eta A\frac{dv}{dx}",
          note=r"Typical values at about $20^\circ$C: water $1.0\times10^{-3}$ Pa s; air $1.8\times10^{-5}$ Pa s; glycerol about $1.4$ Pa s. The viscosity of liquids **decreases** as temperature rises; that of gases **increases**. (Check your textbook for values.)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: a sliding plate", intro=r"A plate of area $0.020$ m² is pulled at $0.50$ m/s over a fixed surface, with a film of oil $2.0$ mm thick between them. The oil has $\eta = 0.10$ Pa s. Find the force needed.", steps=[
            S("Velocity gradient", math=r"\frac{dv}{dx} = \frac{0.50}{2.0\times10^{-3}} = 250\ \text{s}^{-1}"),
            S("Force", math=r"F = \eta A\frac{dv}{dx} = (0.10)(0.020)(250) = 0.50\ \text{N}"),
        ]),
        H("Stokes' law and terminal velocity"),
        T(r"A small sphere moving slowly through a viscous fluid, with laminar flow round it, experiences a viscous drag force. George Stokes showed that for a sphere of radius $r$ moving at speed $v$:"),
        D(r"The viscous drag on a sphere moving slowly through a fluid is", title="Stokes' law", formula=r"F = 6\pi\eta r v",
          note=r"Valid for small spheres at low speed, when the flow is laminar. The drag is proportional to the **speed**, not its square."),
        T(r"When a sphere is released in a fluid, three forces act on it: the weight $mg$ downwards, the **upthrust** $U$ upwards (the weight of fluid displaced), and the viscous drag $6\pi\eta rv$ upwards. As $v$ increases, the drag increases until the forces balance. The sphere then falls at constant speed, the **terminal velocity** $v_t$."),
        F(r"6\pi\eta r v_t = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3(\rho_s - \rho_f)g \;\Rightarrow\; v_t = \frac{2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g}{9\eta}"),
        T(r"Here $\rho_s$ is the density of the sphere and $\rho_f$ the density of the fluid. Note that $v_t \propto r^2$: a sphere twice as wide falls four times as fast. This is why fine mist and dust settle very slowly, while larger drops fall faster."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a steel ball in glycerol", intro=r"A steel ball of radius $2.0$ mm ($\rho = 7800$ kg/m³) falls in glycerol ($\rho = 1260$ kg/m³, $\eta = 1.4$ Pa s). Find its terminal velocity. Take $g = 10$ m/s².", steps=[
            S("Formula", math=r"v_t = \frac{2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g}{9\eta}"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"v_t = \frac{2(2.0\times10^{-3})^2(7800 - 1260)(10)}{9(1.4)}"),
            S("Evaluate", math=r"v_t = \frac{2(4.0\times10^{-6})(6540)(10)}{12.6} = 4.2\times10^{-2}\ \text{m/s}"),
            S("Check the condition for Stokes' law", text=r"The Reynolds number is about $\rho v d/\eta = (1260)(0.042)(0.004)/1.4 = 0.15$, well below 1, so the flow is laminar and Stokes' law applies."),
        ]),
        T(r"**Measuring viscosity.** Time a small steel ball falling a measured distance between two marks in the liquid, after it has reached terminal velocity. Then $\eta = \dfrac{2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g}{9v_t}$. Use a wide tube so the walls do not affect the result."),
        H("Poiseuille's formula"),
        T(r"The volume flow rate $Q$ (volume per second) of a viscous liquid in laminar flow through a long, narrow, horizontal tube depends on the pressure difference $\Delta p$ across its ends, the radius $r$, the length $L$ and the viscosity $\eta$:"),
        D(r"The volume of liquid flowing per second through a tube of radius $r$ and length $L$ with a pressure difference $\Delta p$ is", title="Poiseuille's formula", formula=r"Q = \frac{\pi r^4\,\Delta p}{8\eta L}",
          note=r"The speed across the tube is greatest at the centre (parabolic profile) and zero at the wall. The flow rate is proportional to the **fourth power** of the radius."),
        T(r"The $r^4$ dependence is important. If the radius of an artery is halved by deposits, then for the same pressure difference the flow falls to $\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^4 = \tfrac{1}{16}$ of its value. The heart must raise the pressure a lot to keep the blood flowing."),
        EX("Worked example 3: flow through a pipe", intro=r"Oil of viscosity $0.10$ Pa s flows through a tube of radius $1.0$ mm and length $0.50$ m with a pressure difference of $2.0$ kPa. Find the flow rate, and the flow rate if the radius is doubled.", steps=[
            S("Flow rate", math=r"Q = \frac{\pi r^4\Delta p}{8\eta L} = \frac{\pi(1.0\times10^{-3})^4(2000)}{8(0.10)(0.50)} = 1.57\times10^{-8}\ \text{m}^3/\text{s}"),
            S("Doubling the radius multiplies $Q$ by $2^4$", math=r"Q' = 16Q = 2.5\times10^{-7}\ \text{m}^3/\text{s}"),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the upthrust in the terminal velocity", r"The net downward force is the weight **minus** the upthrust. In a dense fluid such as glycerol this matters a lot. Use $\rho_s - \rho_f$."),
        MISTAKE("Using the diameter in Stokes' law or Poiseuille's formula", r"Both use the **radius**. Halve the diameter first."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking viscous drag is the same as air resistance at high speed", r"Stokes' law gives a drag proportional to $v$ for slow, laminar flow. At high speed, when the flow becomes turbulent, the drag grows with $v^2$ and the law fails."),
        QUIZ(r"Two steel spheres fall through the same oil. Sphere B has twice the radius of sphere A. What is the ratio of their terminal velocities, $v_B/v_A$?",
             [r"2", r"4", r"8", r"$\tfrac{1}{2}$"], 1,
             r"$v_t \propto r^2$, so doubling $r$ multiplies $v_t$ by 4.",
             hint=r"$v_t = 2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g/9\eta$.",
             feedback=[r"The terminal velocity depends on $r^2$, not $r$.", "", r"There is no cubic dependence, since the drag depends on $r$ while the weight depends on $r^3$.", r"A larger sphere falls faster."]),
        QUIZ(r"The radius of a tube carrying a viscous liquid in laminar flow is increased by $20\%$ and nothing else changes. By what factor does the flow rate increase?",
             [r"1.2", r"1.44", r"2.07", r"1.73"], 2,
             r"$Q \propto r^4$, so $Q$ increases by $1.2^4 = 2.07$.",
             hint=r"$Q \propto r^4$.",
             feedback=[r"This treats $Q$ as proportional to $r$.", r"This is $1.2^2$; the power is 4.", "", r"This is $1.2^3$; the power is 4."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Define the coefficient of viscosity of a fluid, and state how the viscosity of a liquid and of a gas changes with temperature.",
           r"The coefficient of viscosity $\eta$ is the tangential force per unit area between layers of fluid, divided by the velocity gradient at right angles to the layers: $\eta = \dfrac{F/A}{dv/dx}$. The viscosity of a liquid decreases as temperature rises; the viscosity of a gas increases as temperature rises."),
        PQ(r"Derive an expression for the terminal velocity of a sphere of radius $r$ and density $\rho_s$ falling in a fluid of density $\rho_f$ and viscosity $\eta$.",
           r"At terminal velocity the forces balance: weight $=$ upthrust $+$ drag. $\tfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3\rho_sg = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3\rho_fg + 6\pi\eta rv_t$. So $6\pi\eta rv_t = \tfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3(\rho_s - \rho_f)g$ and $v_t = \dfrac{2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g}{9\eta}$."),
        PQ(r"A water droplet of radius $0.010$ mm is falling through still air ($\eta = 1.8\times10^{-5}$ Pa s, $\rho_{\text{air}} = 1.2$ kg/m³). Taking $\rho_{\text{water}} = 1000$ kg/m³ and $g = 10$ m/s², find its terminal velocity.",
           r"$v_t = \dfrac{2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g}{9\eta} = \dfrac{2(1.0\times10^{-5})^2(1000 - 1.2)(10)}{9(1.8\times10^{-5})} = 1.2\times10^{-2}$ m/s (about 1 cm/s)."),
        PQ(r"A blood vessel can be modelled as a tube. Explain what happens to the flow rate through it if the radius narrows to $80\%$ of its original value with the same pressure difference.",
           r"By Poiseuille's formula $Q \propto r^4$, so the flow becomes $0.80^4 = 0.41$ of the original, a fall of about $59\%$. To keep the original flow the pressure difference must rise by a factor of about $1/0.41 = 2.4$."),
        TIP(r"Stokes' law and terminal velocity are common in Paper 2 (including the experiment with a falling ball). Poiseuille's formula appears in short calculations and ratio questions in Paper 1."),
        SUM(r"Viscosity: $F = \eta A\,dv/dx$, $\eta$ in Pa s; falls with temperature for liquids.",
            r"Stokes' law: $F = 6\pi\eta rv$ for a slow sphere; terminal velocity $v_t = 2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g/9\eta$.",
            r"Poiseuille: $Q = \pi r^4\Delta p/8\eta L$; flow is proportional to $r^4$.",
            r"These laws apply to laminar flow; fast flow becomes turbulent and the formulae fail."),
    ],
))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 21. Bernoulli's principle
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3,
    topic="Bernoulli's principle",
    title="The continuity equation and Bernoulli's principle",
    slug="phu1-bernoulli-principle",
    minutes=23,
    summary="Use the continuity equation A v = constant and Bernoulli's equation p + 1/2 rho v^2 + rho g h = constant for an ideal fluid, and apply them to a venturi, a draining tank and the lift on a wing.",
    objectives=[
        "State the conditions for an ideal fluid and use the continuity equation",
        "Derive Bernoulli's equation from the work-energy principle",
        "Apply Bernoulli's equation to the venturi meter, Torricelli's outflow and lift",
        "State the limitations of Bernoulli's equation for real fluids",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Ideal fluid", "Continuity equation", "Bernoulli's equation", "Venturi meter", "Torricelli's theorem", "Lift"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Hold the end of a garden hose and squeeze it: the water speeds up and shoots out further. A tall water tower sends water through the pipes of a whole town. An aeroplane wing lifts a heavy aircraft into the air. All three are explained by one idea: when a fluid moves, **its pressure, speed and height are linked**. In this lesson you will learn the link, known as **Bernoulli's principle**."),
        H("Ideal fluid and the continuity equation"),
        T(r"The simplest model of a moving fluid is an **ideal fluid**. It is **incompressible** (constant density $\rho$), has **no viscosity** (no internal friction), and flows **steadily** (the velocity at each point does not change with time) and without turbulence, along streamlines."),
        T(r"In steady flow through a pipe, the mass of fluid entering one end each second equals the mass leaving the other end. For an incompressible fluid, the **volume** per second is the same everywhere. If the cross-section is $A$ and the speed is $v$, the volume per second is $Av$:"),
        D(r"For an ideal fluid in steady flow along a pipe, the volume flow rate is constant:", title="Equation of continuity", formula=r"A_1v_1 = A_2v_2",
          note=r"The fluid speeds up where the pipe narrows and slows down where it widens."),
        EX("Worked example 1: continuity", intro=r"Water flows at $2.0$ m/s in a pipe of cross-sectional area $5.0\times10^{-3}$ m², which narrows to $2.0\times10^{-3}$ m². Find the volume flow rate and the speed in the narrow part.", steps=[
            S("Flow rate", math=r"Q = A_1v_1 = (5.0\times10^{-3})(2.0) = 1.0\times10^{-2}\ \text{m}^3/\text{s} = 10\ \text{litres per second}"),
            S("Speed in the narrow section", math=r"v_2 = \frac{A_1v_1}{A_2} = \frac{1.0\times10^{-2}}{2.0\times10^{-3}} = 5.0\ \text{m/s}"),
        ]),
        H("Bernoulli's equation"),
        T(r"Consider a streamline in an ideal fluid. A small volume $\Delta V$ moves from section 1 (pressure $p_1$, speed $v_1$, height $h_1$) to section 2 (pressure $p_2$, speed $v_2$, height $h_2$). The net work done on it by the pressure forces is $(p_1 - p_2)\Delta V$. This work equals the increase in kinetic energy and in gravitational potential energy of the mass $\rho\Delta V$:"),
        F(r"(p_1 - p_2)\Delta V = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho\Delta V(v_2^2 - v_1^2) + \rho\Delta V g(h_2 - h_1)"),
        T(r"Dividing by $\Delta V$ and rearranging gives:"),
        D(r"Along a streamline in the steady flow of an ideal fluid,", title="Bernoulli's equation", formula=r"p + \tfrac{1}{2}\rho v^2 + \rho gh = \text{constant}",
          note=r"Each term is an energy per unit volume (J/m³ = Pa): the pressure, the kinetic energy density and the potential energy density. $p$ is the pressure in the fluid (use absolute or gauge pressure consistently)."),
        T(r"**Meaning.** Where the fluid is fast, the pressure is low; where it is slow, the pressure is high (at the same height). This is **Bernoulli's principle**. It does not say that high speed 'causes' low pressure; rather, a fluid accelerating into a narrow section must have a pressure difference to push it there."),
        H("The venturi meter"),
        T(r"A **venturi** is a pipe with a constriction. A pressure difference between the wide and narrow parts shows in the heights of two vertical tubes (manometer tubes). At the same height, $p_1 + \tfrac{1}{2}\rho v_1^2 = p_2 + \tfrac{1}{2}\rho v_2^2$."),
        IMG("al-phu1-venturi.svg", alt="A horizontal pipe that narrows in the middle, with fluid moving left to right. A vertical tube on the wide part has a higher liquid column than the vertical tube on the narrow part.", caption="Figure 1. A venturi. The narrow section has faster flow and lower pressure, so the liquid column there is lower."),
        EX("Worked example 2: pressure difference in a venturi", intro=r"For the pipe of Worked example 1 (water, $\rho = 1000$ kg/m³), find the pressure difference between the wide and the narrow parts, and the difference in heights of the liquid in the two vertical tubes. Take $g = 10$ m/s².", steps=[
            S("Bernoulli at the same height", math=r"p_1 - p_2 = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho(v_2^2 - v_1^2) = \tfrac{1}{2}(1000)(5.0^2 - 2.0^2) = 1.05\times10^{4}\ \text{Pa}"),
            S("Height difference", math=r"\Delta h = \frac{\Delta p}{\rho g} = \frac{1.05\times10^{4}}{(1000)(10)} = 1.05\ \text{m}"),
        ]),
        H("Flow out of a tank: Torricelli"),
        T(r"A large open tank has a small hole a depth $h$ below the water surface. At the surface the pressure is atmospheric and the speed is nearly zero (the tank is wide). At the hole the pressure is also atmospheric. Bernoulli gives $\rho gh = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho v^2$:"),
        F(r"v = \sqrt{2gh}"),
        T(r"This is **Torricelli's theorem**: the speed equals that of a body falling freely through the same height. For $h = 1.8$ m and $g = 10$ m/s², $v = 6.0$ m/s. A water tower works on the same idea: the pressure at a tap at ground level, $12$ m below the water surface, is $\rho gh = (1000)(10)(12) = 1.2\times10^{5}$ Pa above atmospheric."),
        H("Lift on a wing"),
        T(r"An aerofoil (wing) is shaped and tilted so that air above it moves faster than the air below it. By Bernoulli's principle the pressure above is lower than the pressure below, giving a net upward force. (This is part of the explanation. A full account also includes the downward deflection of air by the wing, which gives the same force by Newton's laws. Check your textbook.)"),
        EX("Worked example 3: lift", intro=r"Air of density $1.2$ kg/m³ flows over a wing at $70$ m/s above and $60$ m/s below. The wing area is $20$ m². Estimate the lift force.", steps=[
            S("Pressure difference", math=r"\Delta p = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho(v_{\text{above}}^2 - v_{\text{below}}^2) = \tfrac{1}{2}(1.2)(70^2 - 60^2) = 780\ \text{Pa}"),
            S("Lift", math=r"F = \Delta p\,A = (780)(20) = 1.56\times10^{4}\ \text{N} \approx 16\ \text{kN}"),
        ]),
        T(r"**Other applications.** A spray gun or perfume atomiser: fast air over a tube reduces the pressure, and liquid is pushed up the tube by atmospheric pressure. A pitot tube measures the speed of an aircraft by comparing the pressure where the air is brought to rest with the pressure in the free stream. Two ships moving side by side are drawn together, because the fast water between them has lower pressure."),
        H("Limits of Bernoulli's equation"),
        T(r"The equation applies along a streamline for an incompressible, non-viscous fluid in steady flow. In real fluids viscosity causes energy loss (the pressure drops along a pipe, as Poiseuille's formula shows), and turbulence breaks the assumptions. For air at speeds approaching the speed of sound, compressibility matters."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using Bernoulli's equation across a viscous pipe flow with a long pipe", r"In a long narrow pipe the pressure falls steadily because of viscosity. Bernoulli's equation, which ignores viscosity, would predict no fall."),
        MISTAKE("Ignoring the pressure at an open surface", r"At the surface of an open tank, and at an open outlet, the pressure is atmospheric. The two atmospheric pressures cancel in the equation, but do not forget them if one side is closed."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking pressure is highest where the fluid moves fastest", r"At the same height, pressure is **lowest** where the speed is greatest."),
        QUIZ(r"Water flows in a horizontal pipe that narrows to half its diameter. How does the speed in the narrow part compare with that in the wide part?",
             [r"Twice as fast", r"Four times as fast", r"Half as fast", r"The same"], 1,
             r"Area $\propto d^2$, so halving the diameter divides the area by 4. Continuity $Av = $ constant gives a speed four times as great.",
             hint=r"The area depends on the diameter squared.",
             feedback=[r"This takes the area to be halved. Area depends on the square of the diameter.", "", r"The fluid speeds up in a narrow section.", r"Continuity requires the speed to change when the area changes."]),
        QUIZ(r"A large tank of water has a small hole at a depth of $5.0$ m. Taking $g = 10$ m/s², what is the speed of the water leaving the hole?",
             [r"$5.0$ m/s", r"$7.1$ m/s", r"$10$ m/s", r"$50$ m/s"], 2,
             r"$v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2(10)(5.0)} = 10$ m/s.",
             hint=r"Use $v = \sqrt{2gh}$.",
             feedback=[r"This is $h$ or $\tfrac{1}{2}gh$; the formula is $\sqrt{2gh}$.", r"This is $\sqrt{gh}$; the factor 2 is missing.", "", r"This is $gh$, not $\sqrt{2gh}$."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"State the assumptions made in deriving Bernoulli's equation, and state the principle in words.",
           r"The fluid is incompressible, non-viscous, flows steadily and without turbulence along a streamline. In words: along a streamline, where the speed of the fluid is higher the pressure is lower (at the same height); the sum of the pressure, the kinetic energy per unit volume and the potential energy per unit volume is constant."),
        PQ(r"Water leaves a hose of cross-sectional area $2.0\times10^{-4}$ m² at $8.0$ m/s. Find the volume flow rate in litres per second. If the nozzle area is reduced to $5.0\times10^{-5}$ m², find the new exit speed (same flow rate).",
           r"$Q = Av = (2.0\times10^{-4})(8.0) = 1.6\times10^{-3}$ m³/s $= 1.6$ litres per second. New speed $= \dfrac{Q}{A} = \dfrac{1.6\times10^{-3}}{5.0\times10^{-5}} = 32$ m/s."),
        PQ(r"A venturi has a wide section of area $4.0\times10^{-3}$ m² and a throat of area $1.0\times10^{-3}$ m². Water ($\rho = 1000$ kg/m³) flows at $1.5$ m/s in the wide section. Find the speed in the throat and the pressure difference.",
           r"$v_2 = \dfrac{A_1v_1}{A_2} = \dfrac{(4.0)(1.5)}{1.0} = 6.0$ m/s. $\Delta p = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho(v_2^2 - v_1^2) = \tfrac{1}{2}(1000)(36 - 2.25) = 1.7\times10^{4}$ Pa."),
        PQ(r"Explain how an aeroplane wing produces lift, using Bernoulli's principle, and give one limitation of this explanation.",
           r"The wing shape and its tilt make air flow faster over the upper surface than over the lower surface. By Bernoulli's principle the pressure above the wing is lower than the pressure below, so there is a net upward force. Limitation: a full account must also include the downward deflection of air by the wing (Newton's laws), and Bernoulli's equation is for an ideal fluid, ignoring viscosity and turbulence."),
        TIP(r"Bernoulli's principle appears in Paper 1 as short reasoning questions (which tube has the lower column) and in Paper 2 as calculations with the continuity equation. Always check whether the two points are at the same height."),
        SUM(r"Ideal fluid: incompressible, non-viscous, steady flow. Continuity: $A_1v_1 = A_2v_2$.",
            r"Bernoulli: $p + \tfrac{1}{2}\rho v^2 + \rho gh = $ constant along a streamline; at the same height, high speed means low pressure.",
            r"Applications: venturi ($\Delta p = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho(v_2^2 - v_1^2)$), Torricelli ($v = \sqrt{2gh}$) and lift on a wing.",
            r"Real fluids have viscosity and turbulence; Bernoulli's equation is then only an approximation."),
    ],
))
